        "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
      },
      "inventory_expansion_required": true,
      "statement": "Let $M>0$ and let $\\mathcal K=\\Lambda\\mathbb C^4$ with orthonormal basis $|S\\rangle$ indexed by subsets $S\\subseteq\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ and grading $(-1)^{|S|}$. For $j\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ define $c_j|S\\rangle=0$ when $j\\notin S$ and\n$c_j|S\\rangle=(-1)^{|\\{s\\in S:s<j\\}|}|S\\setminus\\{j\\}\\rangle$ when $j\\in S$. Let $c_j^\\dagger$ be the actual Hilbert adjoint. For $I,\\alpha\\in\\{1,2\\}$ put $j=2(I-1)+\\alpha$ and set $Q^I_\\alpha=\\sqrt{2M}\\,c_j$, with $\\bar Q_{\\dot\\alpha I}=(Q^I_\\alpha)^\\dagger$. Take $P^\\mu=(M,0,0,0)$ and $Z^{IJ}=0$. On $\\mathcal D=\\mathcal K$, these operators satisfy the massive N=2 rest-frame supercharge relations with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $\\epsilon_{12}=1$. Since $\\|Z\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=0<M$, the bound is strict and no nonzero vector in this fiber is BPS-saturated. This finite module is a representation of the rest-frame supercharge algebra only; it is not asserted to extend to a full Poincare representation or a physical particle.",
