center `Z_N` acts on Wilson lines by their center character, provided there is
no dynamical matter in a center-charged representation. Adding such matter
breaks that one-form symmetry. This elementary example depends on the global
form and matter content and cannot be universalized to every gauge theory.

A defect is a specified modification of local fields, boundary conditions,
or operator insertions supported on a submanifold of stated codimension. A
topological defect has correlation functions invariant under deformations
that avoid insertions, under the assumptions and Ward identities of the
chosen model. An invertible defect has an inverse under fusion; a
non-invertible defect instead has fusion data without an inverse, often a
direct-sum decomposition. Physical junctions, associators, charge actions,
and defect anomalies are part of the interface, not consequences of a
mathematical braid or categorical action alone.

For a soliton, specify the field space, finite-energy boundary condition,
topological charge, energy functional, and central-charge normalization. In
the static adjoint-Higgs model in the Prasad–Sommerfield limit, choose the
action convention

$$E=\frac{1}{2g^2}\int_{\mathbb R^3}(|B|^2+|D\Phi|^2)\,d^3x.$$

Define `B_i=\tfrac12\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}` and
`m=\lim_{R\to\infty}\int_{S_R^2}\langle\Phi,B_i\rangle n_i\,dS_i`.
Assume smooth fields, `D_iB_i=0`, `D_i\Phi\to0`, asymptotic magnetic field
in the unbroken algebra, and sufficient falloff for the surface limit and
integration by parts. Put `T=g^{-2}m`. Gauge invariance of the inner product
and the Bianchi identity give
`\partial_i\langle\Phi,B_i\rangle=\langle D_i\Phi,B_i\rangle`.
Expanding the two squares on a finite ball and passing to the declared limit
yields

$$E=\frac{1}{2g^2}\int_{\mathbb R^3}|B-D\Phi|^2\,d^3x+T,$$

$$E=\frac{1}{2g^2}\int_{\mathbb R^3}|B+D\Phi|^2\,d^3x-T.$$

Thus `E>=|T|`; for `T>0`, equality requires `B=D\Phi`, and for `T<0`,
equality requires `B=-D\Phi` (the zero-charge equality case is separate).
Flux quantization requires a further derivation from the chosen global gauge
group, unbroken subgroup, transition function, and matter charge lattice. The
square completion establishes neither such quantization nor existence of a
smooth solution. This conditional classical energy bound is not an
experimental observation or a quantum particle state. The mathematical
Hall/BPS and categorical line interfaces are reused only after their exact
input data are provided; a physical protected-index dictionary is additional
data and proof.
For a classical monopole example, fix a `3+1`-dimensional Minkowski model with
compact gauge group, adjoint Higgs field `φ`, gauge connection `A`, coupling,
zero Higgs potential (Prasad–Sommerfield limit), finite-energy static fields
and a chosen vacuum `φ_∞` that breaks `G` to a declared unbroken subgroup.
Define `B_i=\frac12 ε_{ijk}F_{jk}`, `D_iφ`, the asymptotic map/transition data
and magnetic charge `m=∫_{S^2_∞}⟨φ,B_i⟩dS_i`. For the action convention
`E=(1/(2g^2))∫_{R^3}(|B|^2+|Dφ|^2)d^3x`, put `T=g^{-2}m` and require
smooth fields and falloff such
that `D_iB_i=0`, `D_iφ→0` at infinity, the asymptotic magnetic field lies in
the unbroken algebra, the surface flux exists, and integration by parts has no
residual bulk/boundary term. With an invariant inner product the displayed
surface charge is gauge invariant under the allowed asymptotic gauge group.
Then both square completions hold:

`E = (1/(2g^2))∫_{R^3}|B-Dφ|^2d^3x + T`,

`E = (1/(2g^2))∫_{R^3}|B+Dφ|^2d^3x - T`.

For an explicit proof, expand each square on a finite ball `B_R`; invariance
of the Lie-algebra inner product gives
`∂_i⟨φ,B_i⟩=⟨D_iφ,B_i⟩+⟨φ,D_iB_i⟩=⟨D_iφ,B_i⟩`, using the Bianchi identity.
Apply the divergence theorem on `B_R`, then take `R→∞` using the declared
falloff and surface-flux limit. The identities give `E≥|T|`; for `T>0`,
equality requires `B=Dφ`, and for `T<0`, equality requires `B=-Dφ`. A
zero-charge equality case must be handled separately. Tong's lecture uses
`1/e^2` in front of the quadratic energy and `2/e^2 m` for its boundary term;
it matches this normalization with `g^2=e^2/2` and the same invariant inner
product. A carrier must also derive
the gauge-invariant flux quantization from the declared global gauge group,
unbroken subgroup, bundle transition function and matter charge lattice; it
must not import a universal normalization of magnetic charge. This is a
conditional classical energy bound and first-order Bogomolny equation, not an
experimental observation and not a quantum particle state. The repository's
Abelian EM flux example is only a mathematical patching/flux calculation, not
this non-Abelian field model.

Likewise define an instanton/soliton moduli space only after specifying the
PDE, gauge quotient, boundary conditions, regularity, charge and compactness or
compactification. If a physical mass saturates an algebraic central-charge
bound, that is a separate consequence of the named supersymmetry algebra and
the explicit classical or quantum model; a formal classical energy-square
calculation alone does not establish a quantum particle state.

