The former Step 2 assignment stage and former Step 4 splice/integration stage
are deleted. Necessary group metadata is generated mechanically during planning.
Accepted manifests are transcribed into the canonical plan at Step 2's existing
mechanical join, followed by the post-authoring snapshot. Neither operation has
its own agent or workflow stage. The pre-authoring snapshot runs before Step 1
construction. Initial item and page authoring happens entirely in Step 1;
later stages retain their substantive review, repair and finalization duties.

The Step-1 manifest policy join uses `content-policy --manifest-only --authored`:
before canonical plan transcription, an existing local item may be reused only
at its unique authored page home, matching the manifest's page and category.
Canonical plan ownership conflicts, batch capacity, duplicate inventory and
dependency ordering remain errors. Ordinary pre-authoring manifest checks retain
their minting rules; this option is incompatible with audit mode.

## Construction and escalation

Step 1 keeps existing drift/source recovery, readiness, scope, page-capacity and
owner-hold mechanisms. Initial authors use `gpt-6-luna-max`; drift/source recovery
retains its existing model assignments. Both drift reviewers and authors
must escalate significant unmet prerequisites directly to the owner, giving the
smallest missing supplier, consuming claims/steps and affected consumers.
Scaffolding agents fully author all assigned items, pages and proof contracts;
a proof strategy or successful process alone is not a completed carrier.
Readiness records bind the finished carriers. Initial authoring fills every
available worker slot, up to the shared cap of 30, taking lower dependency levels
first across the complete pending queue. Levels are priorities, not completion
barriers: authors start and continue even when another pair's supplier is missing.
As soon as a worker finishes, the engine starts the next pending pair without
waiting for the rest of its level.

Batch levels are computed mechanically during planning, recomputed after drift
materialization and at the drained construction join, and recorded in
`research/RUN-batch-dependency-levels.json`. Queue refill and status use that last
valid full graph while authors edit draft manifests and carrier YAML. The loader
validates its DAG, levels and complete current manifest batch scope without
parsing in-flight drafts. The drained join and gates strictly recompute and check
the current graph, including declared carrier dependencies. No
cross-pair prerequisite-completion barrier applies to initial authoring. A missing
supplier retains its exact planned interface and honest proof-status obligation;
authors complete the consuming argument conditionally and continue their remaining
work. They keep explicit unresolved readiness records, never invent supplier
acceptance or duplicate another pair's ownership. Completion accounting requires
local authored carriers, inventories, contracts, coverage and readiness records.
The complete, drained Step-1 gate battery still requires current supplier content,
resolved readiness and correct dependency levels before review. This adds no stage.

Per-item Step-1 readiness recording retains the complete manifest page/pair and
item ownership index, but hashes only the target and its actual transitive
dependencies, including explicitly examined context. A bare item ID in an
unrelated unfinished inventory does not prevent that local authoring record;
the same unfinished ID in the target's dependency closure fails recording.
Unrelated malformed carrier YAML is deferred during identity/alias indexing;
a reached carrier is parsed strictly, and relevant duplicate identity or alias
claims remain blocking. Malformed JSON, ambiguous ownership and invalid
page/pair structure still fail.
The hash calculation and alias resolution are shared with the strict full-frontier
checker; local readiness records grant no global acceptance or gate authority.

At a drained owner join, `recordStep1Batch(root, {run, records, owner: true})`
from `tools/physics-support/step1-decisions.mjs` can record multiple independently
reviewed readiness dispositions without rebuilding the full alias index per item.
Each record supplies the ordinary `item`, `decision`, `reason` and explicit
`dependencies` fields. The owner preserves prior rows before replacement and
supplies actual scientific evidence; batching grants no review or gate authority.
All records and ownership are validated before any writes. One shared local
snapshot hashes each actual dependency closure plus examined context; a second
fresh snapshot must reproduce every hash before the native-format rows are
written. Relevant mutation aborts the batch without writing rows; unrelated
unfinished drafts retain the ordinary local-recording exclusion. Keep writers
drained through recording. The individual `record` CLI remains unchanged.

Item `dependency_level` is determined before writing item/page carriers, recorded
and independently checked in Step 1: zero
without in-run logical suppliers, otherwise one plus the maximum supplier level.
External suppliers remain context. No rebatching or additional agent stage
is introduced. Logical dependencies must agree between authored bodies and
manifests; aliases resolve to canonical suppliers. Definition discharges and
nonlogical links are hash-bound context, not extra logical scheduling edges.

`node tools/physics-support/depcheck.mjs --help` (or `-h`) prints usage and
budget; duplicate pending requests retain the same label. Only an engine-started,
matching fresh successful native result discharges the request. Coverage masks that
unit until then, including after restart; siblings keep their coverage. Actual
artifacts and the complete ordinary Step-1 battery remain required. A second
continuation for the same unit is refused, and an unresolved result escalates to
the owner. Closed stages cannot be reopened by this command. The CLI only writes a
control request; upgrading controller code and supervising restart/resume remain
owner operations.

## Cross-batch dependency inputs

Each batch writes `research/RUN-batch-N.cross-batch-dependencies.json` as an
array, including an empty array when it has no declared in-run cross-batch
edges. The collector derives edges from current manifest and source `requires`
for pages, and `deps`, `justified_by`, and `forward_refs` for items. Reviews use
one row per exact consumer/supplier edge:

```json
[
  {
    "kind": "item",
    "consumer": "lem-example-consumer",
    "supplier": "def-example-supplier",
    "status": "open",
    "evidence": "Exact declared dependency; the scoped supplier-use check remains pending."
  }
]
```

`kind` is `item` or `page`, and the consumer must belong to the batch writing
the file. `status` is `open`, `verified`, or `removed`, with nonempty evidence.

  ```markdown
  1.1 Establish the first claim, including its full argument. [F1]

  1.2 Complete the argument using the earlier claim. [step 1.1] ∎
  ```
- Common tags include `given`, `F/A/L/C<n>`, `step p.q`, `algebra`, `choose`,
  `construct`, and strategy opener/discharge tags. The accepted vocabulary and
  strategy checks are in the app's `worker/src/precheck.ts`, called by
  `tools/physics-support/precheck.mts`. Adopt any repair it proposes before recording a pass.
- Use `$...$` for inline math and `$$...$$` for display math. A display formula
  must occupy one source line between delimiters; put delimiters on separate
  lines when display-only KaTeX features such as `\tag` or `CD` are needed.
  Avoid wikilinks or nested dollars inside math, dollars inside `\tag{}`,
  blank lines in inline math, and `\(...\)` or `\[...\]` delimiters. KaTeX must
  parse each expression. Keep fenced `tikz` and `tikzcd` outside Facts and
  proof-like sections.

## Pages and categories

- Page file: `library/<category>/<page>.md`. Frontmatter uses `page`, `title`,
  `status`, `items: [id, ...]`, and `examples: [id, ...]`; the body is the page
  summary. The two lists control placement, not item kind. Entries must resolve
  and cannot repeat. A published page may list only published items.
- An item on more than one page draws a warning; dependency tools use its first
  home. A published item without a page also draws a warning. An item homed
  only on a B/examples page cannot be another page's dependency; earlier items
  on that same page are allowed. The plan identifies B pages, with the
  `-examples` suffix as a fallback.
- `library/<category>/_category.md` supplies the category title and a prose
  overview. Missing or short overviews warn; the short threshold is 120 words.
- `library/<category>/_pathway.md` uses `status` and `parts`, each with `part`,
  `title`, and `pages`; `category` is descriptive because the directory selects
  the category. Its body has one `## <part>` brief per part. List each published
  A page once, omit examples companions, keep pages in their category, and put
  each prerequisite in the same or an earlier part. Empty parts, missing pages
  or briefs, duplicate placements, orphan briefs, and out-of-order prerequisites
  are errors. Missing pathways, unplaced draft pages, singleton parts, and briefs
  over 120 words warn; `not-proved-here` is exempt from the missing-pathway warning.

## Checks

- `tools/physics-support/depcheck.mjs` checks IDs, dependencies, page lists, cycles, and
  publication evidence; `tools/physics-support/fwdcheck.mjs` and `tools/physics-support/extcheck.mjs` check
  later and unproved references.
- `tools/physics-support/precheck.mts` checks proof format; `tools/physics-support/rendercheck.mjs` checks YAML,
  math, and diagram rendering; `tools/physics-support/pathcheck.mjs` checks category overviews
  and pathways.
- `node tools/physics-support/proof-layout.mjs items/<id>.md ...` checks step separation and
  blue tags using the actual renderer. Run it after the final item edit or
  formatter. Step 9 requires 100% of numbered rows to have valid blue chips;
  the older line-based precheck's 70% threshold alone is insufficient.
- `tools/physics-support/content-policy.mjs` applies provenance, source, and generated-claim
  rules to an explicit run or audit scope. Other run gates and their commands
  are described in [WORKFLOW.md](WORKFLOW.md).


## Physics extensions (override mathematical-only rules where stated)

This schema applies inside `physics/`. Mathematics uses the root SCHEMA.md and
its unchanged engine. Imported mathematical suppliers retain their original
bytes and IDs, are read-only, and are verified by `research/math-imports.json`.
Run `init` before planning; reconcile supplier updates outside active runs.

New kinds and ID prefixes:

| Kind | Prefix | Body | Acceptance |
|---|---|---|---|
| postulate | post- | Postulate; explanatory prose | Sources, formulation, physical scope |
| physics-theorem | pthm- | Statement; Facts & Assumptions; Proof | Complete conditional proof |
| thought-experiment | texp- | Statement; Facts & Assumptions; Proof | Identical to physics-theorem |
| experiment | exp- | Setup; Procedure; Observations; Uncertainty; Interpretation | Reported source-backed empirical findings |

Every locally authored item requires `domain: mathematics` or `domain: physics`.
The four new kinds require physics; theorem/lemma/proposition/corollary require
mathematics. Definitions, algorithms, examples, remarks, and open problems may have either domain. An open problem retains its no-logical-supplier rule in both domains.
Mathematical items may never depend on physical items, including through aliases
or `justified_by` or load-bearing `forward_refs`. Physical items may depend on either domain. All `deps` remain
acyclic; existing well-definedness rules for `justified_by` still apply.

Every local category requires `library: physics` or `library: mathematics` in
`_category.md`. A page may repeat the classification but cannot contradict its
category. Plan pages require `library`; P entries may inherit it from an existing
page. Mathematics pages may contain mathematical items only. Shared mathematics
uses imported canonical IDs; never re-author an imported item. Its first imported
mathematics placement remains its canonical prerequisite home.

Every local item annotates each `deps` entry in `dependency_roles` with one of:
`mathematical-premise`, `physical-assumption`, `physical-result`,
`empirical-premise`, `operational-prerequisite`, `formulation-prerequisite`.
Mathematical items use only mathematical-premise. Postulates and experiments use
physical suppliers operationally or for formulation, not as proofs of assumptions
or measured outcomes. Experimental interpretation requiring a derivation belongs
in a separate physics-theorem or thought-experiment.

Every physical item states a nonempty `physical_scope`. The new kinds require
source references with HTTP(S) URLs and source-backed statement provenance.
Postulates and experiments use `provenance.proof: not-applicable`, never a fabricated
proof or `proved_here: false`. Their publication requires structured
`verification.sources_checked` with date, scope, and by. Physical-theorem and
thought-experiment use ordinary complete proof formatting and proof audit rules.

Experiments additionally require `empirical_result` with nonempty `observation`,
`uncertainty`, `conditions`, and `source_url`; the URL must match a source reference.
Physical proof items that use empirical premises require `empirical_premises`, an
array of `{experiment, conditions, uncertainty, use}` entries. Carry these entries
through physical-result dependency chains. Mechanical checks establish that the
qualifications are recorded; independent reviewers check their faithfulness.

Nonlogical `relations` is a mapping of arrays of item IDs: `supported_by`,
`tested_by`, `motivated_by`, `replicates`, `challenges`. Except motivated_by, these
name experiments; replicates is used only on experiments. Relations may form
cycles and never supply a proof premise or prerequisite. Mathematical items may
use motivated_by for physical context; empirical evidence cannot establish their
validity. Do not encode empirical
support as deps. See ../PHYSICS-CONTENT-MODEL.md for the complete relation table.
// Structural evidence only. Step 3 independently judges the mathematics.
const text = (s) => typeof s === 'string' && s.trim().length > 0;
const url = (s) => { try { return ['http:', 'https:'].includes(new URL(s).protocol); } catch { return false; } };

export function sourceResolutionErrors(source) {
  const r = source?.source_resolution;
  if (!r) return [];
  const errors = [];
  if (!url(source.url)) errors.push('missing original source URL');
  if (r.status !== 'dropped') errors.push('owner escalation or unresolved source');
  if (!['step-1-scaffolder', 'owner'].includes(r.decided_by) || r.confidence !== 'certain') {
    errors.push('requires a fully confident scaffolder or owner decision; otherwise escalate to owner');
  }
  if (!text(r.reason) || !text(r.search_summary)) errors.push('missing retrieval failure and search evidence');
  if (!Array.isArray(r.attempts) || r.attempts.length < 6
      || r.attempts.some((a) => !url(a?.url) || !text(a?.outcome) || !text(a?.at))) {
    errors.push('record the initial failure and five recovery retries, with URL, time and outcome');
  }
  if (!Array.isArray(r.searches) || !r.searches.length
      || r.searches.some((s) => !text(s?.query) || !text(s?.outcome))) {
    errors.push('missing autonomous web-search record');
  }
  const alternatives = r.alternatives;
  if (!Array.isArray(alternatives)) errors.push('missing alternative proof records');
  for (const h of source.contents ?? []) {
    if (!['included', 'inline'].includes(h.disposition)) continue;
    const a = Array.isArray(alternatives) ? alternatives.find((a) => a?.item === h.item) : null;
    if (!a || !text(a.argument) || !Array.isArray(a.deps) || !a.deps.every(text)) {
      errors.push(`missing alternative argument and dependencies for ${h.item ?? h.name}`);
    }
  }
  return errors;
}

export function sourceDropped(source) {
  return source?.source_resolution?.status === 'dropped'
    && sourceResolutionErrors(source).length === 0;
}
