items/pthm-cm15-control-volume-balance.md:206:- **Inertial conventions.** The velocities $u$, $w$, $w_{\mathrm{in}}$, $w_{\mathrm{out}}$ and the momentum density $\rho u$ are all measured in one inertial frame; the flux uses the *relative* normal velocity $(u-w)\cdot\nu$, and choosing a different auxiliary velocity on the interior of $C_t$ does not change the identities because its contribution is converted to a boundary term by the divergence theorem ([[thm-cm-control-volume-balance]]). The lumped velocity of clause 3 is $v=P/m$, the momentum-weighted mean velocity of the contents, not the velocity of any particular material point.
items/thm-gr-dust-subsidiary-and-residual-vanishing.md:100:[F3] A continuous pointwise positive-definite symmetric matrix family has a uniform positive lower bound on a compact parameter set, and continuous matrix entries and their derivatives are bounded there ([[lem-gr-positive-definite-symmetric-matrix-bounds]], [[thm-extreme-value-metric]]). The divergence theorem applies to bounded piecewise $C^1$ coordinate cones, and Gronwall forces a continuous nonnegative cone energy with zero initial value to vanish ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], [[thm-gronwall-integral-inequality]]).
items/thm-gr-dust-subsidiary-and-residual-vanishing.md:121:The matrix $X(w)$ is skew-adjoint, so the displayed block and the full symbol are symmetric in the stated positive fibre product. This covector formula is tensorial in the principal part: evaluating it at $dt$ and each $dx^i$ gives the coordinate matrices $A^0,A^i$, so all those matrices are symmetric even though the Gaussian time vector need not equal the comoving vector $U$. The $(P,Q)$ block is bounded below by $z-|w|/2$ because $\|w\times\cdot\|\le|w|$, while each transport block is $z$. Since $dt$ is future timelike in the Gaussian chart, $z=dt(U)>|w|$; thus $A^0$ is positive definite. On a compact coordinate cylinder choose $a>0$ and $b<\infty$ with $A^0\ge aI$, $\|A^i\|\le b$, and bound the coefficient derivatives and $C$. Choose $\lambda>\sqrt3 b/a$, giving $\lambda A^0+\nu_iA^i\ge0$ for every unit spatial conormal $\nu$. Shrink $\tau>0$ so the cone based on a disk within the zero slice, with radius $\lambda\tau$, lies in the cylinder. Put $j^0=Z^{\mathsf T}A^0Z$, $j^i=Z^{\mathsf T}A^iZ$. Direct differentiation gives $\partial_tj^0+\partial_i j^i=Z^{\mathsf T}(\partial_tA^0+\partial_iA^i-C-C^{\mathsf T})Z\le C_0j^0$. On the shrinking balls $B_s=\{x:|x-x(p)|\le\lambda(\tau-s)\}$, the lateral flux is proportional to $Z^{\mathsf T}(\lambda A^0+\nu_iA^i)Z\ge0$. The divergence theorem [F3] yields $E(s):=\int_{B_s}j^0dx\le E(0)+C_0\int_0^sE(r)dr$. The base data give $E(0)=0$, so Gronwall [F3] and positivity of $A^0$ give $Z=0$ on the forward cone. Applying the same argument after reversing time gives zero on the backward cone. Their union contains an open double-cone neighborhood of $p$. [step 3.1, F3, given, algebra, step 1.2]
items/pthm-emx-controlled-dipole-power.md:129:[F3] On a fixed compact measurable set, differentiation under the integral is valid for a smooth integrand; the divergence theorem gives $\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}F=\int_{\partial\Omega}F\cdot\nu$ for a $C^1$ field on a bounded $C^1$ domain. ([[lem-em-parameter-integrals]], [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]])
items/pthm-emx-controlled-dipole-power.md:145:1.1 Dipole moment identities. The compact-ball verification in the preceding transverse-radiation theorem [F1] applies to the same $a>0$ and Countable Choice hypothesis: $\overline B_a$ is closed and bounded, hence compact; it is Borel and Lebesgue measurable; and $\lambda_3(\overline B_a)\le\lambda_3(B_{2a})<\infty$. Thus it is an admissible fixed integration set for [F3]. For each $i\in\{1,2,3\}$ put $p_i(u)=\int_{B_a}y_i\rho(y,u)\,dy$. Smoothness and vanishing of $\rho,J$ for $|y|>a$ imply that $\rho$, $J_i$ and their time derivatives vanish on $\partial B_a$. Thus the integrals of $y_i\rho$ and $y_i\rho_t$ over $B_a$ agree with those over that compact measurable finite-measure set $\overline B_a$. Apply the set form of [F3] with integration set $\overline B_a$ and parameter $u$ to $f_i(y,u)=y_i\rho(y,u)$; its parameter derivative is $y_i\rho_t(y,u)$, and both are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a$ times any compact time interval. This gives $p_i'(u)=\int_{B_a}y_i\rho_t(y,u)\,dy$. Fix $R_0>a$ and apply the divergence theorem to $y\mapsto y_iJ(y,u)$ on $B_{R_0}$. The boundary flux is zero because $J=0$ near $\partial B_{R_0}$, while $\operatorname{div}(y_iJ)=J_i+y_i\operatorname{div}J$. Using $\rho_t=-\operatorname{div}J$ and the vanishing of the source outside $B_a$ gives $p_i'(u)=\int_{B_a}J_i(y,u)\,dy$. For each $i$, the integrals of $J_i$ and $J_{i,t}$ over $B_a$ agree with those over $\overline B_a$; the closed ball has the compactness, measurability and finite measure just verified using [F1]. Both integrands are continuous on a neighborhood of $\overline B_a$ times any compact time interval. Apply the set form of [F3] on $\overline B_a$ separately to each component $J_i$ with parameter $u$; differentiating the preceding identity gives $p_i''(u)=\int_{B_a}J_{i,t}(y,u)\,dy$. Collecting the three component identities yields $p''(u)=\int_{B_a}J_t(y,u)\,dy$. [given, F1, F2, F3, algebra, A1]
