items/lem-em-ideal-cylindrical-sheet-field.md:89:**Proof technique:** direct; the nullity of the cylinder surface, the divergence theorem on the cylinder, and two coordinate computations.
items/lem-em-ideal-cylindrical-sheet-field.md:97:4.1 The field $F_0:=\varphi e_3$ is $C^1$ on $\mathbb R^3$, hence on a neighborhood of $\overline V$, and by [F9] its divergence is $\operatorname{div}F_0=\partial_3\varphi=e_3\cdot\nabla\varphi$. The divergence theorem [F7] on the presentation [F6] gives $\int_V e_3\cdot\nabla\varphi\,d\lambda_3=\int_{\partial V}\varphi\,e_3\cdot\nu\,dS$. On the lateral face $\nu=e_\rho$ and $e_3\cdot e_\rho=0$ by [F8]; on the two disc caps $x_3=\pm R$ the test $\varphi$ vanishes because $\operatorname{supp}\varphi\subset\{|x_3|<R\}$; hence $\int_V e_3\cdot\nabla\varphi\,d\lambda_3=0$. With steps 2.1 and 3.1 this proves $\langle\operatorname{div}T_g,\varphi\rangle=0$ for every scalar test. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F6, F7, F8, F9]
items/lem-em-ideal-cylindrical-sheet-field.md:99:4.2 The field $G_0:=\psi\times e_3$ is $C^1$ on $\mathbb R^3$, and by [F8] its coordinates are $G_0=(\psi_2,-\psi_1,0)$, so by [F9] $\operatorname{div}G_0=\partial_1\psi_2-\partial_2\psi_1=(\operatorname{curl}\psi)_3=e_3\cdot\operatorname{curl}\psi$. The divergence theorem [F7] on the presentation [F6] gives $\int_V e_3\cdot\operatorname{curl}\psi\,d\lambda_3=\int_{\partial V}G_0\cdot\nu\,dS$. The disc caps carry $\psi=0$ by the support condition, while on the lateral face $\nu=e_\rho$ and $G_0\cdot e_\rho=(\psi\times e_3)\cdot e_\rho=\psi\cdot e_\varphi$ by [F8]; therefore $\int_V e_3\cdot\operatorname{curl}\psi\,d\lambda_3=\int_{\rho=a,\ |x_3|<R}e_\varphi\cdot\psi\,dS$, and by [F10] the last integral equals $\int_S e_\varphi\cdot\psi\,dS$ because $e_\varphi\cdot\psi$ is compactly supported and vanishes off the compact band $\{\rho=a,\ |x_3|\le R\}$. With steps 2.1 and 3.1 this is $\langle\operatorname{curl}T_g,\psi\rangle=\int_S e_\varphi\cdot\psi\,dS$ for every vector test, which is the claimed identification $\operatorname{curl}T_g=e_\varphi\,\delta_S$. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F6, F7, F8, F9, F10]
