items/pthm-em-restricted-material-energy.md:104:3.1 For (c), fix $t\in(t_0,t_1)$. Each face in the finite presentation of $\partial\Omega\prime$ lies in a regular $C^1$ parametrized patch and is volume-null by [L5]. The finite union $\partial\Omega\prime$ is therefore volume-null. Hence the compact finite-measure set $K=\overline{\Omega\prime}$ and $\Omega\prime$ have the same integrals for the continuous functions used here. Apply [L3] to $K$ and $f=u$: $\frac{d}{dt}\int_{\Omega'}u\,dx=\int_{\Omega'}\partial_tu\,dx$, where the regularity hypotheses on the fields supply the continuous $\partial_tu$ on a neighbourhood of $\overline{\Omega'}\times[a,b]$ for a compact interval $[a,b]\subset I$ containing $t$ in its interior; substituting the local balance of step 2.1 and applying the divergence theorem [L4] to $F=S$ gives $\frac{d}{dt}\int_{\Omega'}u=-\int_{\Omega'}J_f\cdot E-\int_{\Omega'}\operatorname{div}S=-\int_{\Omega'}J_f\cdot E-\oint_{\partial\Omega'}S\cdot n\,dS$, under the $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ hypothesis carried by [L4]. Thus (a)-(c) hold; the reading (b) is step 1.3 substituted into the balance, and (d) is the scope: $u$ is a stored-energy density only for this constant symmetric nondispersive model, no instantaneous stored energy is claimed for dispersive, nonlocal, nonlinear or hysteretic response, the boundary term is retained, and no conservation of $\int_{\Omega'}u$ or empirical statement is asserted. [step 1.3, step 2.1, L3, L4, L5] ∎
