items/thm-gr-harmonic-constraint-propagation.md:76:[L6] On a compact coordinate cylinder, a continuous positive-definite symmetric time matrix has a uniform positive lower bound and smooth coefficient matrices and their derivatives are bounded ([[lem-gr-positive-definite-symmetric-matrix-bounds]], [[thm-extreme-value-metric]]). The divergence theorem applies to bounded piecewise $C^1$ truncated coordinate cones, and a continuous nonnegative energy satisfying $E(t)\le C\int_0^tE(s)ds$ vanishes by Gronwall ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], [[thm-gronwall-integral-inequality]]).
items/thm-gr-harmonic-constraint-propagation.md:84:2.1 (Local cone energy for the subsidiary wave equation.) Fix $p\in\Sigma$ and a relatively compact normal-geodesic chart from [L5], with coordinates $(t,x)$ centered at $p$. Put $w_b=H_b$, $z_b=\partial_tw_b$ and $y_{ib}=\partial_iw_b$. The wave equation [L4] is equivalent on this chart to the first-order system $\partial_tw_b-z_b=0,\qquad \partial_ty_{ib}-\partial_i z_b=0,\qquad \partial_tz_b-h^{ij}\partial_i y_{jb}=F_b(t,x,w,z,y),$ where $F$ is smooth and linear in $(w,z,y)$. Multiplying the middle equations by $h^{ai}$ gives symmetric spatial coefficient matrices and positive time matrix $A^0=\operatorname{diag}(I,I,h^{ij}I)$ on the independent variables $(w,z,y)$. On a compact coordinate cylinder choose $a>0$ and $b<\infty$ such that $A^0\ge aI$ and $\|A^i\|\le b$; all coefficient derivatives and the lower-order matrix $C$ are bounded there. Choose $\lambda>\sqrt3 b/a$. Then $\lambda A^0+\nu_iA^i\ge0$ for every Euclidean unit spatial conormal $\nu$, since $\|\nu_iA^i\|\le\sqrt3 b$. Shrink $\tau>0$ so the double cone $|t|\le\tau$, $|x-x(p)|\le\lambda\tau$ lies in the chart. Step 1.1 and $H=0$ on $\Sigma$ imply $w=z=y=0$ on its base disk at $t=0$. For $0\le s<\tau$, set $B_s=\{x:|x-x(p)|\le\lambda(\tau-s)\}$ and $E(s)=\int_{B_s}V^{\mathsf T}A^0V\,dx$, where $V=(w,z,y)$. For a symmetric system $A^0\partial_tV+A^i\partial_iV+CV=0$, its energy current $j^0=V^{\mathsf T}A^0V$, $j^i=V^{\mathsf T}A^iV$ satisfies $\partial_tj^0+\partial_i j^i=V^{\mathsf T}(\partial_tA^0+\partial_iA^i-C-C^{\mathsf T})V.$ The right side is bounded above by a constant times $j^0$ on the compact cone. The lateral outward flux on the shrinking cone is proportional to $V^{\mathsf T}(\lambda A^0+\nu_iA^i)V\ge0$. Applying the divergence theorem [L6] gives $E(s)\le E(0)+C\int_0^sE(r)dr$. Since $E(0)=0$, Gronwall [L6] and positivity of $A^0$ imply $H=0$ on the forward cone. Reversing time changes each $A^i$ to $-A^i$ and preserves the same bounds, so the same argument gives vanishing on the backward cone. The two cone estimates give vanishing on the open set $W_p=\{(t,x):|t|<\tau,\ |x-x(p)|<\lambda(\tau-|t|)\}$, which contains $p$. Hence $W=\bigcup_{p\in\Sigma}W_p$ is an open neighborhood in $U$ on which $H=0$. [step 1.1, L4, L5, L6, algebra]
items/pthm-em-angular-balance.md:97:[F4] Fixed-volume tools: for compact measurable $K$ with finite Lebesgue measure and $h,\partial_th$ continuous on a neighbourhood of $K\times[a,b]$, $t\mapsto\int_Kh\,d\lambda_n$ is differentiable on $(a,b)$ with derivative $\int_K\partial_th\,d\lambda_n$ ([[lem-em-parameter-integrals]]); if $\Omega$ carries the finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation and $F\in C^1(\overline\Omega;\mathbb R^n)$, then $\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}F=\sum_j\int_{S_j}F\cdot\nu_j\,dS$ with faces counted once off the edge set, under $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]]); and the boundary of such an $\Omega$ is Lebesgue-null by the graph-coordinate Fubini and rigid-change-of-variables argument in Proof step 4.1 of that divergence theorem, followed by finite subadditivity over its faces; the integral of a nonnegative measurable function over a null set vanishes ([[cor-integral-over-a-null-set-vanishes]]), so signed integrals over $\Omega$ and its closure agree.
items/pthm-em-angular-balance.md:101:**Proof technique:** direct; apply the angular identity of the quadratic stress lemma to the momentum balance, and integrate the result over an admissible fixed volume with compact-parameter differentiation and the same divergence theorem.
items/pthm-em-angular-balance.md:107:2.1 Fixed-volume identity: by steps 1.2 and 1.1, $\frac{d}{dt}\int_{\Omega'}\ell_i\,dx=\int_{\Omega'}\partial_t\ell_i\,dx=-\int_{\Omega'}(x\times f)_i\,dx-\int_{\Omega'}(\operatorname{div}(x\times\Pi))_i\,dx$; the fixed-time vector field $x\mapsto((x\times\Pi)_{i1},(x\times\Pi)_{i2},(x\times\Pi)_{i3})(x,t)$ is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of $\overline{\Omega'}$ by [F2], so the divergence theorem of [F4] gives $\int_{\Omega'}(\operatorname{div}(x\times\Pi))_i\,dx=\oint_{\partial\Omega'}\sum_j(x\times\Pi)_{ij}n_j\,dS$ as a finite face sum, which is clause (b) under the stated $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, F2, F4, algebra]
items/thm-cm-stress-matrix-is-symmetric.md:59:[F1] Divergence theorem: for $F\in C^1(\overline D;\mathbb R^3)$ on a bounded $C^1$ domain $D$, $\int_D\operatorname{div}F\,dx=\int_{\partial D}F\cdot\nu\,dS$ ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]]). Applying it to the rows $F_i=(\sigma_{i1},\sigma_{i2},\sigma_{i3})$ gives $\int_{\partial D}\sigma\nu\,dS=\int_Dg\,dx$ with $g_i:=\sum_j\partial_j\sigma_{ij}$.
items/thm-cm-stress-matrix-is-symmetric.md:69:**Proof technique:** direct; differentiate the two integral balances by the divergence theorem and localize.
items/thm-cm-stress-matrix-is-symmetric.md:73:2.1 Fix a bounded $C^1$ domain $D$ with $\overline D\subseteq O$ and a component index $i\in\{1,2,3\}$. By [F2], and applying the divergence theorem [F1] to the components $F^{(i)}_l:=(\varepsilon_{ijk}y_j\sigma_{kl})_l$ of the vector field whose $l$-th entry is $\varepsilon_{ijk}y_j\sigma_{kl}$ (summed over $j,k$), $$\Bigl(\int_{\partial D}y\times(\sigma\nu)\,dS\Bigr)_i=\int_D\sum_{j,k,l}\varepsilon_{ijk}\Bigl(\delta_{jl}\sigma_{kl}+y_j\partial_l\sigma_{kl}\Bigr)dx=\int_D\Bigl(\sum_{j,k}\varepsilon_{ijk}\sigma_{kj}+\bigl(y\times g\bigr)_i\Bigr)dx .$$ Hence (AB) reads $$\int_D\Bigl(\sum_{j,k}\varepsilon_{ijk}\sigma_{kj}+\bigl(y\times(g+b)\bigr)_i\Bigr)dx=0 .$$ By (M), the second term vanishes identically, leaving $\int_D\bigl(\sum_{j,k}\varepsilon_{ijk}\sigma_{kj}\bigr)dx=0$ for every bounded $C^1$ domain with closure in $O$. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
items/thm-nrqm-coulomb-angular-radial-unitary-reduction.md:105:[F9] Under CC, the divergence theorem for a bounded piecewise $C^1$ domain evaluates the integral of a $C^1$ vector field's divergence by its boundary flux ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]]). The fundamental theorem of calculus integrates a differentiable real function's integrable derivative to its endpoint difference; apply it to real and imaginary components for complex functions ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]).
items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:58:      locator: "Section 2.1 printed pp. 17-22 (Gauss's divergence theorem in integral form) and Section 2.2 printed pp. 22-26 (Stokes's theorem in integral form); read in the stamped full text."
items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:134:1.1 For the volume identities of (a), fix $t\in I$ and a volume test geometry $(V,\nu_V)$; the fixed-time fields $E(\cdot,t),B(\cdot,t)$ are $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of $\overline V$ by the said regularity, and the divergence theorem [F4] applies to that presentation: $\oint_{\partial V}E(\cdot,t)\cdot\nu_V\,dS=\int_V\operatorname{div}E(\cdot,t)\,d\lambda_3$ and likewise for $B$. Substituting the Gauss laws of [F1] gives $\oint_{\partial V}E(\cdot,t)\cdot\nu_V\,dS=(1/\varepsilon_0)\int_V\rho(\cdot,t)\,d\lambda_3$ and $\oint_{\partial V}B(\cdot,t)\cdot\nu_V\,dS=0$. [F1, F2, F4, given, algebra]
items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:138:1.3 For clause (b), fix a closed axis-parallel box $K=\prod_{i<3}[a_i,b_i]\subseteq\Omega$ with $a_i<b_i$ and let $V$ be its open interior, a volume test geometry with $\overline V=K$ by [F2]. The volume identity hypothesis and the divergence theorem give $0=\oint_{\partial V}E(\cdot,t)\cdot\nu_V\,dS-(1/\varepsilon_0)\int_V\rho(\cdot,t)\,d\lambda_3=\int_Vh\,d\lambda_3$ for the continuous function $h:=\operatorname{div}E-\rho/\varepsilon_0$, where linearity [F9] combines the two finite integrals and [F4] identifies the flux with $\int_V\operatorname{div}E$; by [F2] and [F9] this equals $\int_Kh\,d\lambda_3$, because $K\setminus V$ is $\lambda_3$-null. Since every closed axis-parallel box $K\subseteq\Omega$ with positive sides arises this way, [F8] with $n=3$ gives $h=0$ on $\Omega$, that is $\operatorname{div}E=\rho/\varepsilon_0$. The same computation with the second volume identity and $\int_V\operatorname{div}B=0$ gives $\operatorname{div}B=0$. [F2, F4, F8, F9, given, algebra]
items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:142:2.1 Steps 1.1 and 1.2 prove that the smooth-branch equations imply all the integral identities of (a) for every volume and patch test geometry, and steps 1.3 and 1.4 prove the converse for the same exact class; the recovery uses the open interiors of all closed axis-parallel boxes and all affine square patches, so no single selected volume or patch suffices, the marked $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is exactly the convention of the divergence theorem and of the surface-measure interface used in [F4], [F2] and [F5], and the fields, sources and constants throughout are those of the framework definitions recorded in [F10]. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, F10, given] ∎
items/pthm-em-integral-maxwell.md:146:The passage between the differential and integral forms over the declared test geometries is the standard localization argument of the cited treatments, written here with the library's own divergence theorem, patch Stokes theorem, parameter-integral and cube-localization interfaces. The textbooks supply the formulation and the physical reading; the deductions use only the imported mathematical items and the adopted smooth-branch postulate.
items/ex-emx-dipole-dark-direction.md:78:[F4] Mixed partial derivatives of a smooth function commute, and the divergence theorem applies to a smooth compactly supported vector field on a ball containing its support. ([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]], [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]])
items/ex-emx-dipole-dark-direction.md:92:3.1 The current derivative is $J_t=G''f e_3+H''(-\partial_3f\,e_1+\partial_1f\,e_3)$. Each integral $\int_{\mathbb R^3}\partial_i f\,dy$ is zero by the divergence theorem applied to $f e_i$ on a ball containing $\operatorname{supp}f$, since its boundary flux vanishes. Thus [F3] gives $p''(u)=\int J_t(y,u)\,dy=G''(u)F e_3=2\alpha F e_3$, which is nonzero and parallel to $n=e_3$. Therefore $n\times(n\times p''(u))=0$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F3, F4, algebra, A1]
