items/lem-em-newtonian-kernel-curl-identity.md:75:[F5] The spherical shell $\Omega_\varepsilon=B_R(x)\setminus\overline{B_\varepsilon(x)}$, $0<\varepsilon<R$, carries the specified finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation of [[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]] given by coordinate-maximal faces of its two sphere boundary components, with outward normals $\nu_R=(y-x)/|y-x|$ on the outer sphere and $\nu_\varepsilon=-(y-x)/|y-x|$ on the inner sphere; the divergence theorem of [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]] then holds for every $C^1$ field on a neighborhood of the compact closure.
items/lem-em-newtonian-kernel-curl-identity.md:95:1.2 Fix $x\in\mathbb R^3$ and $j,k\in\{1,2,3\}$, let $\Phi(z)=1/(4\pi|z|)$, and choose $R>0$ with $\operatorname{supp}f\subset B_R(x)$. For $0<\varepsilon<R$ let $\Omega_\varepsilon:=B_R(x)\setminus\overline{B_\varepsilon(x)}$, presented as in [F5]. On a neighborhood of the compact closure the scalar product $y\mapsto f_k(y)\Phi(x-y)$ is $C^1$, and by [F4] its partial derivative is $\partial_{y_j}(f_k\Phi(x-y))=(\partial_jf_k)(y)\Phi(x-y)+f_k(y)\partial_{y_j}\Phi(x-y)$. Applying the divergence theorem [F5] to the field $f_k\Phi(x-\cdot)e_j$ on $\Omega_\varepsilon$ gives $\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}(\partial_jf_k)\Phi(x-y)\,d\lambda_3(y)=\int_{\partial B_R(x)}f_k\Phi(x-y)(\nu_R)_j\,dS+\int_{\partial B_\varepsilon(x)}f_k\Phi(x-y)(\nu_\varepsilon)_j\,dS-\int_{\Omega_\varepsilon}f_k(y)\partial_{y_j}\Phi(x-y)\,d\lambda_3(y)$. The outer sphere contributes $0$ because $f=0$ there; the inner sphere term is bounded in absolute value, by [F8] and the bound $|\Phi|=1/(4\pi|y-x|)=1/(4\pi\varepsilon)$ there, by $\|f_k\|_\infty\frac1{4\pi\varepsilon}\,|\partial B_\varepsilon(x)|=\|f_k\|_\infty\frac{|S^2|}{4\pi}\varepsilon\to0$ as $\varepsilon\to0$ by [F6]. [given, F4, F5, F6, F8]
items/post-sr-matter-isolation-and-total-stress-energy.md:23:    - title: "B. Crowell, Special Relativity, revision 2020-12-10 (author-published undergraduate textbook, 246 PDF pages); signature (+---) converted to (-+++) and c = 1 formulas converted to x^0 = c t; Chapter 9 pp. 179-197: 9.1 pp. 179-182 (current vector, flux, charge conservation), 9.2 pp. 183-191 (stress-energy tensor, conservation and flux, symmetry, dust, rank-2 transformation law, pressure, perfect fluid, worked examples, energy conditions), 9.3 pp. 192-197 (Gauss's theorem, integral conservation laws, the energy-momentum vector, angular momentum); the printed 9.2.8 dominant energy condition p. 191 has the defective |P| > rho and is not used"
items/pthm-em-retarded-potentials.md:94:[F10] Divergence theorem for a bounded $C^1$ Euclidean domain $\Omega$ and $F\in C^1(\overline\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$: $\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}F\,dx=\int_{\partial\Omega}F\cdot\nu\,dS$ with outward normal. ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]])
items/pthm-em-retarded-potentials.md:114:1.4 Total charge. By the support hypothesis, $Q(t)=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\rho(y,t)dy=\int_{B_a(0)}\rho(y,t)dy$ for every $t$, and $Q(t)=0$ for $t<t_0$; the integral depends smoothly on $t$ with $Q'(t)=\int_{B_a(0)}\partial_t\rho(y,t)dy$ by [F11] applied to the fixed compact $\overline B_a(0)$ (the integrals over the open and closed balls agree since the smooth source vanishes on the boundary) (the integrands $\rho,\partial_t\rho$ are continuous on a neighbourhood of $\overline B_a(0)\times[t-1,t+1]$) [F5, F12]. Fix $t$ and $R>a$; the continuity equation and the divergence theorem [F10] give $\int_{B_a(0)}\operatorname{div}J(y,t)dy=\int_{B_R(0)}\operatorname{div}J(y,t)dy=\int_{\partial B_R(0)}J(y,t)\cdot\nu\,dS=0$, because $J$ vanishes outside $\overline B_a(0)$ and $R>a$; hence $Q'(t)=-\int_{B_a(0)}\operatorname{div}J(y,t)dy=0$. Thus $Q$ is constant on $\mathbb R$ and, being zero at times before $t_0$, is identically zero, which is (f). [F5, F9, F10, F11, algebra]
items/lem-gr-finite-speed-independence-of-extension.md:58:[L2] The divergence theorem applies on bounded piecewise $C^1$ domains ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]]).
items/lem-gr-finite-speed-independence-of-extension.md:70:2.1 (Truncated cone flux.) For $0<\tau<t_0$, apply the divergence theorem to $D_\tau=\{(s,x):0\le s\le\tau,|x-x_0|\le\lambda(t_0-s)\}$. Base flux is $-E(0)$ and top flux is $E(\tau)$, where $E(s)=\int_{|x-x_0|\le\lambda(t_0-s)}j^0(s,x)\,dx$. The lateral outward unit normal is $(\lambda,\xi)/\sqrt{1+\lambda^2}$, so its flux is nonnegative by step 1.1. Step 1.2 gives $E(\tau)\le E(0)+(M/a)\int_0^\tau E(s)\,ds$. The domains avoid the cone vertex, so their boundaries are piecewise C1 as required. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L2, algebra]
items/lem-em-interface-derivatives.md:67:[F3] Divergence theorem and domains: under $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, a bounded open set with a specified finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation admits the divergence theorem $\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}G=\sum_j\int_{S_j}G\cdot\nu_j\,dS$ for $G\in C^1(\overline\Omega)$ ([[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]], [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]).
items/lem-em-interface-derivatives.md:75:**Proof technique:** direct; reduce to a local single-patch computation by a partition of unity, integrate by parts on each side with the divergence theorem, and read off the jump terms.
items/lem-em-interface-derivatives.md:82:2.1 Local computation: let $\varphi\in\mathcal D(\Omega)$ have support in a chart box $B=B_k$ as in step 1.2. By [F2], $\langle\partial_iF_j,\varphi\rangle=-\int_{\Omega}F_j\partial_i\varphi=-\int_{\Omega_+}F_j\partial_i\varphi-\int_{\Omega_-}F_j\partial_i\varphi$. On each side apply the divergence theorem [F3] to $G^\pm:=F_j\varphi\,e_i$. Its values and first spatial derivatives extend continuously to the closed half-box by the one-sided trace hypothesis and the product rule, so it belongs to $C^1(\overline{B_\pm})$ in the stated up-to-boundary convention, which requires no ambient extension. It vanishes near every outer box face because $\operatorname{supp}\varphi\subseteq B$. For complex $\varphi$, apply the real divergence theorem separately to its real and imaginary parts; the outward normal of $\Omega_+$ on the interface face is $-n$ and that of $\Omega_-$ is $+n$, so $\int_{\Omega_+}\partial_i(F_j\varphi)=-\int_{S\cap B}(F_j)_+\varphi\,n_i\,dS$ and $\int_{\Omega_-}\partial_i(F_j\varphi)=\int_{S\cap B}(F_j)_-\varphi\,n_i\,dS$. Substituting $\int_{\Omega_\pm}F_j\partial_i\varphi=\int_{\Omega_\pm}\partial_i(F_j\varphi)-\int_{\Omega_\pm}(\partial_iF_j)\varphi$ gives $\langle\partial_iF_j,\varphi\rangle=\int_{\Omega}\{\partial_iF_j\}\varphi+\int_{S}((F_j)_+-(F_j)_-)\varphi\,n_i\,dS=\int_{\Omega}\{\partial_iF_j\}\varphi+\langle n_i[F_j]\delta_S,\varphi\rangle$, which is the componentwise identity. [step 1.2, F2, F3, algebra]
items/lem-em-smooth-domain-elliptic-regularity.md:75:1.1 Local reduction: in an interior patch, or in a flattened smooth boundary or interface patch, write the weak equation in divergence form $\int a_{ij}\partial_ju\,\partial_iv=\int fv+\int G_i\partial_iv$ with $\tilde a=|\det D\Phi^{-1}|D\Phi\,a\,D\Phi^{\mathsf T}$ uniformly elliptic by the positivity of the Jacobian bounds and the bounds on $D\Phi$ ([[lem-c-k-boundary-flattening-preserves-wkp-locally]]); Dirichlet data are removed by subtracting a smooth lift $g(y)\eta(t)$ built from a finite subordinate smooth partition and a normal cutoff, and smooth Neumann flux is converted to smooth volume data by a collar vector field $G$ with $G\cdot n=g$ and the divergence theorem, so in every case the local datum has the form $f\in C^\infty$ plus a divergence term with smooth $G$. [given, F1, F2, F5, algebra]
items/lem-em-parameter-dependent-smooth-poisson-boundary-solvers.md:72:[F2] **Flattening, traces and calculus.** Chart flattening preserves local Sobolev classes with the chain rule; the first Green identity, the Euclidean divergence theorem, the difference-quotient tools, the weak Leibniz rule, half-space extension, compactly supported smooth density and the FTC/chain/algebra rules are available ([[lem-c-k-boundary-flattening-preserves-wkp-locally]], [[cor-first-green-identity-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]], [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]], [[def-first-difference-quotient]], [[lem-difference-quotient-integration-by-parts]], [[thm-difference-quotient-characterisation-of-w-one-p-for-p-greater-than-one]], [[lem-weak-leibniz-rule-with-a-smooth-factor]], [[thm-wkp-extension-from-a-half-space]], [[cor-compactly-supported-smooth-functions-are-dense-in-wkp-of-rn]], [[thm-ftc-first-part]], [[thm-ftc-second-part]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]).
