items/pthm-em-poynting-balance.md:118:**Proof technique:** direct; differentiate the quadratic energy density along time, expand the divergence of the Poynting vector by the cross-product identity, cancel the magnetic terms with the two curl equations, and integrate the local identity over an admissible fixed volume with the divergence theorem and compact-parameter differentiation.
items/pthm-em-poynting-balance.md:128:3.1 Fixed-volume identity: by step 1.3 and clause (a) just proved, $\frac{d}{dt}\int_{\Omega'}u\,dx=\int_{\Omega'}\partial_tu\,dx=-\int_{\Omega'}J\cdot E\,dx-\int_{\Omega'}\operatorname{div}S(\cdot,t)\,dx$. The field $S(\cdot,t)$ is $C^1$ on a neighbourhood of $\overline{\Omega'}$ by [F3] and step 1.1, so the divergence theorem of [F5] applies to it and $\int_{\Omega'}\operatorname{div}S(\cdot,t)\,dx=\oint_{\partial\Omega'}S\cdot n\,dS$ as a finite face sum; substituting gives clause (b), under the stated $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$. [step 1.2, step 1.3, step 2.1, F5, algebra]
items/thm-fd-common-wall-residual-error.md:93:The bounded $C^1$ divergence theorem used for the wall energy identity: [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]].
items/thm-cm-burgers-riemann-shock-and-rarefaction.md:66:Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the Lebesgue weak formulation and the imported divergence theorem.
items/thm-cm-burgers-riemann-shock-and-rarefaction.md:96:with $[h]:=h_+-h_-$ the jump from the left trace to the right trace across the line $x=st$. **Proof.** Put $J:=(u\varphi,F(u)\varphi)$ on $(0,\infty)\times\mathbb R$, and let $D_\pm:=\{(t,x):0<t<T,\ \pm(x-st)>0,\ |x|<R\}$ for $T,R$ large enough that the support of $\varphi$ avoids the top and lateral boundaries; it may meet the initial boundary. These are bounded piecewise $C^1$ domains, and $\operatorname{div}J=u\varphi_t+F(u)\varphi_x$ on each (the classical equation kills the derivative of $u$). The divergence theorem on a plane polygon ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]], [[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]], [[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]]) gives $\int\int_{D_\pm}\operatorname{div}J=\int_{\partial D_\pm}J\cdot\nu\,dS$. The outward normals are $(-1,0)$ on the bottom segments $\{t=0\}$, $(0,-1)$ or $(0,1)$ on the far vertical sides (where $\varphi=0$), $ (s,-1)/\sqrt{1+s^2}$ on the interface from $D_+$ and $(-s,1)/\sqrt{1+s^2}$ from $D_-$. Summing the two identities, the vertical sides contribute $0$, the bottom segments contribute $-\int f\varphi(0,x)dx$ because $J\cdot(-1,0)=-u\varphi$, and the interface contributes $\int_0^\infty\varphi(t,st)\,(s\,[u]-[F(u)])dt$: indeed, with $dS=\sqrt{1+s^2}\,dt$, $J\cdot\nu_+=[su_+-F(u_+)]\varphi/\sqrt{1+s^2}$ and $J\cdot\nu_-=[-su_-+F(u_-)]\varphi/\sqrt{1+s^2}$. Rearranging gives the displayed identity. ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one]], [[def-integral-over-a-measurable-set]].)
items/thm-cm-burgers-riemann-shock-and-rarefaction.md:108:1.2 For (c), first integrate on $\varepsilon<t<T$, splitting the truncated domain into the left constant region, the fan, and the right constant region, with $T$ and the spatial cutoff outside the support of the test function. There $u=x/t$ is $C^1$ on the closed fan and satisfies $u_t+uu_x=0$ by [F3]; the constant pieces do too. Apply the divergence theorem to each piece. On a shared fan edge the outward normals are opposite and the traces of $u$, $F(u)$ and $\varphi$ agree, so the two boundary integrals cancel. The bottom term is $-\int u(\varepsilon,x)\varphi(\varepsilon,x)\,dx$. As $\varepsilon\downarrow0$, $u(\varepsilon,x)$ differs from $f(x)$ only between the rays $x=u_L\varepsilon$ and $x=u_R\varepsilon$, an interval of length $(u_R-u_L)\varepsilon$, and it is uniformly bounded by $\max(|u_L|,|u_R|)$. Thus the difference in the bottom integral tends to zero; continuity of $\varphi$ on its compact support replaces $\varphi(\varepsilon,x)$ by $\varphi(0,x)$. The integrands over $0<t<\varepsilon$ are bounded on a fixed compact spatial support, so their integrals also tend to zero. Taking the limit gives (W) with the actual initial term. [given, F1, F3, algebra]
items/ex-fd-conduction-energy-entropy.md:91:2.1 The constant density and zero velocity satisfy mass balance; $\sigma=b=0$ satisfy momentum balance. Since $D=0$, $e=cT$, $q=-\kappa\nabla T$, and $r=0$, the internal-energy equation in [F1] reduces to $\rho c\,\partial_tT=\kappa\Delta T$, which holds by step 1.1. For the material formulation, choose a bounded $C^1$ ball $B$ containing $Q$ in its interior, take $U=\mathbb R^3$, $\chi(t,a)=a$ and $\rho_0=\rho$. Every admissible material subregion $V\Subset B$ is stationary. Constant mass, zero momentum and zero stress give its mass, linear and angular balances. Integrating $\rho c\,\partial_tT+\operatorname{div}q=0$ over $V$, with differentiation permitted by smoothness on compact closures and the divergence theorem [F2] under [A4], gives the adopted material total-energy balance. Thus the hypotheses of [F1], including $Q\subset B$ for all $t\in I$, are met. Periodicity is [A2]. [given, A1, A2, A4, F1, F2, step 1.1]
items/thm-fd-x10.md:82:2.1 Put $E(t)=\int_Q|w|^2$. Multiply the perturbation equation in step 1.1 by $w$ and integrate over one cell. The $U_b$ transport integral is $\frac12\int_Q\nabla\cdot(U_b|w|^2)=0$: $\nabla\cdot U_b=0$, its normal component vanishes at the $y$ walls, and paired periodic-face terms cancel. The nonlinear transport integral is $\frac12\int_Q\nabla\cdot(w|w|^2)=0$ because $\nabla\cdot w=0$, $w=0$ at the walls, and periodic-face terms cancel. The pressure integral is $-\int_Q\nabla\cdot(qw)+\int_Qq\nabla\cdot w=0$ for the same wall and periodic reasons. Componentwise integration by parts gives $\int_Qw\cdot\Delta w=-\|\nabla w\|_{L^2(Q)}^2$, with zero wall term and canceling periodic faces. Since $(w\cdot\nabla)U_b=S w_2(1,0,0)$, the remaining identity is $\frac12E'(t)=-\nu\|\nabla w\|_{L^2(Q)}^2-S\int_Qw_1w_2$. These integrations use the one-dimensional FTC on one periodic cell, not the bounded $C^1$ Euclidean divergence theorem. [given, A1, step 1.1, algebra]
items/cex-tgr-open-region-entropy-export.md:43:      locator: "Background for the curved-space divergence theorem, slice charge and outward side flux. The counterexample is a new mathematical construction, not a reported physical solution or observation."
items/pthm-fd-wall-mechanical-power-balance.md:133:2.1 Put $k=\rho|u|^2/2$. Since $\rho$ is constant and $\operatorname{div}u=0$, the product rule gives $\partial_t k+\operatorname{div}(ku)=\rho u\cdot(\partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u)$. In branch (B), the divergence theorem applied to $ku$ gives boundary flux $k(u\cdot n)=0$; in branch (P), periodic-coordinate endpoint values cancel and the two wall fluxes vanish because $u\cdot n=0$. Substituting the local equation from step 1.1 therefore yields $K'=\int_\Omega u\cdot\operatorname{div}\sigma\,dx+\int_\Omega\rho b\cdot u\,dx$. [given, F1, F3, F4, F6, A2, step 1.1, algebra]
