items/lem-em-ideal-line-current-field.md:84:[F9] The cylindrical annulus $V_\varepsilon=\{\varepsilon<\rho<R,\ |x_3|<R\}$, $0<\varepsilon<R$, has the finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation of [[def-bounded-piecewise-c-one-euclidean-domain]] given by two planar cap annuli and four quarter-cylinder faces on each of $\rho=\varepsilon$ and $\rho=R$. At radius $b\in\{\varepsilon,R\}$ the faces are $X_b([j\pi/2,(j+1)\pi/2]\times[-R,R])$, $j=0,1,2,3$, with $X_b(\theta,z)=(b\cos\theta,b\sin\theta,z)$, each restricted from an injective regular chart on a larger angular interval of length less than $2\pi$. The seams and rims are surface-null: in lateral coordinates they lie on finitely many horizontal or vertical parameter lines, null by [F3] and [F5], and a planar rim circle has at-most-two-point sections, giving nullity by the same facts. The outward normal on the inner cylinder is $-e_\rho$. The divergence theorem of [[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains]] applies to every $C^1$ field on a neighborhood of the compact closure.
items/lem-em-ideal-line-current-field.md:96:**Proof technique:** direct; local integrability by polar coordinates, one excision limit, and the divergence theorem on a cylindrical annulus.
items/lem-em-ideal-line-current-field.md:108:4.1 On the neighborhood $\{\rho>\varepsilon/2\}$ of the compact closure $\overline{V_\varepsilon}$ the field $\psi\times f$ is $C^1$, since $f$ is smooth there, and by [F7] its divergence is $\operatorname{div}(\psi\times f)=f\cdot\operatorname{curl}\psi-\psi\cdot\operatorname{curl}f$; here $\operatorname{curl}f=0$ on $V_\varepsilon$ by step 1.2. Applying the divergence theorem [F9] on $V_\varepsilon$ gives $\int_{V_\varepsilon}f\cdot\operatorname{curl}\psi\,d\lambda_3=\int_{\partial V_\varepsilon}(\psi\times f)\cdot\nu\,dS$. The faces at $\rho=R$ and at $|x_3|=R$ contribute $0$ because $\operatorname{supp}\psi\subset\{\rho<R,\ |x_3|<R\}$; on the inner cylinder the outward normal is $-e_\rho$ and $f=e_\varphi/\varepsilon$, so by [F8] the remaining term is $(\psi\times e_\varphi)\cdot(-e_\rho)/\varepsilon=-\psi\cdot(e_\varphi\times e_\rho)/\varepsilon=\psi_3/\varepsilon$, and the surface integral of [F10] over the chart $X(\theta,z)=(\varepsilon\cos\theta,\varepsilon\sin\theta,z)$ gives $\int_{\rho=\varepsilon,|x_3|<R}\frac{\psi_3}{\varepsilon}\,dS=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_{-R}^{R}\psi_3(\varepsilon\cos\theta,\varepsilon\sin\theta,z)\,dz\,d\theta .$ [given, step 1.2, step 3.1, F7, F8, F9, F10]
items/lem-em-ideal-line-current-field.md:110:4.2 On the same neighborhood the field $\varphi f$ is $C^1$, and $\operatorname{div}(\varphi f)=f\cdot\nabla\varphi+\varphi\operatorname{div}f$ by [F7]; here $\operatorname{div}f=0$ on $V_\varepsilon$ by step 1.2. The divergence theorem [F9] gives $\int_{V_\varepsilon}f\cdot\nabla\varphi\,d\lambda_3=\int_{\partial V_\varepsilon}\varphi\,f\cdot\nu\,dS$. On both cylinder faces $f\cdot\nu=0$ because $f\perp e_\rho$, and on the two cap annuli $\varphi=0$ because $\operatorname{supp}\varphi\subset\{|x_3|<R\}$; hence $\int_{V_\varepsilon}f\cdot\nabla\varphi\,d\lambda_3=0$ for every $\varepsilon$. [given, step 1.2, step 3.1, F7, F9]
items/thm-cm-local-momentum-balance.md:47:Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), as used by the transport and divergence theorems. Let $I\subseteq\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval, let $B\subseteq\mathbb R^3$ be a material region, let $\chi:I\times O\to\mathbb R^3$ be a motion of $B$ on $I$ with spatial velocity $u$ and material derivative $D_t$ ([[def-cm-continuum-motion]], [[def-cm-euclidean-spatial-calculus]]), and let $J_0\subseteq I$ be an open interval with $\overline{J_0}\subseteq I$. Define the swept region $\Sigma=\{(t,x):t\in\overline{J_0},\ x\in\chi_t(\overline B)\}$.
items/thm-cm-local-momentum-balance.md:73:[F2] Divergence theorem on a bounded $C^1$ domain: for $F\in C^1(\overline\Omega;\mathbb R^3)$, $\int_\Omega\operatorname{div}F\,dx=\int_{\partial\Omega}F\cdot\nu\,dS$, with interior-up-to-boundary regularity ([[thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-c-one-euclidean-domains]], [[def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal]]). Applied to the rows $F_i:=(\sigma_{i1},\sigma_{i2},\sigma_{i3})$ on $\Omega=\chi_t(B')$, whose closure lies in $U$, it gives $\int_{\partial\chi_t(B')}\sigma\nu\,dS=\int_{\chi_t(B')}g\,dx$ componentwise; the hypothesis (L) provides the surface integral with the outward normal convention of [[def-cm-traction-and-stress]].
