### def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data ###
## Definition

Assume the axiom of countable choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional, Hausdorff, second-countable smooth manifold without boundary. A **non-Abelian gauge datum on $M$** consists of the following specified objects.

First choose a finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$ that is compact in its manifold topology (every open cover has a finite subcover) and noncommutative (there are $g,h\in G$ with $gh\ne hg$). The actual group $G$, including its global form, is part of the datum. Its Lie algebra is $\mathfrak g=\operatorname{Lie}(G)=T_eG$ with the bracket transported from left-invariant smooth vector fields.

Choose a smooth right principal $G$-bundle $\pi:P\to M$: the projection and right action are smooth, the action is free and transitive on each fibre, and there are local trivializations $\pi^{-1}(U)\cong U\times G$ that are equivariant diffeomorphisms over each open $U\subseteq M$.

Choose a specified set $I$ of representation labels and, for each $a\in I$, a finite-dimensional complex vector space $V_a$, a positive-definite Hermitian form $h_a$, and a smooth group homomorphism $\rho_a:G\to U(V_a,h_a)$. Here $U(V_a,h_a)$ is the group of complex-linear isometries of $h_a$, and a representation is the corresponding finite-dimensional group action. Its associated vector bundle is the quotient $E_a=P\times_{\rho_a}V_a$ with relation $[pg,v]=[p,\rho_a(g)v]$.

Two smooth right principal $G$-bundles over $M$ are isomorphic over $M$ when a smooth diffeomorphism between their total spaces covers $\operatorname{id}_M$ and commutes with the right $G$-actions. A **bundle sector** is one equivalence class under this relation. The datum specifies a set $\Sigma$ of admitted bundle sectors and requires $[P]\in\Sigma$; a model may take $\Sigma=\{[P]\}$. The Lie algebra alone does not replace the chosen group or sector. This definition does not choose a connection, an action functional, or a quantum theory.


### def-qftx-principal-connection-form ###
## Definition

Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, and let $\pi:P\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle for a finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$, with $\mathfrak g=\operatorname{Lie}(G)=T_eG$. A supplied principal connection is a smooth, right-invariant complement $TP=VP\oplus H$, where $VP=\ker d\pi$; write $v:TP\to VP$ for the projection along $H$, and let $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$ be its vertical-valued curvature. For $Z\in\mathfrak g$ and $p\in P$, define the fundamental vertical vector $Z_P(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}p\exp_G(tZ)$. The orbit differential $\zeta_p: \mathfrak g\to V_pP$, $Z\mapsto Z_P(p)$, is a linear isomorphism, as proved below. Define the **principal connection 1-form** by

$$\omega_p(X):=\zeta_p^{-1}(v_pX),\qquad X\in T_pP.$$

Then $\omega$ is a smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued 1-form satisfying $\omega(Z_P)=Z$, $\ker\omega=H$, and $R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega$ for every fixed $g\in G$. Conversely, a smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued 1-form with the reproduction and equivariance properties determines the principal connection $H=\ker\omega$. The converted vertical curvature $\kappa_H:=\omega\circ K_H$ is a $\mathfrak g$-valued alternating 2-form; its equality with the Cartan curvature is established in the curvature lemma.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, a smooth right principal $G$-bundle $\pi:P\to M$, and a supplied principal connection $H$.

[A1] $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is countable choice: every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function. [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] The principal bundle has a smooth right action with equivariant local principal charts. [[def-smooth-principal-g-bundle]]

[F2] A principal connection is a smooth right-invariant complement $TP=VP\oplus H$, and $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$. [[def-principal-connection-and-vertical-curvature]]

[F3] $\mathfrak g=T_eG$ with the bracket transported from left-invariant smooth vector fields is a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra. [[thm-the-tangent-space-at-the-identity-is-a-lie-algebra]]

[F4] $\exp_G(tZ)$ is the one-parameter subgroup whose initial tangent is $Z$. [[def-exponential-map-of-a-lie-group]], [[thm-one-parameter-subgroups-are-integral-curves-of-left-invariant-fields]]

[F5] $\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e$, where $C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}$, and $\operatorname{Ad}$ is a smooth group representation. [[def-conjugation-and-the-adjoint-representation-of-a-lie-group]], [[prop-adjoint-is-a-smooth-lie-group-representation]]

[F6] For smooth maps, differentials compose by the chain rule. [[def-differential-of-a-smooth-map]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-differentials-of-smooth-maps]]

## Proof

1.1 Write $p=s(x)a$ in an equivariant principal chart, with $s$ its local section and $a\in G$. Then $\phi_p(h)=p h=s(x)(a h)$; freeness and transitivity give bijectivity on the fibre, and this coordinate formula gives a smooth inverse. Hence $\phi_p:G\to P_x$, $h\mapsto p h$, is a diffeomorphism, so its differential at $e$ is a linear isomorphism $T_eG\to T_pP_x=V_pP$. The curve $t\mapsto p\exp_G(tZ)$ is $\phi_p\circ\exp_G(tZ)$, so its derivative is exactly $d(\phi_p)_eZ$; hence $\zeta_p$ is the stated isomorphism. [A1, F1, F3, F4, F6]

2.1 In the same chart, $\zeta_p$ is the derivative of the smooth action map and its inverse is the derivative of the inverse fibre coordinate, so the family $\zeta$ and its inverse are smooth bundle maps. The complement $TP=VP\oplus H$ gives a smooth bundle projection $v$, so $\omega=\zeta^{-1}\circ v$ is a smooth fibrewise-linear 1-form. On a vertical vector $Z_P(p)=\zeta_p(Z)$, the projection is the vector itself; therefore $\omega(Z_P)=Z$. Also $v_pX=0$ exactly when $X\in H_p$, so $\ker\omega=H$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1]

2.2 For $g\in G$, let $R_g(p)=pg$. The maps $R_g\circ\phi_p$ and $\phi_{pg}\circ C_{g^{-1}}$ agree because $p h g=pg(g^{-1}hg)$. Differentiating at $e$ by [F6] gives $d(R_g)_p Z_P(p)=(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Z)_P(pg)$. Since $H$ and $VP$ are right-invariant, their projections commute with $dR_g$; applying the definition of $\omega$ yields $\omega_{pg}(dR_gX)=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega_p(X)$. [F1, F2, F5, F6, step 1.1]

3.1 Conversely, if $\omega$ has the stated reproduction and equivariance properties, define $v:=\zeta\circ\omega$ and $H:=\ker\omega$. Reproduction gives $v\circ\zeta=\zeta$, hence $v^2=v$, $\operatorname{im}v=VP$, and $TP=VP\oplus H$. Equivariance of $\omega$ and the orbit differential identity from step 2.2 make $H$ right-invariant; smoothness of $\omega$ makes it a smooth subbundle. Thus this is a principal connection and the construction is inverse to step 2.1. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2]

4.1 The imported curvature $K_H$ is a smooth alternating map with values in $VP$, and $\omega$ restricts to the inverse of $\zeta:P\times\mathfrak g\to VP$; consequently $\kappa_H=\omega\circ K_H$ is a well-defined smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued alternating 2-form. This is only the conversion of its target fibre; the Cartan identity is proved separately. [F2, step 1.1, step 2.1] ∎




### def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket ###
## Definition

Let $M$ be a smooth manifold and $\mathfrak g$ a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra. For homogeneous $\alpha\in\Omega^p(M,\mathfrak g)$ and $\beta\in\Omega^q(M,\mathfrak g)$, define their **graded bracket** by

$$[\alpha\wedge\beta](X_1,\ldots,X_{p+q}) := \frac{1}{p!q!}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{p+q}}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)[\alpha(X_{\sigma(1)},\ldots,X_{\sigma(p)}),\beta(X_{\sigma(p+1)},\ldots,X_{\sigma(p+q)})].$$

Here $\Omega^p(M,\mathfrak g)$ is the space of smooth alternating $p$-linear forms with values in $\mathfrak g$, the inner bracket is the Lie-algebra bracket, and the sum is over all permutations. This is a smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued $(p+q)$-form. It obeys graded skew-symmetry $[\alpha\wedge\beta]=-(-1)^{pq}[\beta\wedge\alpha]$, graded Jacobi

$$\left[\alpha\wedge\left[\beta\wedge\gamma\right]\right]=\left[\left[\alpha\wedge\beta\right]\wedge\gamma\right]+(-1)^{pq}\left[\beta\wedge\left[\alpha\wedge\gamma\right]\right],$$

and the exterior-derivative rule

$$d[\alpha\wedge\beta]=[d\alpha\wedge\beta]+(-1)^p[\alpha\wedge d\beta].$$

In particular, for $\mathfrak g$-valued 1-forms $A,B$, $[A\wedge B](X,Y)=[A(X),B(Y)]-[A(Y),B(X)]$, so $\tfrac12[A\wedge A](X,Y)=[A(X),A(Y)]$. This fixes the normalization used by the Cartan curvature formula.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A smooth manifold $M$, a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra $\mathfrak g$, and homogeneous forms $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ of degrees $p,q,r$.

[F1] A finite-dimensional real Lie algebra has a bilinear alternating bracket satisfying Jacobi. [[def-finite-dimensional-lie-algebra]]

[F2] A smooth $\mathfrak g$-valued form has smooth scalar components in any finite basis, and its exterior derivative is taken componentwise. [[def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative]]

[F3] The scalar exterior derivative is a graded derivation of the wedge product. [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-is-a-graded-derivation]]

[F4] Differential forms form an associative graded-commutative algebra. [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]], [[prop-differential-forms-form-a-graded-commutative-algebra]]

## Proof

1.1 Choose a finite basis $(e_a)_{a=1}^n$ of $\mathfrak g$ with dual basis $(e^a)_{a=1}^n$, and write $\alpha=\sum_a e_a\alpha^a$, $\beta=\sum_b e_b\beta^b$, where $\alpha^a=e^a\circ\alpha$ and $\beta^b=e^b\circ\beta$ are smooth scalar forms. Expanding the alternating permutation sum gives $[\alpha\wedge\beta]=\sum_{a,b}[e_a,e_b]\,\alpha^a\wedge\beta^b$; this finite sum is smooth and alternating, hence is in $\Omega^{p+q}(M,\mathfrak g)$. [F1, F2, F4, construct]

2.1 Swapping the two blocks of $p$ and $q$ arguments in the defining permutation sum contributes the sign $(-1)^{pq}$; antisymmetry of the inner Lie bracket contributes the minus sign. Therefore $[\alpha\wedge\beta]=-(-1)^{pq}[\beta\wedge\alpha]$. [F1, F4, step 1.1, algebra]

2.2 In the finite basis expansion of step 1.1, apply the scalar graded derivation rule [F3] to each $\alpha^a\wedge\beta^b$ and retain the fixed coefficient $[e_a,e_b]$. Summing the resulting finite terms gives $d[\alpha\wedge\beta]=[d\alpha\wedge\beta]+(-1)^p[\alpha\wedge d\beta]$. [F2, F3, step 1.1]

2.3 For homogeneous $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$, the same basis expansion writes $\left[\alpha\wedge\left[\beta\wedge\gamma\right]\right]$ with coefficient $[e_a,[e_b,e_c]]$ and form factor $\alpha^a\wedge\beta^b\wedge\gamma^c$. The right-hand terms have coefficients $\left[\,\left[e_a,e_b\right],e_c\right]$ and, after commuting $\beta^b$ past $\alpha^a$ using [F4], $[e_b,[e_a,e_c]]$, with the same form factor. The equality of coefficients is exactly the Lie-algebra Jacobi identity in [F1], so the graded Jacobi formula follows. [F1, F4, step 1.1]

3.1 If $\mathfrak g=0$, all forms and brackets are zero; if $p=0$ or $q=0$, the same formulas use the empty wedge with sign $+1$. The finite basis in step 1.1 is one finite-dimensional coordinate calculation, not a choice from a family. The definition and identities hold on the empty manifold as well. [F1, F2, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.3] ∎




### def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative ###
## Definition

Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, $G$ a finite-dimensional real Lie group, $\pi:P\to M$ a smooth right principal $G$-bundle, and $\omega$ the principal connection 1-form of a supplied horizontal distribution. Fix a finite-dimensional complex representation $\rho:G\to\operatorname{GL}_{\mathbb C}(V)$ that is smooth, and let $E=P\times_\rho V$ be its associated vector bundle with relation $[pg,v]=[p,\rho(g)v]$. Define $\rho_*:=d\rho_e:\mathfrak g\to\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(V)$, where the differential is taken after viewing $\rho$ as a smooth matrix-valued map. For any open cover with smooth local sections $s_i:U_i\to P$, write $A_i=s_i^*\omega$. A smooth section $\sigma$ of $E$ has unique local components $\psi_i:U_i\to V$ given by $\sigma(x)=[s_i(x),\psi_i(x)]$. Define the **associated covariant derivative** $\nabla^\omega\sigma$ by its local components

$$
(\nabla^\omega\sigma)_i=d\psi_i+\rho_*(A_i)\psi_i,
$$

where $(\rho_*(A_i)\psi_i)(X)=\rho_*(A_i(X))\psi_i$ for $X\in TU_i$. These expressions patch to a smooth $E$-valued 1-form on $M$, independently of the chosen local sections, and satisfy $\nabla^\omega(f\sigma)=df\otimes\sigma+f\nabla^\omega\sigma$ for every smooth scalar function $f$ on $M$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, a smooth right principal bundle $P\to M$, a principal connection form $\omega$, a smooth finite-dimensional complex representation $\rho$, its associated vector bundle $E$, and a smooth section $\sigma$ of $E$.

[A1] The only inherited choice assumption is $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, used in the supplied Lie-group tangent-bracket and horizontal-connection constructions. [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] The smooth right action has equivariant local principal charts and each point has unique group coordinates relative to a local section. [[def-smooth-principal-g-bundle]]

[F2] The associated quotient relation is $[pg,v]=[p,\rho(g)v]$. [[def-associated-bundle-to-a-principal-bundle-and-representation]]

[F3] The quotient has smooth vector-bundle charts induced by local sections; its coordinate transition is the representation applied to the principal transition element. [[thm-associated-vector-bundle-is-well-defined]]

[F4] The principal form reproduces fundamental fields and obeys $R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega$. [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]]

[F5] The left Maurer–Cartan form is $(\theta_L)_g=d(L_{g^{-1}})_g$, so $\theta_L(g)(X)=g^{-1}dg(X)$ in group notation. [[def-left-maurer-cartan-form]]

[F6] Differentials are linear and satisfy the chain rule. [[def-differential-of-a-smooth-map]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-differentials-of-smooth-maps]]

[F7] For fixed $g\in G$, the adjoint map is $\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e$, where $C_g(h)=ghg^{-1}$. [[def-conjugation-and-the-adjoint-representation-of-a-lie-group]]

[F8] The curve $t\mapsto\exp_G(tZ)$ has initial tangent $Z$ at the identity. [[def-exponential-map-of-a-lie-group]], [[thm-one-parameter-subgroups-are-integral-curves-of-left-invariant-fields]]

[F9] The representation $\rho$ is a group homomorphism, so $\rho(gh)=\rho(g)\rho(h)$ and $\rho(e)=\operatorname{id}_V$. [[def-finite-dimensional-representation-of-a-group-over-a-field]]

## Proof

1.1 On a principal chart, each $p\in\pi^{-1}(U_i)$ has a unique expression $p=s_i(x)h$. The associated-bundle chart therefore assigns $[s_i(x)h,v]$ the vector coordinate $\rho(h)v$. In particular $[s_i(x),\psi_i(x)]$ determines a unique smooth component $\psi_i$. If $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$, then $[s_j,\psi_j]=[s_i,\rho(g_{ij})\psi_j]$; equality with $[s_i,\psi_i]$ gives $\psi_j=\rho(g_{ij}^{-1})\psi_i$. [F1, F2, F3, given]

2.1 In a finite basis of $V$, $\operatorname{GL}_{\mathbb C}(V)$ is the open subset where the determinant is nonzero, so its tangent space at the identity is $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}(V)$. Thus the differential $\rho_*$ is real-linear by [F6]. For fixed $g$, [F9] gives $\rho\circ C_{g^{-1}}=C_{\rho(g^{-1})}\circ\rho$. The derivative of target conjugation is the linear map $T\mapsto\rho(g^{-1})T\rho(g)$, while the derivative of source conjugation is $\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}$ by [F7]. The chain rule [F6] therefore gives $\rho_*(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}Z)=\rho(g^{-1})\rho_*(Z)\rho(g)$. For a smooth transition $g_{ij}:U_i\cap U_j\to G$, differentiation of $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$ decomposes a tangent vector into the right translate of the tangent from $s_i$ and the fundamental vertical vector with generator $\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$, by [F5]. Reproduction and equivariance in [F4] yield $A_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+\theta_L(g_{ij})$. [A1, F1, F4, F5, F6, F7, F9, step 1.1]

3.1 Put $g=g_{ij}(x)$, $Z=\theta_L(g)(X)$, and $R=\rho(g^{-1})$. Along a curve through $x$ tangent to $X$, the transition curve has initial tangent $d(L_g)_eZ$, by [F5]. The curve $t\mapsto g\exp_G(tZ)$ has the same initial value and tangent, so the differentials of smooth composites along these curves agree by [F6]. Its inverse is exactly $\exp_G(-tZ)g^{-1}$; [F8] and the homomorphism law [F9] therefore give $dR(X)=-\rho_*(Z)R$. Using step 1.1, step 2.1, and the adjoint-intertwining identity in step 2.1, $d\psi_j(X)+\rho_*(A_j(X))\psi_j=-\rho_*(Z)R\psi_i+R\,d\psi_i(X)+\rho_*(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}A_i(X))R\psi_i+\rho_*(Z)R\psi_i=R\bigl(d\psi_i(X)+\rho_*(A_i(X))\psi_i\bigr)$. The two Maurer–Cartan terms cancel, and the remaining equality uses $\rho(g)R=\mathrm{id}_V$. This is precisely the associated-bundle coordinate transition for an $E$-valued 1-form, so the local expressions define one global smooth $E$-valued 1-form and do not depend on the selected sections. [F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F8, F9, step 1.1, step 2.1]

4.1 In any local chart, multiplication by a scalar function obeys $d(f\psi_i)=df\otimes\psi_i+f\,d\psi_i$; the endomorphism term obeys $\rho_*(A_i)(f\psi_i)=f\rho_*(A_i)\psi_i$ by pointwise linearity. Adding these identities gives $(\nabla^\omega(f\sigma))_i=df\otimes\psi_i+f(\nabla^\omega\sigma)_i$, which is the asserted Leibniz rule in the associated bundle. If $V=0$, every term is zero and the same formulas hold; the argument is pointwise and also applies on an empty base. [F2, F3, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎



### lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi ###
## Statement

Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, and let $\pi:P\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle with a principal connection form $\omega$ associated to $H$, using $Z_P(p)=\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_0p\exp_G(tZ)$ for the right action. For Lie-algebra-valued forms use the graded bracket defined by alternating shuffle and the Lie bracket; in degree one, $[A\wedge B](X,Y)=[A(X),B(Y)]-[A(Y),B(X)]$. Define

$$\Omega_\omega:=d\omega+\frac12[\omega\wedge\omega],\qquad D_\omega\eta:=d\eta+[\omega\wedge\eta].$$

Then $\Omega_\omega$ is horizontal and $\operatorname{Ad}$-equivariant, and $\Omega_\omega=\omega\circ K_H$ for the imported vertical-valued curvature $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$. For any trivializing cover with smooth sections $s_i:U_i\to P$, set $A_i=s_i^*\omega$ and $F_i=s_i^*\Omega_\omega$. On an overlap, let $g_{ij}:U_i\cap U_j\to G$ be the smooth map determined by $s_j=s_i g_{ij}$, and write $\theta_L(g_{ij})=g_{ij}^*\theta_L$ for the left Maurer–Cartan form. Then

$$A_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i+\theta_L(g_{ij}),\qquad F_i=dA_i+\frac12[A_i\wedge A_i],\qquad F_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}F_i,\qquad D_{A_i}F_i=0,$$

where $D_{A_i}\eta:=d\eta+[A_i\wedge\eta]$. The transition equations concern changes of local sections; they do not identify an arbitrary map $M\to G$ with a global automorphism of a nontrivial bundle.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, a smooth right principal $G$-bundle $P\to M$, a principal connection $H$, its form $\omega$, and local smooth sections $s_i$.

[A1] $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is countable choice, inherited through the Lie-group tangent-bracket and horizontal-connection inputs. [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] The connection form satisfies $\omega(Z_P)=Z$, $\ker\omega=H$, and $R_g^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega$; $\kappa_H=\omega\circ K_H$. [[def-qftx-principal-connection-form]]

[F2] The vertical curvature is $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$, with $TP=VP\oplus H$ and right-invariant horizontal distribution. [[def-principal-connection-and-vertical-curvature]]

[F3] The graded bracket of $\mathfrak g$-valued forms has the shuffle convention, graded skew-symmetry, graded Jacobi, and the exterior-derivative rule stated in its definition. [[def-qftx-lie-algebra-valued-form-bracket]]

[F4] Each $\operatorname{Ad}_g=d(C_g)_e$ is a Lie-algebra automorphism, and $\operatorname{Ad}_{gh}=\operatorname{Ad}_g\operatorname{Ad}_h$. [[def-conjugation-and-the-adjoint-representation-of-a-lie-group]], [[prop-adjoint-is-a-smooth-lie-group-representation]], [[thm-differential-of-a-lie-group-homomorphism-is-a-lie-algebra-homomorphism]]

[F5] The derivative of the adjoint representation at the identity is $d(\operatorname{Ad})_e(Z)=\operatorname{ad}_Z$. [[thm-the-differential-of-adjoint-is-ad]]

[F6] The left Maurer–Cartan form has fibre map $(\theta_L)_g=d(L_{g^{-1}})_g$; thus $\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)=d(L_{g_{ij}^{-1}})_{g_{ij}}(dg_{ij}(X))$. [[def-left-maurer-cartan-form]]

[F7] The componentwise exterior derivative is natural under pullback and satisfies $d^2=0$. [[def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative]], [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-commutes-with-pullback]], [[thm-the-exterior-derivative-squares-to-zero]]

[F8] A smooth principal bundle has local equivariant diffeomorphisms $\pi^{-1}(U_i)\cong U_i\times G$, hence smooth local sections and unique smooth transition maps on overlaps. [[def-smooth-principal-g-bundle]]

[F9] For a 1-form $\lambda$, $(\mathcal L_X\lambda)(Y)=X(\lambda(Y))-\lambda([X,Y])$, from the flow definition of tensor Lie derivative and $\mathcal L_XY=[X,Y]$. [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-tensor-field]], [[def-lie-derivative-of-a-differential-form]], [[thm-lie-derivative-of-a-vector-field-equals-the-lie-bracket]], [[def-lie-bracket-of-smooth-vector-fields]]

## Proof

1.1 For a fundamental field $Z_P$, $\omega(Z_P)=Z$ is constant. The exterior-derivative formula therefore gives $d\omega(Z_P,Y)=Z_P(\omega(Y))-\omega([Z_P,Y])=(\mathcal L_{Z_P}\omega)(Y)$ by [F9]. Differentiating $R_{\exp(tZ)}^*\omega=\operatorname{Ad}_{\exp(-tZ)}\omega$ at $t=0$, [F5] yields $\mathcal L_{Z_P}\omega=-[Z,\omega]$. The shuffle convention gives $\tfrac12[\omega\wedge\omega](Z_P,Y)=[Z,\omega(Y)]$, so $\Omega_\omega(Z_P,Y)=0$. Every vertical vector is fundamental by the orbit-differential isomorphism, hence $\Omega_\omega$ is horizontal. [A1, F1, F3, F5, F7, F9]

1.2 Pullback commutes with $d$, and the fixed linear map $\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}$ preserves the Lie bracket by [F4]. Consequently, for fixed $g\in G$, $R_g^*\Omega_\omega=d(\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega)+\tfrac12[\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega\wedge\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\omega]=\operatorname{Ad}_{g^{-1}}\Omega_\omega$. This is the claimed equivariance. [F1, F3, F4, F7]

1.3 On an overlap, differentiate $s_j(x)=s_i(x)g_{ij}(x)$ along $X\in T_xM$. The derivative is the sum of $d(R_{g_{ij}(x)})ds_i(X)$ and the fundamental vertical vector at $s_j(x)$ generated by $\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$, since $g_{ij}(x)^{-1}g_{ij}(x(t))$ has initial tangent $\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$ at the identity. Applying $\omega$, its right equivariance and reproduction property give $A_j(X)=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}A_i(X)+\theta_L(g_{ij})(X)$. [F1, F6, F8]

1.4 By [F7] and the graded-derivation rule in [F3], $d\Omega_\omega=\tfrac12 d[\omega\wedge\omega]=[d\omega\wedge\omega]$, because graded skew-symmetry gives $[\omega\wedge d\omega]=-[d\omega\wedge\omega]$. Hence $D_\omega\Omega_\omega=d\Omega_\omega+[\omega\wedge\Omega_\omega]=\tfrac12[\omega\wedge[\omega\wedge\omega]]=0$, the last equality being graded Jacobi from [F3] and the Jacobi identity in [F1]. Pulling back by each $s_i$ gives $D_{A_i}F_i=dF_i+[A_i\wedge F_i]=0$. [F1, F3, F7]

2.1 For horizontal vector fields $X,Y$, $\omega(X)=\omega(Y)=0$, so $\Omega_\omega(X,Y)=d\omega(X,Y)=-\omega([X,Y])$. Meanwhile $\omega(K_H(X,Y))=-\omega(v([X,Y]))=-\omega([X,Y])$, since $\omega$ vanishes on the horizontal component. Both $\Omega_\omega$ and $K_H$ depend only on the horizontal components of their inputs, so the equality extends to all $X,Y$: $\Omega_\omega=\omega\circ K_H$. This fixes the sign relative to the imported vertical-valued curvature. [F1, F2, step 1.1]

2.2 By [F7], pullback along $s_i$ commutes with the componentwise exterior derivative; the bracket is pointwise and pullback preserves its alternating arguments. Thus $F_i=s_i^*\Omega_\omega=dA_i+\tfrac12[A_i\wedge A_i]$. On the overlap, the same differentiated-section decomposition as in step 1.3 has a vertical term, which vanishes when inserted into $\Omega_\omega$ by step 1.1. Its remaining term is transformed by the equivariance in step 1.2, yielding $F_j=\operatorname{Ad}_{g_{ij}^{-1}}F_i$. [F1, F3, F7, F8, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3]

3.1 The statements include the zero-dimensional or abelian Lie-algebra cases: then the corresponding brackets vanish, and each displayed identity reduces to the ordinary exterior-derivative identity. A zero-dimensional base has no nonzero 2-forms, and the formulas remain valid. Only the declared $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is inherited from [F1] and [F2]; no global section or full choice principle is assumed. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F7, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 1.3, step 2.2, step 1.4] ∎



