research/plan-spec.json-17461-          "statement": "Take $g=l=1$ in [[prop-cm-pendulum-small-angle-linearization-bound]], so that $\\omega=1$,\n$F(\\theta,v)=(v,-\\sin\\theta)$, $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $L=1$ and\n$\\lVert A\\rVert=1$. Choose the ball radius $r=1/2$, the datum\n$z_0=(\\delta,0)$ with $\\delta=1/100$, and the horizon $T=1$. Then\n$\\delta e^{LT}=e/100<3/100<1/2=r$ because $2<e<3$\n([[cor-two-less-than-e-less-than-three]]), so the proposition applies and gives\n\n$$\\lVert z(t)-y(t)\\rVert_2\\le\\frac{1}{12\\cdot10^4}\\bigl(e^{2t}-e^{t}\\bigr)\\qquad(0\\le t\\le1),$$\n\nwhere $y(t)=\\delta(\\cos t,-\\sin t)$ solves $y'=Ay$, $y(0)=z_0$; at $t=1$ the bound reads\n$(e^2-e)/(12\\cdot10^4)$ and is at most $e^3/(12\\cdot10^4)$. The remainder checks at\n$\\lVert z\\rVert_2\\le r$ are: $\\lvert\\sin\\theta-\\theta\\rvert\\le\\lvert\\theta\\rvert^3/6\\le1/48$, and\n$\\lvert\\sin\\theta\\rvert\\le\\lvert\\theta\\rvert\\le r=1/2$. All constants are exact; the single\ninequality $e<3$ is the only numerical input.",
research/plan-spec.json-17462-          "strategy": "Instantiate the proposition at the stated constants, verify the smallness condition with the published bound on e, evaluate the integral by the fundamental theorem, and check the pointwise remainder bounds.",
--
research/plan-spec.json-17521-          "reference": "mechanical-horseshoe.md#1 (the sign alphabet X={-1,+1}^Z, the shift, and the ball B_R of bounded bilateral sequences)",
research/plan-spec.json-17522-          "design_state": "completed-local-definition",
research/plan-spec.json:17523:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-17524-          "statement": "Write $\\Sigma_{\\pm}:=\\{-1,+1\\}$ and\n\n$$X_{\\pm}\\ :=\\ \\Sigma_{\\pm}^{\\mathbb Z}\\ =\\ \\{\\,s\\ :\\ s \\text{ is a function } \\mathbb Z\\to\\Sigma_{\\pm}\\,\\},$$\n\nwith the function-set convention of [[def-function-space]]; for $s\\in X_{\\pm}$ write $s_k:=s(k)$. The elements of $X_{\\pm}$ are called **two-sign bilateral sequences**.\n\n**Coding correspondence with the published two-symbol space.** Let\n\n$$\\varphi:X_{\\pm}\\longrightarrow\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb Z},\\qquad \\varphi(s)_k:=\\frac{s_k+1}{2}\\in\\{0,1\\},$$\n\nand let $X_2:=\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb Z}$, $d_2$ and $\\sigma_2$ be the set, the metric expression and the shift map of [[def-cm-bilateral-shift-space-and-cylinders]]. The formulas $\\varphi(s)_k=(s_k+1)/2$ and $\\varphi^{-1}(t)_k=2t_k-1$ define maps $X_{\\pm}\\to X_2$ and $X_2\\to X_{\\pm}$ that are inverse to each other, coordinate by coordinate.\n\n**Metric expression on $X_{\\pm}$.** Define\n\n$$D_{\\pm}(s,t):=d_2\\bigl(\\varphi(s),\\varphi(t)\\bigr)\\qquad(s,t\\in X_{\\pm}).$$\n\nUnwinding the series of [[def-cm-bilateral-shift-space-and-cylinders]] and using $|\\varphi(s)_k-\\varphi(t)_k|=\\tfrac12|s_k-t_k|$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ gives the explicit expression\n\n$$D_{\\pm}(s,t)=\\sum_{k\\ge 0}2^{-(k+1)}\\,|s_k-t_k|\\;+\\;\\sum_{k\\ge 1}2^{-(k+1)}\\,|s_{-k}-t_{-k}| .$$\n\nBoth series converge by comparison with the geometric series $\\sum_{k\\ge0}2^{-k}=2$ ([[def-series]], [[thm-geometric-series]], [[def-integer-power]], [[def-abs-value]]), because $|s_k-t_k|\\in\\{0,2\\}$ for every $k$; hence $D_{\\pm}(s,t)$ is a well-defined nonnegative real number. Whether $D_{\\pm}$ is a metric is **not** asserted here; it is proved in the companion lemma on this page, together with the statement that $\\varphi$ is an isometry of $(X_{\\pm},D_{\\pm})$ onto $(X_2,d_2)$.\n\n**The shift.** The **(bilateral) shift** of $X_{\\pm}$ is\n\n$$S_{\\pm}:X_{\\pm}\\longrightarrow X_{\\pm},\\qquad S_{\\pm}(s)_k:=s_{k+1}\\qquad(k\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ s\\in X_{\\pm}).$$\n\nFor $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ its iterate satisfies $S_{\\pm}^{m}(s)_k=s_{k+m}$; in particular $S_{\\pm}$ is a bijection with inverse $S_{\\pm}^{-1}(s)_k=s_{k-1}$, and $S_{\\pm}=\\varphi^{-1}\\circ\\sigma_2\\circ\\varphi$.\n\n**Bounded bilateral sequences.** A function $x:\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R$ is **bounded** when its range $\\{x_k:k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}$ is a bounded subset of $\\mathbb R$ ([[def-bounded-set]]). Write\n\n$$\\ell^{\\infty}(\\mathbb Z):=\\{\\,x:\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb R\\ :\\ x \\text{ is bounded}\\,\\},\\qquad \\|x\\|_{\\infty}:=\\sup\\{\\,|x_k|\\ :\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z\\,\\}.$$\n\nThe set $\\{|x_k|:k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}$ whose supremum is taken is nonempty and bounded above ([[def-bounded-set]]), so $\\|x\\|_\\infty$ is a well-defined nonnegative real number ([[lem-sup-unique]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]). For $R>0$ put\n\n$$B_R:=\\{\\,x\\in\\ell^{\\infty}(\\mathbb Z)\\ :\\ \\|x\\|_{\\infty}\\le R\\,\\}.$$\n\nThus $B_R$ is the closed ball of radius $R$ in the supremum norm, and $\\|x\\|_\\infty\\le R$ is by definition the statement that $|x_k|\\le R$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ ([[def-bounded-set]], [[def-abs-value]]).",
research/plan-spec.json-17525-          "strategy": "Define the listed objects over the published two-symbol shift space and its metric; state exactly which properties are deferred to the companion items.",
--
research/plan-spec.json-17583-          "reference": "mechanical-horseshoe.md#1 (completeness of the bounded-sequence space and of B_R)",
research/plan-spec.json-17584-          "design_state": "completed-local-argument",
research/plan-spec.json:17585:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-17586-          "statement": "Let $S$ be a nonempty set, let $\\mathcal B(S)$ be the set of bounded functions $S\\to\\mathbb R$ and let $d_\\infty$ be the supremum metric on $\\mathcal B(S)$ ([[lem-sup-metric-is-a-metric]]); write $0$ for the zero function. For $R\\ge0$ put\n\n$$B_R(S):=\\{\\,f\\in\\mathcal B(S)\\ :\\ |f(s)|\\le R\\ \\text{for every }s\\in S\\,\\}.$$\n\nLet $X_{\\pm}$, $\\varphi$, $X_2$, $d_2$, $D_\\pm$ be as in [[def-bilateral-symbol-and-bounded-sequence-space]]. Then:\n\n1. $(\\mathcal B(S),d_\\infty)$ is a complete metric space ([[def-complete-metric-space]]);\n2. for every $R\\ge0$, $(B_R(S),d_\\infty)$ is a complete metric space;\n3. $D_\\pm(s,t)=d_2(\\varphi(s),\\varphi(t))$ is a metric on $X_\\pm$ ([[def-metric-space]]), and $\\varphi:(X_\\pm,D_\\pm)\\to(X_2,d_2)$ is a bijective isometry ([[def-metric-convergence]] for the underlying notion of distance);\n4. consequently $\\ell^{\\infty}(\\mathbb Z)$ with the distance $\\|x-y\\|_\\infty$ is a complete metric space, $B_R$ is complete for every $R\\ge0$, and the same holds for the one-sided bounded sequences, that is for $S=\\mathbb N$.",
research/plan-spec.json-17587-          "strategy": "Coordinatewise Cauchy limits, the completeness of R, a uniform two-index estimate for sup convergence, and the stability of the bound |f(s)|<=R under pointwise limits.",
--
