research/plan-spec.json-17775-          "statement": "Fix $a\\ge100$, let $R=\\sqrt a+3$, $L=\\sqrt{a-2R}$, let $X_\\pm$ and $S_\\pm$ be the two-sign bilateral shift space and its shift ([[def-bilateral-symbol-and-bounded-sequence-space]]), let $x(s)\\in B_R$ be the coded sequence of [[lem-coded-quadratic-recurrence-is-a-contraction]], let\n\n$$F(x,y):=(y,\\;y^2-a-x),\\qquad h(s):=\\bigl(x_{-1}(s),\\,x_0(s)\\bigr)\\qquad(s\\in X_\\pm),$$\n\nand put $\\Lambda:=h(X_\\pm)\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$. Then:\n\n1. $F\\circ h=h\\circ S_\\pm$, i.e. $F(h(s))=h(S_\\pm s)$ for every $s\\in X_\\pm$, and $\\Lambda$ is invariant: $F(\\Lambda)=\\Lambda$;\n2. $h$ is a continuous bijection of $(X_\\pm,D_\\pm)$ onto $\\Lambda$; it is a homeomorphism onto $\\Lambda$ with the Euclidean metric ([[def-metric-continuity]]) once the Axiom of Countable Choice is granted, since the proof uses $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ exactly once, through the equivalence between closed and sequentially closed subsets of $\\mathbb R^2$ ([[thm-metric-sequential-closure]]);\n3. $\\Lambda$ is sequentially compact ([[def-metric-compactness-variants]]) and, granting the Axioms of Countable and Dependent Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-dependent-choice]]), compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]) and a continuous image of the compact space $X_\\pm$ ([[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]]);\n4. **(chaos)** Granting the Axiom of Countable Choice as in clause 2, $F|_\\Lambda:\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda$ is continuous, topologically mixing, has dense periodic points, and has sensitive dependence on initial conditions with sensitivity constant $2L$; hence $F|_\\Lambda$ is chaotic in the stated sense ([[def-cm-topologically-mixing-dense-periodic-and-sensitive-maps]]).",
research/plan-spec.json-17776-          "strategy": "Translate the recurrence into the phase map, use closedness of the coding map under sequential compactness for the inverse, transfer mixing and periodic density through the conjugacy, and exhibit sensitivity by flipping a far symbol so that the sign separation 2L appears in the observed coordinate.",
--
