research/plan-spec.json-19865-          "statement": "**Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice** $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), which is used by the imported Fourier-basis theorem quoted below.\n\n**The three-torus and its measure.** Let $\\mathbb T:=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be the one-dimensional torus with its quotient topology and its normalized Haar integral $m_{\\mathbb T}$ ([[def-the-one-dimensional-torus-and-normalized-haar-integral]]); $\\mathbb T$ is compact Hausdorff ([[lem-finite-tori-are-compact-hausdorff-character-spaces]]). Put $\\mathbb T^3:=\\mathbb T\\times\\mathbb T\\times\\mathbb T$ with the finite product topology. Let $dx:=m_{\\mathbb T}\\otimes m_{\\mathbb T}\\otimes m_{\\mathbb T}$ be the product measure on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb T^3$ ([[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]], [[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]); being a product of probability measures it is a probability measure. For a vector $k=(k_1,k_2,k_3)\\in\\mathbb Z^3$ and $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)\\in\\mathbb T^3$ define the **character**\n$$e_k(x):=\\exp\\bigl(2\\pi i\\,(k_1x_1+k_2x_2+k_3x_3)\\bigr)=e^{2\\pi i\\,k\\cdot x},$$\nwhere each $x_j$ is inserted through any real representative and $\\exp$ is the complex exponential ([[def-fourier-coefficients-and-trigonometric-polynomials]]); the definition does not depend on the representatives. Then $|e_k(x)|=1$, $e_k e_l=e_{k+l}$, and $e_{-k}=\\overline{e_k}$.\n\n**Function spaces.** Write $L^2:=L^2(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb R^3)$ for the real Hilbert space of (equivalence classes of) real Borel vector fields $u:\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ with $\\int_{\\mathbb T^3}|u|_2^2\\,dx<+\\infty$, inner product $\\langle u,v\\rangle:=\\int_{\\mathbb T^3}u\\cdot v\\,dx$ and norm $|u|_2:=\\langle u,u\\rangle^{1/2}$; here $u\\cdot v$ is the Euclidean inner product of $\\mathbb R^3$ ([[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]). Similarly $L^1(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb C^3)$ denotes the integrable complex vector fields.\n\n**Fourier coefficients.** For $u\\in L^1(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb C^3)$ and $k\\in\\mathbb Z^3$ define\n$$\\widehat u(k):=\\int_{\\mathbb T^3}u(x)\\,e_{-k}(x)\\,dx\\ \\in\\mathbb C^3 .$$\nThe integral depends only on the class of $u$. A field $u\\in L^1$ is real almost everywhere if and only if $\\widehat u(-k)=\\overline{\\widehat u(k)}$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb Z^3$; the forward direction uses $\\widehat{\\overline u}(k)=\\overline{\\widehat u(-k)}$, and the reverse uses $L^1$ Fourier uniqueness, proved below. On the circle this uniqueness follows from [[thm-fejer-convergence-in-lp]] with $p=1$: zero coefficients give zero Fejer means, whose $L^1$ limit is the function. To pass to $\\mathbb T^d$ for $d=2,3$, use [[thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces]]: for fixed $k'\\in\\mathbb Z^{d-1}$, $g_{k'}(x_1)=\\int_{\\mathbb T^{d-1}}u(x_1,x')e_{-k'}(x')dx'$ is in $L^1(\\mathbb T)$ and all its coefficients vanish. Hence $g_{k'}=0$ almost everywhere. The countable union of the exceptional sets over $k'$ is null. For every remaining $x_1$, the $L^1$ section in $x'$ has all coefficients zero, so induction in $d$ makes it zero almost everywhere. Fubini then makes $u=0$ almost everywhere on $\\mathbb T^d$. Apply this uniqueness to $u-\\overline u$ to obtain the reverse real-valuedness implication. For $u\\in L^2\\subset L^1$ (finite measure) the coefficient map is the one of the imported theorem, which states: $(e_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^3}$ is an orthonormal basis of the complex Hilbert space $L^2(\\mathbb T^3;\\mathbb C)$, the finite-subset-net expansion $u=\\sum_k\\widehat u(k)e_k$ and the Par
... 3917752 bytes omitted ...
atrix identity cannot be transferred by name alone. The general comparison identity can be written before imposing a second symmetry, but the black-hole uniqueness application also needs the axial reduction and positivity conditions. Reduction using a stationary Killing field can change the target signature where that field becomes null or spacelike; the positive Hermitian representation therefore needs proof on the actual domain. A spacetime argument must separately establish symmetry inheritance (for example, invariance of the Maxwell two-form under both specified generators), commutation, circularity, the global smooth quotient and its boundary structure, regularity on the axis and any selected null horizon, and the falloff conditions on any declared asymptotic end. It must then control the boundary terms in the comparison argument under those exact hypotheses. The 1996 textbook presents a uniqueness argument for a restricted Kerr–Newman class with its own axis, horizon and infinity analysis; that argument is not imported here. The 2012 review distinguishes the local identity from the global boundary step and warns in Section 8.4.1 that complete derivative control at the singular axis and horizon had not been supplied in the literature it reviewed; Section 8.4.2 discusses a distance-function alternative. These are dated source assessments, not current-status claims.\n\nThis page asserts no stationary electrovacuum uniqueness theorem, no dynamical stability result, and no cosmic-censorship conclusion. It makes no blanket current-status claim about broader uniqueness or stability questions.",
