research/plan-spec.json-227203-          },
research/plan-spec.json-227204-          "physical_scope": "A purely geometric definition of affine completeness within a specified Lorentzian manifold; it asserts neither curvature blow-up nor the existence of a singular point.",
research/plan-spec.json:227205:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-227206-          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold with metric-selected connection $\\nabla$. A **future-directed affinely parametrized null geodesic** is a smooth nonconstant curve $\\gamma:J\\to M$, where $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an interval with nonempty interior, such that its tangent $\\dot\\gamma$ is future-directed and null at every interior parameter and $D_\\lambda\\dot\\gamma=0$. Here “null” means $g(\\dot\\gamma,\\dot\\gamma)=0$ and $\\dot\\gamma\\ne0$, and future-directed means $g(\\dot\\gamma,N)<0$. The geodesic equation and covariant derivative are those of the metric-selected connection.\n\nThe **maximal future affine interval** of such a geodesic is the future endpoint interval of its maximal extension as an affinely parametrized geodesic in $M$. It is **future complete** when that interval has no finite upper endpoint, equivalently when its affine parameter tends to $+\\infty$. The spacetime is **future null geodesically complete** when every future-directed null geodesic is future complete, and **future null geodesically incomplete** when at least one is not.\n\nAn affine change of parameter $\\widetilde\\lambda=a\\lambda+b$ with $a>0$ preserves whether the maximal future interval is unbounded above, so these properties do not depend on the chosen positive affine normalization.",
research/plan-spec.json-227207-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-227244-          },
research/plan-spec.json-227245-          "physical_scope": "A differential-topological statement about two smooth Cauchy hypersurfaces and a supplied complete timelike product flow; no field equation or empirical claim is involved.",
research/plan-spec.json:227246:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-227247-          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold. Suppose a smooth three-manifold $S_0$ and a smooth diffeomorphism $\\Psi:\\mathbb R\\times S_0\\to M$ are supplied such that each curve $s\\mapsto\\Psi(s,z)$ is an inextendible future timelike curve and $\\Psi(0,z)=z$. Let $\\Sigma\\subset M$ be a smooth spacelike Cauchy hypersurface, meaning that it is met exactly once by every inextendible causal curve. Then the second-coordinate projection\n$$P:=\\operatorname{pr}_{S_0}\\circ\\Psi^{-1}\\big|_\\Sigma:\\Sigma\\to S_0$$\nis a diffeomorphism. In particular, $\\Sigma$ and $S_0$ have the same connectedness and compactness properties.",
research/plan-spec.json-227248-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-227309-          },
research/plan-spec.json-227310-          "physical_scope": "Pure Lorentzian-geometric definitions. No field equation, cosmic-censorship assumption, event-horizon existence, or empirical claim is asserted.",
research/plan-spec.json:227311:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-227312-          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$. Let $S\\subset M$ be a nonempty smooth embedded two-manifold, compact without boundary, whose induced metric $q$ is positive definite. At each point its normal plane $(T_pS)^\\perp$ is a Lorentzian two-plane with two future null rays. On any open patch carrying a smooth ordered pair of future null normals $\\ell,k$ with $g(\\ell,k)=-2$, define\n$$\\chi_\\ell(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_X\\ell,Y),\\qquad \\theta_\\ell=\\operatorname{tr}_q\\chi_\\ell,\\qquad \\chi_k(X,Y)=g(\\nabla_Xk,Y),\\qquad \\theta_k=\\operatorname{tr}_q\\chi_k$$\nfor tangent vectors $X,Y$ to $S$. These are the null second fundamental forms and their expansions. A **future trapped surface** is such an $S$ for which, on every local patch and for both future null normal rays, the corresponding expansion is strictly negative at every point. Positive rescaling of a null normal multiplies its expansion by the same positive factor, so the sign condition is independent of its affine normalization and of a local interchange of the two rays.\n\nWhen a specified conformal completion is supplied, write it as $(\\overline M,\\overline g,\\overline N,\\Omega,j,\\mathscr I^+)$. Here $\\overline M$ is a smooth four-dimensional manifold with boundary in the senses of [[def-topological-manifold-with-boundary]] and [[def-smooth-chart-atlas-and-structure-on-a-manifold-with-boundary]]. At each point $\\overline g$ is a symmetric bilinear form on the tangent space, its components extend smoothly in every boundary chart, and its pointwise inertia is $(3,1,0)$ in the convention of [[def-lorentz-form-and-inertia]]. The vector field $\\overline N$ likewise has components that extend smoothly in every boundary chart; it satisfies $\\overline g(\\overline N,\\overline N)<0$ everywhere and $\\overline g(\\overline N,j_*N)<0$ on the interior. The map $j:M\\to\\overline M^\\circ$ is a diffeomorphism onto the interior. The function $\\Omega:\\overline M\\to\\mathbb R$ is smooth in the boundary-chart sense, positive on $j(M)$, and zero with nonzero differential on the designated smooth three-dimensional boundary subset $\\mathscr I^+\\subseteq\\partial\\overline M$. On $j(M)$ the metric satisfies $\\overline g|_{j(M)}=\\Omega^2(j^{-1})^*g$, where pullback is as in [[def-pullback-of-a-covariant-tensor-field]]. The restriction of $\\overline g$ to $T\\mathscr I^+$ has a one-dimensional kernel at every point; this null line is the generator direction, and its future ray is the one whose vectors $v$ satisfy $\\overline g(v,\\overline N)<0$. Thus the designated boundary is null and future-oriented; no completeness of its generators is assumed.\n\nA curve $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to\\overline M$ is **locally absolutely continuous** when it is continuous and, around each parameter value, its coordinate functions in a boundary or interior chart are absolutely continuous on every compact subinterval of that chart neighborhood, in the sense of [[def-absolutely-continuous-function]]. Boundary charts take values in relatively open subsets of the closed half-space. To compare two charts around a parameter value, shrink its parameter neighborhood so the coordinate image lies in a compact convex subset of a local-extension domain of the transition map. Its derivative is bounded there, so the mean-value estimate ([[cor-mean-value-theorem]]) makes the transition Lipschitz. Applying that bound to the finite disjoint-interval condition for absolute continuity shows that the transformed coordinate functions remain absolutely continuous; the argument in both directions proves chart independence. At an interior parameter value where the coordinate derivatives exist, $\\dot\\gamma(t)$ is the tangent vector with those components; the chain rule for the chart transitions ([[prop-chain-rule-for-smooth-half-space-maps]]) makes it independent of chart. A **future causal curve in $\\overline M$** is a locally absolutely continuous curve for which, for almost every $t\\in(0,1)$, this derivative exists and satisfies $\\overline g(\\dot\\gamma,\\dot\\gamma)\\le0$ and $\\overline g(\\dot\\gamma,\\overline N)<0$. These inequalities make the derivative nonzero and select the future direction. On the interior, $\\overline g(j_*v,j_*v)=\\Omega^2g(v,v)$ for every tangent vector $v$, so causal character is unchanged, and the specified orientation condition makes the future cone agree with that of $N$ ([[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]). Define $\\overline J^-_{\\overline g,\\overline N}(\\mathscr I^+):=\\{x\\in\\overline M:\\text{there are }y\\in\\mathscr I^+\\text{ and a future causal curve in }\\overline M\\text{ from }x\\text{ to }y\\}$. The **black region relative to this completion** and its **event horizon** are, respectively,\n$$\\mathcal B_{\\mathscr I^+}:=\\{p\\in M:j(p)\\notin\\overline J^-_{\\overline g,\\overline N}(\\mathscr I^+)\\},\\qquad \\mathcal H_{\\mathscr I^+}:=\\partial_M\\mathcal B_{\\mathscr I^+}.$$ \nIf no such completion is supplied, these two sets are undefined; their existence is not a consequence of the definition of a trapped surface.\n\nGiven in addition a designated smooth future null normal ray field $[\\ell_{\\mathrm{out}}]$ on $S$, called the **outward direction**, $S$ is **marginally outer trapped** relative to this choice when $\\theta_{\\ell_{\\mathrm{out}}}=0$ everywhere. If the outward label comes from a side of an embedded spacelike hypersurface or from an asymptotic end, that side or end is part of the supplied data; it is not inferred from $S$ alone. The zero condition is invariant under positive rescaling of the chosen ray.",
