research/plan-spec.json-227407-          "statement": "Assume the Axioms of Countable Choice and Dependent Choice. Let $(M,g,N)$ be a time-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold that is globally hyperbolic, meaning strongly causal with compact causal diamonds. Let $S\\subset M$ be a nonempty compact smooth embedded spacelike two-surface whose two future null expansions are negative everywhere. Assume the direct null Ricci condition $\\operatorname{Ric}(\\ell,\\ell)\\ge0$ for every null vector $\\ell$ and assume every future-directed affinely parametrized null geodesic in $M$ is future complete. Define\n$$I^+(S)=\\bigcup_{s\\in S}I^+(s),\\qquad J^+(S)=\\bigcup_{s\\in S}J^+(s),\\qquad E^+(S)=J^+(S)\\setminus I^+(S).$$\nThen $E^+(S)=\\partial I^+(S)$ is a closed topological three-manifold without boundary and is compact in the subspace topology of $M$.",
research/plan-spec.json-227408-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-227483-          },
research/plan-spec.json-227484-          "physical_scope": "A conditional Lorentzian-geometric theorem. The null Ricci inequality is assumed directly; no Einstein equation, matter-energy condition, event horizon, curvature blow-up, or singular point in the manifold is concluded.",
research/plan-spec.json:227485:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-227486-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ that is globally hyperbolic (strongly causal with compact causal diamonds). Let $\\Sigma\\subset M$ be a connected noncompact smooth spacelike Cauchy hypersurface, meaning that every inextendible causal curve meets $\\Sigma$ exactly once. Let $S\\subset M$ be a nonempty compact smooth spacelike two-surface on which both future null expansions are negative for the two local future null normal rays. Assume directly that $\\operatorname{Ric}_p(v,v)\\ge0$ for every point $p\\in M$ and every null vector $v\\in T_pM$. Then $(M,g,N)$ is future null geodesically incomplete: at least one maximal future-directed affinely parametrized null geodesic has an affine parameter interval with finite upper endpoint. This conclusion asserts no curvature divergence or singular point belonging to $M$.",
research/plan-spec.json-227487-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-227551-          },
research/plan-spec.json-227552-          "physical_scope": "An exact flat Lorentzian geometry calculation; it is not an empirical statement and does not claim that Minkowski space contains no other trapped surfaces.",
research/plan-spec.json:227553:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-227554-          "statement": "Use the Minkowski model with metric $g=-(dx^0)^2+(dx^1)^2+(dx^2)^2+(dx^3)^2$ and future direction $N=\\partial_{x^0}$. For $R>0$, let $S=\\{x^0=0,\\ (x^1)^2+(x^2)^2+(x^3)^2=R^2\\}$. The future null normals $\\ell=\\partial_{x^0}+\\partial_r$ and $k=\\partial_{x^0}-\\partial_r$ obey $g(\\ell,k)=-2$ and have expansions $\\theta_\\ell=2/R$ and $\\theta_k=-2/R$. Thus these round spheres fail the future-trapping hypothesis because one expansion is positive.",
research/plan-spec.json-227555-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-227635-          },
research/plan-spec.json-227636-          "physical_scope": "An exact Lorentzian counterexample to omitting the noncompact-Cauchy hypothesis. The model is mathematical; no observational or matter interpretation is asserted.",
research/plan-spec.json:227637:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-227638-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For $H>0$, let $M_H$ be the hyperboloid\n$$-X_0^2+X_1^2+X_2^2+X_3^2+X_4^2=H^{-2}$$\nin five-dimensional Minkowski space, with its induced Lorentzian metric and future orientation. The global coordinates\n$$X_0=H^{-1}\\sinh(H\\tau),\\qquad (X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4)=H^{-1}\\cosh(H\\tau)\\,\\omega,\\qquad \\omega\\in S^3$$\ngive $g=-d\\tau^2+a(\\tau)^2\\gamma_{S^3}$ with $a(\\tau)=H^{-1}\\cosh(H\\tau)$. Use geometric length coordinates: $\\tau$ and affine parameters are in metres, $H$ is in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. Every slice $\\Sigma_\\tau=\\{\\tau\\}\\times S^3$ is a compact connected Cauchy hypersurface, and the spacetime is globally hyperbolic. Every future null geodesic is complete and $\\operatorname{Ric}(v,v)=0$ for every null vector $v$. For any $\\tau_0<0$, the equatorial sphere $S_{\\tau_0}=\\{\\tau=\\tau_0,\\ \\chi=\\pi/2\\}\\cong S^2$ is future trapped, with both expansions equal to $2H\\tanh(H\\tau_0)<0$ (units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$). Thus this model satisfies the trapped-surface and null-Ricci hypotheses and is null complete; its compact Cauchy slices show why the noncompact-Cauchy hypothesis in the Penrose theorem cannot be omitted.",
research/plan-spec.json-227639-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-227713-          },
research/plan-spec.json-227714-          "physical_scope": "A mathematical Lorentzian metric datum on the subextremal exterior domain; it asserts no astrophysical realization or observational claim.",
research/plan-spec.json:227715:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-227716-          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the tangent-bundle smooth structure of the\nLorentzian conventions.\n\nLet $M>0$ and $a\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy $|a|<M$, and put\n$r_\\pm:=M\\pm\\sqrt{M^2-a^2}$.\n\nLet $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm K}:=\\mathbb R\\times(r_+,\\infty)\\times S^2$ carry the\nproduct smooth structure, where $S^2=\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3:x^2+y^2+z^2=1\\}$\nhas its usual smooth structure and round metric $\\gamma_{S^2}$. Let\n$\\pi:\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm K}\\to S^2$ be the third projection, let $\\xi$ be the\nsmooth rotation field on $S^2$ generated by $(x,y,z)\\mapsto(-y,x,0)$, and put\n$\\gamma:=\\pi^*\\gamma_{S^2}$ and\n$\\xi^\\flat:=\\pi^*(\\gamma_{S^2}(\\xi,\\cdot))$. Let $t,r$ be the first and\nsecond coordinate functions on $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm K}$, and let\n$\\zeta:=z\\circ\\pi$ be the third Euclidean coordinate on the sphere factor.\nDefine the global smooth functions\n\n$$\\Sigma:=r^2+a^2\\zeta^2,\\qquad \\Delta:=r^2-2Mr+a^2.$$\n\nOn the axis-avoiding polar coordinate domain, let $0<\\theta<\\pi$ and regard\n$\\phi$ as the circle-valued azimuth in $\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$. Each\nchoice of an open real interval of length less than $2\\pi$ lifting $\\phi$\ngives an ordinary local chart. In any such chart,\n$\\zeta=\\cos\\theta$, $\\Sigma=r^2+a^2\\cos^2\\theta$,\n$\\gamma=d\\theta^2+\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\phi^2$, and\n$\\xi^\\flat=\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\phi$.\n\nDefine the symmetric covariant $2$-tensor $g$ globally on $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm K}$\nby\n\n$$g=-\\left(1-\\frac{2Mr}{\\Sigma}\\right)dt\\otimes dt+\\frac{\\Sigma}{\\Delta}\\,dr\\otimes dr+\\Sigma\\gamma+\\frac{a^2(\\Sigma+2Mr)}{\\Sigma}\\,\\xi^\\flat\\otimes\\xi^\\flat-\\frac{2aMr}{\\Sigma}\\left(dt\\otimes\\xi^\\flat+\\xi^\\flat\\otimes dt\\right).$$\n\nOn any such local polar chart, its Boyer-Lindquist line element is\n\n$$ds^2=-\\left(1-\\frac{2Mr}{\\Sigma}\\right)dt^2-\\frac{4Mar\\sin^2\\theta}{\\Sigma}\\,dt\\,d\\phi+\\frac{\\Sigma}{\\Delta}\\,dr^2+\\Sigma\\,d\\theta^2+\\frac{(r^2+a^2)^2-a^2\\Delta\\sin^2\\theta}{\\Sigma}\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\phi^2.$$\n\nThe displayed cross term is $2g_{t\\phi}\\,dt\\,d\\phi$. This definition makes\nno nondegeneracy or signature claim; Lorentzian signature and a time orientation\nare established separately.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-227717-          "provenance": {
--
