research/plan-spec.json-228051-          },
research/plan-spec.json-228052-          "physical_scope": "Local solution of the geodesic Hamilton-Jacobi equation on the axis-avoiding Kerr exterior chart; no global integrability through turning points is asserted.",
research/plan-spec.json:228053:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-228054-          "statement": "On an axis-avoiding Kerr exterior local coordinate rectangle\n$I_t\\times I_r\\times I_\\theta\\times I_\\phi$, where each $I_t,I_r,I_\\theta,I_\\phi$\nis a nonempty open real interval, $I_r\\subset(r_+,\\infty)$,\n$I_\\theta\\subset(0,\\pi)$, and $I_\\phi$ has length less than $2\\pi$ and is a\nlocal real lift of the azimuthal coordinate, define the geodesic\nHamilton-Jacobi equation for a $C^1$ scalar $S$ by\n\n$$g^{ab}\\partial_aS\\,\\partial_bS=-\\mu^2.$$\n\nFor constants $E,L,\\mu,Q\\in\\mathbb R$, set\n\n$$\\mathcal R(r):=[E(r^2+a^2)-aL]^2-\\Delta[\\mu^2r^2+(L-aE)^2+Q],\\qquad \\Theta(\\theta):=Q-\\cos^2\\theta\\left[a^2(\\mu^2-E^2)+\\frac{L^2}{\\sin^2\\theta}\\right].$$\n\nIf $\\mathcal R\\ge0$ on $I_r$ and $\\Theta\\ge0$ on $I_\\theta$, then local\nseparated $C^1$ solutions are given by\n\n$$S=-Et+L\\phi+S_r(r)+S_\\theta(\\theta),\\qquad S_r'=\\pm\\frac{\\sqrt{\\mathcal R}}{\\Delta},\\qquad S_\\theta'=\\pm\\sqrt{\\Theta}.$$\n\nWhere both radicands are strictly positive the branches are smooth. No\nsmoothness at a turning point or global continuation of a branch is asserted.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-228055-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-228090-          },
research/plan-spec.json-228091-          "physical_scope": "Unit and parameter interpretation for the Kerr metric; it asserts no measured value, astrophysical source model, or observational result.",
research/plan-spec.json:228092:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-228093-          "statement": "In the SI coordinate convention $x^0=ct_{\\mathrm{SI}}$, the Kerr metric's\ncoordinate $t$ is the length coordinate $x^0$ (not the time in seconds), and\n$r$ is a length; $\\theta,\\phi$ are dimensionless. The metric parameters $M$\nand $a$ in the\nKerr exterior definition are geometric lengths: the associated physical mass\nis $m_{\\mathrm{phys}}=c^2M/G_N$, and the physical angular momentum is\n$J_{\\mathrm{phys}}=c^3Ma/G_N$. Thus $a=J_{\\mathrm{phys}}/(m_{\\mathrm{phys}}c)$\nis the specific angular-momentum parameter. The horizon parameter\n$\\Omega_H=a/(r_+^2+a^2)$ has units of inverse length; the physical angular\nfrequency is $c\\Omega_H$ in inverse seconds.\n\nFor the local Hamilton-Jacobi solutions on an axis-avoiding coordinate\nrectangle, adopt the following action normalization. Let\n$S_{\\mathrm{SI}}:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be a $C^1$ reduced action with units\n$\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm s=\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm m^2\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$, and define\n$S:=(G_N/c^3)S_{\\mathrm{SI}}$. Thus $[S]=\\mathrm{length}^2$.\nThe momentum covector is $p=dS$; on the separated branches\n$S=-Et+L\\phi+S_r(r)+S_\\theta(\\theta)$, define $E:=-p_t$ and $L:=p_\\phi$.\nThe real constant $\\mu$ is the normalization parameter in\n$g^{ab}p_ap_b=-\\mu^2$, and the angular separation constant is\n$$Q:=p_\\theta^2+\\cos^2\\theta\\left[a^2(\\mu^2-E^2)+\\frac{L^2}{\\sin^2\\theta}\\right].$$\nThese are the constants of the local separation theorem, with its domain\nand radicand restrictions. Since $t,r$ have length units and the angles are\ndimensionless, $[p_t]=[p_r]=\\mathrm{length}$ and\n$[p_\\theta]=[p_\\phi]=\\mathrm{length}^2$. The inverse-metric contraction has\nunits $\\mathrm{length}^2$, so this convention assigns\n$[\\mu]=[E]=\\mathrm{length}$, $[L]=\\mathrm{length}^2$, and\n$[Q]=\\mathrm{length}^4$. These assignments apply also when a constant\nvanishes. They fix an action normalization; they are not dimensions forced\nby the unnormalized Hamilton-Jacobi equation alone. If a positive rest mass\n$m_0$ is assigned in a massive-particle interpretation, its corresponding\npositive parameter is $\\mu=G_Nm_0/c^2$; that interpretation is additional\nmodel data, not an existence or motion claim here.\n\nThese conversions restore units in the stated framework. They do not assert\nnumerical parameter values, a particular astrophysical source, or an\nobservation.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-228094-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-228158-          },
research/plan-spec.json-228159-          "physical_scope": "Pure mathematical limit and separation example; no measurement or claim about gravitational collapse is made.",
research/plan-spec.json:228160:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-228161-          "statement": "At $a=0$, the Kerr exterior is exactly the Schwarzschild exterior with\nSchwarzschild radius $2M$, and $\\Omega_H=0$. Its Ricci tensor vanishes. The\nHamilton-Jacobi separation constant reduces to\n$Q=p_\\theta^2+L_z^2\\cot^2\\theta=L_{\\mathrm{sph}}^2-L_z^2$, where\n$p_\\theta:=S_\\theta'$, $L_z:=\\partial_\\phi S$, and\n$L_{\\mathrm{sph}}^2:=p_\\theta^2+L_z^2/\\sin^2\\theta$. The axial Killing field\n$\\Phi$ also gives the reducible Killing tensor\n$K^\\Phi=\\Phi^\\flat\\otimes\\Phi^\\flat$, whose quadratic integral is constant\nalong affine geodesics.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-228162-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-228216-          },
research/plan-spec.json-228217-          "physical_scope": "A local sign calculation for the canonical scalar stress tensor and stationary Kerr multiplier; it makes no stability or observational claim.",
research/plan-spec.json:228218:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-228219-          "statement": "At an exterior point $p$ where the stationary Killing field $T=\\partial_t$ is\nspacelike, let $U$ be an oriented coordinate neighborhood of $p$, restrict the\nreal scalar-field model to $U$, set its potential to $V=0$, fix a coupling\n$K>0$, and let $n$ be any observer at $p$. There is a smooth scalar field\n$\\varphi:U\\to\\mathbb R$ for which the associated Killing-multiplier density\n$\\rho_T(p;n)$ is negative. Thus the density $\\rho_T(p;n)$ is not nonnegative\non all scalar first-derivative data at $p$, so this multiplier cannot provide\na pointwise nonnegative density for all such data there. If a scalar field on\n$U$ satisfies its scalar equation, its current is conserved. This example does\nnot assert that an on-shell field exists with the prescribed derivative.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-228220-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-228731-            "def-gr-fx-fixed-gauge-data-topology": "mathematical-premise"
