research/plan-spec.json:228733:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-228734-          "statement": "Fix one evolution problem, its state space and its declared classical solution class. For the general-relativity application here, use one fixed periodic symmetric-hyperbolic system on $\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}$ with integer regularity $s\\ge5$, a fixed coordinate gauge and reconstruction, smooth coefficient maps on an open state domain $O$, symmetric principal matrices and a positive time matrix. The relevant fixed-gauge context is the one defined by `def-gr-fx-fixed-gauge-data-topology`: one coefficient family and one matter branch, compact state sets $K_0\\subset\\operatorname{int}K_1\\subset\\operatorname{int}K_2\\subset O$, a uniform positive lower and finite upper bound for $A^0$ on $K_2$, and separate bounds $R_z,R_t$ for the state and its time derivative. The state components are divided by their fixed unit scales before their Euclidean-valued Sobolev norms are formed; these are coordinate norms and do not quotient by diffeomorphisms. On each compact time slab, the resulting normalized state $u$ belongs to $L^\\infty H^s\\cap C^0H^{s-1}$ and has $\\partial_tu\\in L^\\infty H^{s-1}$.\n\nA forward solution is given on $[0,T_{\\max})$, where $T_{\\max}\\in(0,\\infty]$. It is **maximal** in this solution class if, when $T_{\\max}<\\infty$, no solution on $[0,T_{\\max}+\\rho)$ for any $\\rho>0$ agrees with it on $[0,T_{\\max})$. Let $E_{\\rm raw}$ be a specified nonnegative scalar functional of the solution, continuous on every compact subinterval of $[0,T_{\\max})$. If $E_{\\rm raw}$ has physical units, choose a fixed positive scale $E_*$ with the same units and set $E=E_{\\rm raw}/E_*$. If $E_{\\rm raw}$ is dimensionless, set $E=E_{\\rm raw}$. In either case $E$ is dimensionless. In the general-relativity convention the time coordinate is $x^0=ct_{\\rm phys}$ in metres; choose a fixed length scale $L_*>0$ and use the dimensionless time $t=x^0/L_*$. Thus $\\partial_t=L_*\\partial_{x^0}$. The fixed-gauge bound $R_t$ is on $\\partial_{x^0}u$ and has units $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; the corresponding bound with respect to $t$ is $L_*R_t$. The constants $A$, $\\epsilon$ and $\\eta$ below are dimensionless as well. In a purely mathematical application, take these variables as real scalars without a physical interpretation.\n\nSpecify a finite list of dimensionless margin functions $m_j:[0,T_{\\max})\\to\\mathbb R$ and thresholds $\\mu_j>0$. Write $\\mathcal M(T)$ when $m_j(t)\\ge\\mu_j$ for every listed $j$ and every $0\\le t<T$. The **continuation criterion** is a separate result for the chosen evolution problem: if a solution has a finite right endpoint $T$ with $E(t)\\le\\eta$ for every $0\\le t<T$ and $\\mathcal M(T)$, then it extends past $T$ in the same solution class.\n\nFor constants $A>0$, $\\epsilon>0$ and $\\eta>0$ with $2A\\epsilon^2<\\eta$, a **bootstrap estimate** is the implication, required on every finite $T<T_{\\max}$,\n\n$$\\left[ E(t)\\le2A\\epsilon^2\\text{ for all }0\\le t\\le T\\right]\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\left[ E(t)\\le A\\epsilon^2\\text{ for all }0\\le t\\le T\\right].$$\n\nThis contract records the continuity and continuation inputs needed by an application. It does not itself prove the bootstrap estimate, the margin bounds, or any Einstein evolution estimate.\n\n",
