research/plan-spec.json-230753-          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $(M,g,N,o)$ be an oriented and\ntime-oriented smooth Lorentzian four-manifold, and let\n$P^+_g(M)$ be its smooth oriented time-oriented orthonormal frame bundle from\nthe oriented-frame-bundle proposition. Set\n$\\mathrm{Spin}^+(1,3):=SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ and let\n$\\rho:SL(2,\\mathbb C)\\to SO^+(1,3)$ be the Hermitian-matrix covering\nhomomorphism of the Hermitian-matrix double-cover proposition. A **Lorentzian spin\nstructure** is a smooth right principal $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$-bundle\n$\\widetilde P\\to M$ together with a smooth map $\\lambda:\\widetilde P\\to\nP^+_g(M)$ over the identity of $M$ such that\n\n$$\\lambda(ua)=\\lambda(u)\\rho(a)\\qquad (u\\in\\widetilde P,\\ a\\in SL(2,\\mathbb C)),$$\n\nand the restriction of $\\lambda$ to each fiber is a surjective two-sheeted\ncovering map. The map is over the identity, meaning $\\pi^+\\circ\\lambda=\\widetilde\\pi$; the square below displays this base condition and is not used as a proof.\n\n```tikzcd\n\\begin{tikzcd}\n\\widetilde P \\arrow[r,\"\\lambda\"] \\arrow[d,\"\\widetilde\\pi\"'] & P^+_g(M) \\arrow[d,\"\\pi^+\"] \\\\\nM \\arrow[r,\"\\mathrm{id}_M\"'] & M\n\\end{tikzcd}\n```\n\nA smooth finite-dimensional complex representation here consists of a\nfinite-dimensional complex vector space $V_\\sigma$ and a smooth group\nhomomorphism $\\sigma:SL(2,\\mathbb C)\\to GL(V_\\sigma)$, where\n$GL(V_\\sigma)$ is the group of invertible complex-linear maps on\n$V_\\sigma$, with the smooth structure inherited as an open subset of the\nreal vector space of complex-linear endomorphisms of $V_\\sigma$. Its\n**associated vector bundle** is the quotient\n$$\\widetilde P\\times_\\sigma V_\\sigma:=(\\widetilde P\\times V_\\sigma)/\\sim,$$\nwhere $\\sim$ is the equivalence relation generated by\n$(ua,v)\\sim(u,\\sigma(a)v)$ for $u\\in\\widetilde P$,\n$a\\in SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ and $v\\in V_\\sigma$; equivalently\n$(u,v)\\sim(ua,\\sigma(a)^{-1}v)$, using\nthe right-principal associated-bundle convention. Write $[u,v]$ for\nthe class of $(u,v)$. Since every $\\sigma(a)$ is complex-linear, the\nfiberwise vector-space operations\n$[u,v]+[u,w]=[u,v+w]$ and $z[u,v]=[u,zv]$ for $z\\in\\mathbb C$ are\nindependent of the representative. The associated-bundle local-triviality proposition\ngives local triviality; on an overlap with local sections satisfying\n$s_j(x)=s_i(x)a_{ij}(x)$, the smooth principal charts give transition maps\n$(x,v)\\mapsto(x,\\sigma(a_{ij}(x))v)$. These are smooth because $\\sigma$ is\nsmooth and define the smooth vector-bundle structure. The **defining spinor representation** here is $V_\\sigma=\\mathbb C^2$ with the action $\\sigma(a)v=av$ for $a\\in SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ and column vectors $v\\in\\mathbb C^2$. It is a smooth group homomorphism because matrix multiplication is smooth and $(ab)v=a(bv)$. Its associated bundle is called the **rank-two complex spinor bundle** in this convention; a **spinor field** is a smooth section of that bundle.\n\nTo define a **lift of a Lorentz connection**, first supply a principal\nconnection $H^+\\subset TP^+_g(M)$ on the frame bundle: it is a smooth vector\nsubbundle complementary to $VP^+:=\\ker(d\\pi^+)$ and invariant under the\nright $SO^+(1,3)$ action, as in\nthe principal-connection definition. This $H^+$ is additional\nglobal data; the local affine connection and local matrix $\\omega$ in\nthe local tetrad-connection definition do not determine it unless they are\nspecified as restrictions of a chosen global metric-compatible affine\nconnection whose induced principal connection is the supplied $H^+$. A lift\nis a supplied principal connection $\\widetilde H\\subset T\\widetilde P$,\nlikewise a smooth\nright-invariant horizontal complement to\n$V\\widetilde P:=\\ker(d\\widetilde\\pi)$, such that\n$d\\lambda_u(\\widetilde H_u)=H^+_{\\lambda(u)}$ at each\n$u\\in\\widetilde P$. It induces a covariant derivative on the associated\nvector bundle by horizontal differentiation: a section is represented by a\nsmooth equivariant map $f:\\widetilde P\\to V_\\sigma$ with\n$f(ua)=\\sigma(a)^{-1}f(u)$, and its derivative along a smooth base vector\nfield $X$ at $x$ is $\\nabla_Xs(x):=[u,df_u(\\widetilde X^H_u)]$ for\n$u\\in\\widetilde P_x$. Here $s(x)=[u,f(u)]$, and the **horizontal lift** is the smooth field\n$\\widetilde X^H_u:=(d\\widetilde\\pi_u|_{\\widetilde H_u})^{-1}X_x$; the restriction is a linear isomorphism because $\\widetilde H$ complements the vertical kernel. Right invariance and uniqueness give\n$\\widetilde X^H_{ua}=dR_a(\\widetilde X^H_u)$. Differentiating $f\\circ R_a=\\sigma(a)^{-1}f$ therefore gives\n$df_{ua}(\\widetilde X^H_{ua})=\\sigma(a)^{-1}df_u(\\widetilde X^H_u)$, and the associated-bundle relation makes $[u,df_u(\\widetilde X^H_u)]$ independent of $u$. In principal charts this formula is smooth, is real $C^\\infty(M)$-linear in $X$ and complex-linear in $s$. For $h\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb C)$, the product rule applied to $(h\\circ\\widetilde\\pi)f$ gives $\\nabla_X(hs)=X(h)s+h\\nabla_Xs$. These are the covariant-derivative properties used here. The local\nClifford-compatibility calculation is a separate proposition about supplied\ngamma matrices and does not follow from the existence of $\\sigma$ alone.\nA spin structure, a global spinor field, and a lifted connection are extra data: this definition asserts\nneither existence nor uniqueness of a spin lift, global tetrad, or connection\nlift. The matrix group and covering homomorphism are the Lorentzian ones specified above.",
--
