research/plan-spec.json-231677-          "dependency_level": 3,
research/plan-spec.json-231678-          "statement": "Take the oriented triangle $v_0\\xrightarrow{e_1}v_1\\xrightarrow{e_2}v_2\\xrightarrow{e_3}v_0$, assign $g_{e_1}=g_{e_2}=g_{e_3}=I_2$, and use the defining representation $\\rho:SU(2)\\to U(\\mathbb C^2)$ given by $\\rho(g)v:=gv$ for column vectors $v\\in\\mathbb C^2$. Let $k=\\operatorname{diag}(i,-i)\\in SU(2)$ and take the vertex gauge assignment $h_{v_0}=k$, $h_{v_1}=h_{v_2}=I_2$. The first and third edge matrices change to $k^{-1}$ and $k$, respectively, while the loop product remains $I_2$ and its Wilson trace remains $2$.\n\n",
--
research/plan-spec.json-231715-            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-231716-          },
research/plan-spec.json:231717:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-231718-          "dependency_level": 3,
research/plan-spec.json-231719-          "statement": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Take the graph with three distinct vertices $u_1,u_2,v$, edges $a_1:u_1\\to v$, $a_2:u_2\\to v$ and their reverses $\\bar a_1,\\bar a_2$, and no other edges. Order the edges as $a_1<a_2<\\bar a_1<\\bar a_2$ and fix any vertex order. Assign the defining representation $\\rho(g)w:=gw$ on $V=\\mathbb C^2$, with its standard inner product, to $a_1,a_2$; assign the trivial representation on $\\mathbb C$ to each reverse edge. Thus $\\rho_{\\bar a_j}(g)z=z$ for every $g\\in SU(2)$ and $z\\in\\mathbb C$; the Haar projection's hypotheses impose no relation between representations assigned to reverse orientations.\n\nThe full vertex space is $W_v=V\\otimes V\\otimes\\mathbb C^*\\otimes\\mathbb C^*$. Write $\\mathbf 1^*(z)=z$ and identify $V\\otimes V$ with $W_v$ by the linear isomorphism $\\iota(\\xi)=\\xi\\otimes\\mathbf 1^*\\otimes\\mathbf 1^*$. For any basis $e_1,e_2$ of $V$, the nonzero tensor\n\n$$\\epsilon=e_1\\otimes e_2-e_2\\otimes e_1$$\n\nis invariant under $\\rho(g)\\otimes\\rho(g)$. Consequently the normalized Haar projection $P_v:W_v\\to W_v$ fixes $\\iota(\\epsilon)$; equivalently, $\\iota^{-1}P_v\\iota(\\epsilon)=\\epsilon$.\n\n",
--
research/plan-spec.json-231784-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-231785-          },
research/plan-spec.json:231786:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-231787-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert\nspace and let $S:D(S)\\to H$ be a densely defined, closed, symmetric linear\noperator. The domain $D(S)$ is part of the operator data. Its adjoint is the\noperator $S^*:D(S^*)\\to H$ characterized by\n$\\langle Sf,g\\rangle=\\langle f,S^*g\\rangle$ for every $f\\in D(S)$ and\n$g\\in D(S^*)$, using an inner product linear in its first argument. A **graph\ncore** for $S$ is a linear subspace $C\\subseteq D(S)$ dense for the graph norm\n$\\|f\\|_S=(\\|f\\|^2+\\|Sf\\|^2)^{1/2}$; equivalently, the graph of the restriction\n$S|_C$ is dense in the graph of $S$.\n\nFix a positive scalar $E_*$ and write $I_H$ for the identity on $H$. The\n**scaled deficiency subspaces** of $S$ at $E_*$ are\n$N_+(S;E_*):=\\ker(S^*-iE_*I_H)$ and\n$N_-(S;E_*):=\\ker(S^*+iE_*I_H)$. Their **deficiency indices** are\n$n_\\pm(S;E_*):=\\dim N_\\pm(S;E_*)$, where Hilbert dimension means the\ncardinality of an orthonormal basis as in the standard deficiency-index\ndefinition. The spaces are closed: if $g_k\\in N_\\pm(S;E_*)$ and $g_k\\to g$,\nthen $S^*g_k=\\pm iE_*g_k\\to\\pm iE_*g$, so closedness of $S^*$ gives\n$g\\in D(S^*)$ and $S^*g=\\pm iE_*g$. The dimension convention is well defined\nunder Choice by the cited deficiency-index definition.\n\nFor any specified linear trace map $\\gamma:D(S^*)\\to B$ into a vector space\n$B$ and specified linear subspace $\\Lambda\\subseteq B$, the restriction of\n$S^*$ to $\\{f\\in D(S^*):\\gamma(f)\\in\\Lambda\\}$ is the boundary realization\nspecified by $(\\gamma,\\Lambda)$. Its domain and trace data are part of the\noperator realization; the differential expression alone is not.",
research/plan-spec.json-231788-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-231858-            "def-dependent-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-231859-          },
research/plan-spec.json:231860:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-231861-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, Countable Choice, and Dependent Choice. Let\n$0<L<\\infty$, let $H=L^2(0,L;\\mathbb C)$, and use the spaces $H^1(0,L)$ and\n$H^2(0,L)$ and their continuous endpoint traces defined by the one-dimensional\nSobolev suppliers. Define $T_c f=-D^2f$ on $D(T_c)=C_c^\\infty(0,L)$. Then\n$T_c$ is densely defined and symmetric, its adjoint is\n$T_{\\max}f=-D^2f$ on exactly $D(T_{\\max})=H^2(0,L)$, and its closure\n$T_{\\min}=\\overline{T_c}=T_c^{**}$ has domain\n$$D(T_{\\min})=\\{f\\in H^2(0,L): f(0)=f'(0)=f(L)=f'(L)=0\\},$$\nwith $T_{\\min}f=-D^2f$. The trace map\n$\\gamma:H^2(0,L)\\to\\mathbb C^4$ defined by\n$\\gamma(f)=(f(0),f(L),f'(0),f'(L))$ is surjective.",
research/plan-spec.json-231862-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-231928-            "def-injection-surjection-bijection": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-231929-          },
research/plan-spec.json:231930:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-231931-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert\nspace and $S:D(S)\\to H$ a densely defined, closed, symmetric linear operator.\nFix $E_*>0$ and let $N_\\pm=N_\\pm(S;E_*)$ and\n$n_\\pm=\\dim N_\\pm$ be the scaled deficiency spaces and indices. For every\nunitary map $V:N_+\\to N_-$, the operator\n$$D(S_V)=D(S)\\oplus\\{u+Vu:u\\in N_+\\},\\qquad S_V(f+u+Vu)=Sf+iE_*u-iE_*Vu$$\nis a self-adjoint extension of $S$. Every self-adjoint extension of $S$ is\nuniquely $S_V$ for one such $V$. Consequently $S$ has a self-adjoint\nextension if and only if $n_+=n_-$.",
research/plan-spec.json-231932-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-231997-            "def-dependent-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-231998-          },
research/plan-spec.json:231999:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232000-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, Countable Choice, and Dependent Choice. Let\n$0<L<\\infty$, let $H=L^2(0,L;\\mathbb C)$, and let $T_{\\min}$ and\n$T_{\\max}=T_{\\min}^*$ be the minimal and maximal realizations of\n$-D^2$ from the preceding interval-domain lemma. Fix a positive reference\nlength $\\ell$. For $f\\in H^2(0,L)$ define\n$\\Gamma_0f=(f(0),f(L))$ and\n$\\Gamma_1f=(f'(0),-f'(L))$. Let\n$U(2)=\\{U\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):U^*U=UU^*=I_2\\}$, where $U^*$ is conjugate\ntranspose. For each $U\\in U(2)$ define\n$$D(T_U)=\\{f\\in H^2(0,L):(U-I_2)\\Gamma_0f+i\\ell(U+I_2)\\Gamma_1f=0\\},\\qquad T_Uf=-D^2f.$$\nThen $T_{\\min}\\subseteq T_U\\subseteq T_{\\max}$, each $T_U$ is self-adjoint,\nand every self-adjoint realization between $T_{\\min}$ and $T_{\\max}$ equals\nexactly one $T_U$. The reference length makes the value and scaled derivative\nboundary vectors have the same units if the interval coordinate is assigned\nunits; in the dimensionless mathematical model they are numerical vectors.",
research/plan-spec.json-232001-          "justified_by": [],
--
