research/plan-spec.json-232313-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232314-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232315:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232316-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space, let $Q\\subseteq H$ be a dense linear subspace, and let\n$q:Q\\times Q\\to\\mathbb C$ be a symmetric sesquilinear form, linear in its\nfirst variable. Write $q[u]=q(u,u)$. Suppose that for some $c\\ge0$,\n$q[u]\\ge-c\\|u\\|^2$ for every $u\\in Q$, and that $Q$ is complete in the form\nnorm $\\|u\\|_{q,c}^2=q[u]+(c+1)\\|u\\|^2$. Then there is a unique self-adjoint\noperator $H_q\\ge-cI$ such that\n$$D((H_q+cI)^{1/2})=Q,\\qquad q(u,v)=\\langle H_qu,v\\rangle\\quad(u\\in D(H_q),\\ v\\in Q).$$\nEquivalently, $D(H_q)$ consists exactly of those $u\\in Q$ for which there is\n$f\\in H$ satisfying $q(u,v)=\\langle f,v\\rangle$ for all $v\\in Q$, and then\n$H_qu=f$. Under the $e^{tA}$ generator convention for associated forms, the\nassociated operator is $A=-H_q$. If $q_0$ is a densely defined closed symmetric\nform with $q_0[u]\\ge0$ and $v$ is a symmetric sesquilinear form on $Q(q_0)$\nsatisfying\n$$|v[u,u]|\\le a q_0[u]+b\\|u\\|^2\\quad(u\\in Q(q_0)),\\qquad 0\\le a<1,\\ b\\ge0,$$\nthen $q_0+v$ is closed and bounded below by $-b$ on the same domain. It has a\nunique self-adjoint representing operator there. The form bound is not an\noperator-relative bound.",
research/plan-spec.json-232317-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232370-            "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232371-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232372:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232373-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $H_0$ be a self-adjoint operator on a complex\nHilbert space $H$, with inner product linear in its first variable and\n$\\inf\\sigma(H_0)>-\\infty$. Let $g\\in H$, $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$, and define\n$P_g:H\\to H$ by $P_gf=\\langle f,g\\rangle g$. Put\n$H_\\lambda=H_0+\\lambda P_g$ on $D(H_\\lambda)=D(H_0)$ and\n$R_0(z)=(H_0-zI)^{-1}$. Then $H_\\lambda$ is self-adjoint. For every\n$z\\in\\rho(H_0)$ such that\n$d_\\lambda(z):=1+\\lambda\\langle R_0(z)g,g\\rangle\\ne0$, one has\n$z\\in\\rho(H_\\lambda)$ and\n$$R_\\lambda(z):=(H_\\lambda-zI)^{-1}=R_0(z)-\\frac{\\lambda R_0(z)P_gR_0(z)}{1+\\lambda\\langle R_0(z)g,g\\rangle}.$$\nFor real $E<\\inf\\sigma(H_0)$ and $g\\ne0$, $E$ is an eigenvalue of $H_\\lambda$\nif and only if $d_\\lambda(E)=0$; in that case the eigenspace is one-dimensional.",
research/plan-spec.json-232374-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232439-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
