research/plan-spec.json-232442-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. On\n$H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$ use the unitary Fourier transform\n$\\widehat u(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}u(x)\\,dx$ on\n$L^1\\cap L^2$. Let $H_0=-\\Delta$ be the self-adjoint multiplier\n$\\mathcal F_2^*M_{4\\pi^2|\\xi|^2}\\mathcal F_2$ with domain\n$D(H_0)=W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$. Put\n$Q_0=W^{1,2}(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$ and\n$$q_0(u,v)=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\langle \\partial_j u,\\partial_j v\\rangle_{L^2}\\quad(u,v\\in Q_0),$$\nwhere $\\partial_j$ are weak derivatives. Then $q_0$ is dense, symmetric,\nnonnegative, and closed; its associated self-adjoint operator is exactly $H_0$,\nwith $Q(H_0)=D(H_0^{1/2})=Q_0$ and\n$$q_0(u,v)=\\langle H_0u,v\\rangle\\quad(u\\in D(H_0),\\ v\\in Q_0).$$",
research/plan-spec.json-232443-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232502-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232503-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232504:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232505-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $H\\ne\\{0\\}$ be a complex Hilbert space and\nlet $A$ be a self-adjoint operator with $A\\ge-cI$ for some $c\\ge0$. Let\n$E_A$ be its spectral projection-valued measure, let\n$Q(A)=D((A+cI)^{1/2})$, and define the associated form by\n$$q_A[u]=\\|(A+cI)^{1/2}u\\|^2-c\\|u\\|^2\\quad(u\\in Q(A)).$$\nSet $\\alpha=\\inf\\sigma_{\\mathrm{ess}}(A)$, with $\\alpha=+\\infty$ when the\nessential spectrum is empty. For each integer $k\\ge1$, put\n$$\\lambda_k(A)=\\inf_{\\substack{L\\subseteq Q(A)\\text{ linear},\\ \\dim L=k}}\\ \\sup_{\\substack{u\\in L\\\\\\|u\\|=1}}q_A[u],$$\nwhere the infimum of an empty family is $+\\infty$. Then $\\lambda_k(A)$ is the\n$k$th eigenvalue below $\\alpha$, counted with multiplicity, when at least $k$\nsuch eigenvalues exist; otherwise $\\lambda_k(A)=\\alpha$. If the inclusion\n$Q(A)\\hookrightarrow H$ is compact in the form norm\n$\\|u\\|_{Q(A)}^2=q_A[u]+(c+1)\\|u\\|^2$, then $A$ has a complete orthonormal\neigenbasis; every eigenvalue has finite multiplicity, and the only possible\naccumulation is at $+\\infty$.",
research/plan-spec.json-232506-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232561-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232562-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232563:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-232564-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. On\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$ use\n$\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\int e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}f(x)\\,dx$ and the unitary\nextension $\\mathcal F_2$. For $\\kappa>0$, let\n$$G_\\kappa(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\kappa|x|}}{4\\pi|x|}\\quad(x\\ne0),$$\nwith any value at $x=0$. Then $G_\\kappa\\in L^1\\cap L^2$, and\n$$\\widehat G_\\kappa(\\xi)=\\frac1{4\\pi^2|\\xi|^2+\\kappa^2}.$$\nConsequently convolution by $G_\\kappa$ is the bounded operator\n$(-\\Delta+\\kappa^2)^{-1}$ on $L^2$, with its convolution integral interpreted\nin the usual almost-everywhere $L^2$ sense.",
research/plan-spec.json-232565-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232626-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232627-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232628:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232629-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let\n$W:\\mathbb R^3\\to[0,\\infty)$ be measurable, essentially bounded, and zero\nalmost everywhere outside a compact set $S$. Put $s(x)=\\sqrt{W(x)}$ almost\neverywhere. On $L^2=L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$, let\n$M_s,M_W:L^2\\to L^2$ be the multiplication operators $(M_sf)(x)=s(x)f(x)$\nand $(M_Wf)(x)=W(x)f(x)$, interpreted as almost-everywhere classes. These are\nbounded nonnegative self-adjoint operators with $M_s^2=M_W$ and\n$\\|M_s\\|^2,\\|M_W\\|\\le\\|W\\|_{L^\\infty}$. Let $H_0=-\\Delta$ be the free\nLaplacian with domain\n$D(H_0)=W^{2,2}(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$, and set\n$H=H_0-M_W$ on $D(H_0)$. For $\\kappa>0$ define\n$$K_\\kappa=M_s(H_0+\\kappa^2 I)^{-1}M_s.$$\nThen $H$ is self-adjoint and bounded below, and $K_\\kappa$ is positive,\nself-adjoint, and compact, with integral kernel\n$$s(x)\\frac{e^{-\\kappa|x-y|}}{4\\pi|x-y|}s(y).$$\nThe eigenspaces at $-\\kappa^2$ for $H$ and at $1$ for $K_\\kappa$ are linearly\nisomorphic. Moreover, the finite number of eigenvalues of $H$ strictly below\n$-\\kappa^2$, counted with multiplicity, equals the finite number of\neigenvalues of $K_\\kappa$ strictly greater than $1$, counted with\nmultiplicity.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-232630-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232696-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232697-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232698:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232699-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. On\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$ let\n$(H_0f)(p)=|p|^2f(p)$ with domain\n$D(H_0)=\\{f\\in L^2:|p|^2f(p)\\in L^2\\}$, let\n$g(p)=C e^{-|p|^2/2}$ for a constant $C\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and\nlet $\\lambda<0$. Define $P_gf=\\langle f,g\\rangle g$, using the\nfirst-variable-linear $L^2$ pairing. The self-adjoint rank-one perturbation\n$H_\\lambda=H_0+\\lambda P_g$ has a negative eigenvalue\n$-\\kappa^2$ exactly when\n$$1+\\lambda\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{|g(p)|^2}{|p|^2+\\kappa^2}\\,dp=0,\\qquad\\kappa>0.$$\nThere is at most one such eigenvalue. More precisely, it exists exactly when\n$1+\\lambda I_0<0$, where\n$I_0=\\int |g(p)|^2|p|^{-2}\\,dp\\in(0,\\infty)$; equality is the threshold\ncase and yields no negative eigenvalue.",
research/plan-spec.json-232700-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232747-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
