research/plan-spec.json:232749:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232750-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. For the free Hamiltonian\n$H_0=-\\Delta$ on $L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$ with form\ndomain $Q(H_0)=H^1(\\mathbb R^3)$, it is false that every normalized $L^2$\nvector is an admissible finite-energy Rayleigh–Ritz trial vector. In the\n$2\\pi$ Fourier convention, the normalized vector with\n$$\\widehat f(\\xi)=c(1+|\\xi|^2)^{-1},\\qquad c=\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}(1+|\\xi|^2)^{-2}\\,d\\xi\\right)^{-1/2},$$\nlies in $L^2$ but not in $H^1$, so it is outside the form domain and has no\nfinite free kinetic form value.",
research/plan-spec.json-232751-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232820-            "thm-canonical-spectral-type-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232821-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232822:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232823-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\mathcal H_0,\\mathcal H$ be separable complex Hilbert spaces, $H_0$ and $H$ self-adjoint operators on them, and $J:\\mathcal H_0\\to\\mathcal H$ a bounded linear map. Write $E_{H_0}$ and $E_H$ for their spectral projection valued measures. The absolutely continuous subspace $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)$ consists of vectors $u$ for which the scalar measure $B\\mapsto\\|E_{H_0}(B)u\\|^2$ is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure; $P_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)$ is its orthogonal projection. Define the two-space wave operators, when the pointwise strong limits exist on $\\mathcal H_0$, by\n\n$$\\Omega_\\pm(H,H_0;J):=\\operatorname*{s-lim}_{t\\to\\pm\\infty} e^{itH}J e^{-itH_0}P_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H_0),$$\n\nwhere a strong limit means that for every $u\\in\\mathcal H_0$ the vector-valued expression converges in $\\mathcal H$. The two signs are separate limits. When $J=I$ and $\\mathcal H_0=\\mathcal H$, write $\\Omega_\\pm(H,H_0)$. A wave operator is complete when its range is all of $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H)$. When both wave operators exist and are isometries on $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)$, the scattering operator on that subspace is\n\n$$S(H,H_0;J):=\\Omega_+^*\\Omega_-.$$\n\nWhenever both wave operators exist, are isometries on $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H_0)$, and are complete with range $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H)$, the scattering operator $S(H,H_0;J)$ is unitary. For a fixed integer $r\\ge1$ and an open energy interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$, finite-channel data consists of the standard space $\\mathbb C^r$ and a matrix field $S:I\\to U(r)$, where\n\n$$U(r):=\\{U\\in M_r(\\mathbb C):U^*U=UU^*=I_r\\}.$$\n\nHere $U^*$ is conjugate transpose, $\\operatorname{tr}U=\\sum_{j=1}^r U_{jj}$, and $\\det U$ is the usual alternating determinant. This matrix field is separate specified data; the definition does not assert that it is obtained from the abstract wave operators above.",
research/plan-spec.json-232824-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232865-            "thm-self-adjoint-resolvent-estimate": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232866-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232867:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-232868-          "statement": "Let $T$ be self-adjoint on a complex Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$ whose inner product is linear in the first variable, use the resolvent convention $R_T(z)=(T-zI)^{-1}$, and let $X\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a specified vector subspace. For $f,h\\in X$, the scalar resolvent matrix element is\n\n$$r_{f,h}(z):=\\langle R_T(z)f,h\\rangle,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z>0.$$\n\nGiven an open real interval $I$, a point $E_0\\in I$, and a disk $D$ centered at $E_0$, a local analytic continuation of these matrix elements through $E_0$ is a specified family of holomorphic functions $\\widetilde r_{f,h}:D\\to\\mathbb C$ agreeing with $r_{f,h}$ on $D\\cap\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>0\\}$, for the stated $f,h\\in X$. A **meromorphic continuation** on $D$ is instead a specified family $\\widetilde r_{f,h}$ agreeing with $r_{f,h}$ on $D\\cap\\{\\operatorname{Im}z>0\\}$, holomorphic on $D$ except for isolated poles. At every crossing point where no pole occurs it is a regular analytic continuation on a smaller neighborhood. For such a meromorphic continuation, if $z_0\\in D$ is nonreal and for some $f,h\\in X$ the continued function satisfies\n\n$$\\widetilde r_{f,h}(z)=(z-z_0)^{-k}q(z)$$\n\non a punctured neighborhood of $z_0$, for an integer $k\\ge1$ and a holomorphic $q$ with $q(z_0)\\ne0$, then $z_0$ is a pole of order $k$ of that continued matrix element. Such a pole is called a resonance matrix-element pole for the specified pair $(T,X)$ and continuation. This definition asserts neither that a continuation exists nor that a pole is an eigenvalue of $T$; the continuation and the chosen test vectors are part of the data.",
research/plan-spec.json-232869-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232922-            "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232923-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232924:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-232925-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. On $\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,dp;\\mathbb C)$ with $\\langle f,h\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}f(p)\\overline{h(p)}\\,dp$, let $H_0=M_{|p|^2}$ on its maximal domain $\\{f:|p|^2f\\in L^2\\}$, let $g(p)=\\pi^{-3/4}e^{-|p|^2/2}$, and put\n\n$$m(z):=\\langle(H_0-zI)^{-1}g,g\\rangle,\\qquad \\operatorname{Im}z>0.$$\n\nThis is a dimensionless model; no physical units are assigned to $p$ or $z$.\n\nFor $w\\in\\mathbb C$, define the entire function\n\n$$\\mathcal E(w):=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt\\pi}\\int_{\\Gamma_w}e^{-\\zeta^2}\\,d\\zeta,\\qquad \\Gamma_w=\\{w+t:t\\ge0\\},$$\n\nwhere $\\Gamma_w$ is the horizontal ray oriented from $w$ toward $+\\infty+w$. For any $E_0>0$, take a disk $D$ centered at $E_0$ with $0\\notin D$, and the holomorphic square root $k:D\\to\\mathbb C$ with $k(E_0)=\\sqrt{E_0}>0$. Then $m$ has the local analytic continuation through $E_0$\n\n$$m_{\\mathrm{cont}}(z)=2+2i\\sqrt\\pi\\,k(z)e^{-z}\\mathcal E(-ik(z)),\\qquad z\\in D.$$",
research/plan-spec.json-232926-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-232979-            "thm-canonical-spectral-type-decomposition": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-232980-          },
research/plan-spec.json:232981:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-232982-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let\n$V\\in L^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$ and suppose there is a compact set\n$K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ such that $V=0$ almost everywhere outside $K$.\nLet $\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$, and set\n$H_0=-\\Delta$ and $H=H_0+M_V$ on\n$D(H_0)=D(H)=H^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C)$. Use\n\n$$\\widehat f(p)=(2\\pi)^{-3/2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-ip\\cdot x}f(x)\\,dx,\\qquad \\Omega_\\pm=\\operatorname*{s-lim}_{t\\to\\pm\\infty}e^{itH}e^{-itH_0}, \\qquad S=\\Omega_+^*\\Omega_-.$$\n\nHere $M_Vf=Vf$, and the strong limits act on every $f\\in\\mathcal H$. Then:\n\n(a) The wave operators exist, are isometries, and satisfy\n\n$$\\operatorname{ran}\\Omega_+=\\operatorname{ran}\\Omega_-=\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H)=\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm c}(H), \\qquad \\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{sc}}(H)=\\{0\\}.$$\n\nFor $u\\in\\mathcal H$, write $\\mu_u(B)=\\|E_H(B)u\\|^2$. The subspace\n$\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H)$ consists of vectors with\n$\\mu_u\\ll dE$; $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{sc}}(H)$ is the subspace with atomless\nscalar spectral measures singular with respect to Lebesgue measure;\n$\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{pp}}(H)$ is the closed span of eigenspaces, and\n$\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm c}(H)=\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{pp}}(H)^\\perp$. These are the\ncanonical spectral-type subspaces of the PVM.\n\n(b) The scattering operator $S$ is unitary on $\\mathcal H$ and commutes with $e^{-itH_0}$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nUse time in the corresponding $\\hbar=1$ evolution convention and the\nstandard scaled Schrödinger normalization in which the kinetic\ncoefficient $\\hbar^2/(2m)$ is set to $1$. When length is dimensional,\n$E$ and $V$ then have units $L^{-2}$ and $k=\\sqrt E$ has units\n$L^{-1}$; the proof is an operator deduction in this normalization.\n\n(c) Put $\\mathfrak h=L^2(S^2,d\\sigma)$, where $d\\sigma(S^2)=4\\pi$, and define the normalized free energy transform\n\n$$(F_0f)(E,\\omega)=\\frac{E^{1/4}}{\\sqrt2}\\widehat f(\\sqrt E\\,\\omega), \\qquad F_0:\\mathcal H\\to L^2((0,\\infty),dE;\\mathfrak h).$$\n\nFix a bounded ball $B$ containing $K$. For $E>0$,\n$k=\\sqrt E$, define bounded maps $Z_0(E):L^2(B)\\to\\mathfrak h$ and\n$Z_0(E)^*:\\mathfrak h\\to L^2(B)$ by\n\n$$(Z_0(E)f)(\\omega)=\\frac{\\sqrt k}{4\\pi^{3/2}}\\int_Be^{-ik\\omega\\cdot x}f(x)\\,dx,\\qquad (Z_0(E)^*a)(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt k}{4\\pi^{3/2}}\\int_{S^2}e^{ik\\omega\\cdot x}a(\\omega)\\,d\\sigma(\\omega).$$\n\nLet $R_H(E+i0):L^2(B)\\to L^2(B)$ be the operator-norm limit\n$\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\mathbf1_B(H-(E+i\\epsilon)I)^{-1}\\mathbf1_B$;\nexistence and continuity for $E>0$ are part of the stated supplier input.\nThe products below are these bounded maps on $B$. Then $F_0$ is unitary and\nthere is a measurable unitary field $S_{\\mathrm{stat}}(E)\\in\\mathcal B(\\mathfrak h)$ such that\n\n$$F_0SF_0^*=M_{S_{\\mathrm{stat}}(E)},\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{stat}}(E)=I_{\\mathfrak h}-2\\pi i\\,Z_0(E)\\bigl[V-VR_H(E+i0)V\\bigr]Z_0(E)^*, \\quad E>0.$$\n\nThe formula uses only positive energies; it makes no claim at the threshold $E=0$, and no long-range Coulomb potential is included.",
research/plan-spec.json-232983-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-233024-            "def-nrqmx-scattering-channel-data": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-233025-          },
research/plan-spec.json:233026:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-233027-          "statement": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open energy interval with fixed finite channel number $r\\ge1$, let $S:I\\to U(r)$ be $C^1$ in the finite-channel convention of [[def-nrqmx-scattering-channel-data]], and let $\\hbar>0$. Define\n\n$$Q(E):=-i\\hbar\\,S(E)^*S'(E),\\qquad E\\in I.$$\n\nThen $Q(E)$ is Hermitian and\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}Q(E) =-i\\hbar\\,\\frac{(\\det S)'(E)}{\\det S(E)} =-i\\hbar\\,\\frac{d}{dE}\\log\\det S(E),$$\n\nwhere the last expression means the logarithmic derivative $(\\det S)'/\\det S$, independent of a choice of logarithm branch. If the incoming and outgoing bases are changed by energy-independent unitary matrices $U_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and $U_{\\mathrm{out}}$, so that $S^\\sharp=U_{\\mathrm{out}}^*SU_{\\mathrm{in}}$, then\n\n$$Q^\\sharp=U_{\\mathrm{in}}^* Q U_{\\mathrm{in}}.$$\n\nIf $E$ is measured in joules and $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, $Q$ has units of seconds. This is an algebraic scattering-matrix result; it does not by itself establish an operational arrival-time or dwell-time interpretation.",
research/plan-spec.json-233028-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-233093-            "thm-nrqmx-rank-one-resolvent": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-233094-          },
research/plan-spec.json:233095:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-233096-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $H_0$ be a self-adjoint operator on a complex Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$ with inner product linear in its first variable, bounded below, let $g\\in\\mathcal H$, let $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and put\n\n$$P_gf=\\langle f,g\\rangle g,\\qquad H_\\lambda=H_0+\\lambda P_g\\quad\\hbox{on }D(H_0),\\qquad R_0(z)=(H_0-zI)^{-1}.$$\n\nAdopt as an explicit conditional input the rank-one resolvent interface: $H_\\lambda$ is self-adjoint, and whenever $z\\in\\rho(H_0)$ and $d(z):=1+\\lambda m(z)\\ne0$, where $m(z)=\\langle R_0(z)g,g\\rangle$, one has\n\n$$(H_\\lambda-zI)^{-1} =R_0(z)-\\frac{\\lambda R_0(z)P_gR_0(z)}{d(z)}.$$\n\nSpectral variables use the energy unit of $H_0$. If $\\|g\\|$ is\ndimensionless, $m(z)$ has inverse-energy units and $\\lambda$ has energy\nunits; in general the coupling is chosen so that $\\lambda m(z)$ is\ndimensionless.\n\nLet $E_\\star\\in\\mathbb R$, take an open disk $D$ centered at $E_\\star$, and let $E_\\star$ lie in an open interval $I$ on which the spectral PVM $E_{H_0}(\\cdot)$ of $H_0$ is absolutely continuous: $E_{H_0}(N)=0$ for every Lebesgue-null Borel set $N\\subseteq I$. Suppose $m$ and, for specified test vectors $f,h$ in a subspace $X\\subseteq\\mathcal H$, the free matrix elements\n\n$$\\langle R_0(z)f,h\\rangle,\\qquad a_f(z):=\\langle R_0(z)f,g\\rangle,\\qquad b_h(z):=\\langle R_0(z)g,h\\rangle$$\n\nhave holomorphic continuations through $E_\\star$ to $D$. Suppose $d_{\\mathrm{cont}}(z)=1+\\lambda m_{\\mathrm{cont}}(z)$ has a zero $z_0\\in D$ with $\\operatorname{Im}z_0<0$ of finite order $k\\ge1$, meaning that on some neighborhood of $z_0$ one has $d_{\\mathrm{cont}}(z)=(z-z_0)^k q(z)$ for a holomorphic function $q$ with $q(z_0)\\ne0$, and suppose $a_f(z_0)b_h(z_0)\\ne0$. Then the continued matrix element $\\langle(H_\\lambda-zI)^{-1}f,h\\rangle$ has a resonance pole of order $k$. In the simple-zero case its residue is\n\n$$-\\frac{a_f(z_0)b_h(z_0)}{m_{\\mathrm{cont}}'(z_0)}.$$\n\nIn particular, for the Gaussian model $H_0=M_{|p|^2}$, $g(p)=\\pi^{-3/4}e^{-|p|^2/2}$, and $X=\\operatorname{span}\\{g\\}$, take $E_\\star>0$, an open disk $D$ centered at $E_\\star$ with $0\\notin D$, and the holomorphic square-root branch $k_D:D\\to\\mathbb C$ normalized by $k_D(E_\\star)=\\sqrt{E_\\star}>0$. (The subscript distinguishes this branch from the zero order $k$.) The continuation in the Gaussian lemma with precisely these disk and branch data supplies $m_{\\mathrm{cont}}$; every interval $I\\subset(0,\\infty)$ is absolutely continuous by the radial spectral measure formula. Every finite-order zero of $1+\\lambda m_{\\mathrm{cont}}$ in the lower half of such a disk gives a pole of the continued scalar element $\\langle(H_\\lambda-zI)^{-1}g,g\\rangle$. These poles are not nonreal eigenvalues of the self-adjoint $H_\\lambda$.\n\n",
