research/plan-spec.json:234430:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-234431-          "statement": "Give $\\mathbb R^4$ its Euclidean topology, whose basic open sets are the\nEuclidean open balls, and write a point as an ordered pair $(x,y)$ with\n$x,y\\in\\mathbb R^2$. The **ordered configuration space** is the subspace\n$$F_2(\\mathbb R^2):=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R^2:x\\ne y\\}$$\nwith the subspace topology. The transposition acts by $(x,y)\\mapsto(y,x)$.\nThe **unordered configuration space of two distinct planar points** is\n$$C_2(\\mathbb R^2):=F_2(\\mathbb R^2)/\\bigl((x,y)\\sim(y,x)\\bigr)$$\nwith the quotient topology of the canonical projection\n$q:F_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\to C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$. We write its points as unordered\nsets $\\{x,y\\}$; equality in this set means equality up to transposition.\nThe diagonal $x=y$ is excluded, so collisions are not points of this space.\n\nThe topology on each finite Cartesian coordinate space used below is its\nEuclidean topology; on a finite product it is equivalently the topology with\nbasis the products of open sets in the factors.",
research/plan-spec.json-234432-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234462-            "lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234463-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234464:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-234465-          "statement": "For each integer $N\\geq0$, let $P_N(\\mathbb C,2)$ be the quotient of the free\nunital associative complex algebra on $u_1,\\ldots,u_{N-1}$ by the relations\n$$u_i^2=2u_i,\\qquad u_iu_j=u_ju_i\\ (|i-j|>1),\\qquad u_iu_{i+1}u_i=u_i,\\quad u_{i+1}u_iu_{i+1}=u_{i+1}.$$\nThis complex Temperley–Lieb algebra has a\nconjugate-linear anti-involution $*$ determined by\n$$c^*=\\overline c\\quad(c\\in\\mathbb C),\\qquad u_i^*=u_i\\quad(1\\leq i<N),\\qquad (xy)^*=y^*x^*.$$\nFor $N=0$ or $N=1$ this is complex conjugation on $\\mathbb C$.",
research/plan-spec.json-234466-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234518-            "def-trace-of-an-endomorphism": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234519-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234520:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-234521-          "statement": "Let $d\\geq1$ and $N\\geq0$ be integers. Let $V=\\mathbb C^d$ with standard\ncoordinate basis $e_1,\\ldots,e_d$ and inner product\n$$\\langle z,w\\rangle_V:=\\sum_{r=1}^d z_r\\overline{w_r},$$\nlinear in its first argument. Put $H_N:=V^{\\otimes N}$, with $H_0:=\\mathbb C$.\nFor $N\\geq1$ equip $H_N$ with the sesquilinear form linear in its first\nargument specified on elementary tensors by\n$$\\langle v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N,w_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes w_N\\rangle_{H_N}:=\\prod_{j=1}^N\\langle v_j,w_j\\rangle_V,$$\nand extended sesquilinearly; for $N=0$ use $\\langle z,w\\rangle_{H_0}:=z\\overline w$\n(the empty product is $1$). Let\n$$U(d):=\\{A\\in M_d(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I_d\\},$$\nwhere $A^*$ is the conjugate transpose. For $\\pi\\in S_N$ use the left place action\n$$\\rho(\\pi)(v_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_N):=v_{\\pi^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\pi^{-1}(N)},$$\nand for $U\\in U(d)$ use the diagonal action $\\Delta_N(U):=U^{\\otimes N}$.\nFor every partition $\\lambda\\vdash N$, let $S^\\lambda$ be the corresponding\ncomplex Specht module and put\n$$M_\\lambda:=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,H_N).$$\nFor $\\ell(\\lambda)\\le d$, also put\n$$d_\\lambda:=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S^\\lambda.$$\nFor each $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with $\\ell(\\lambda)\\le d$, equip $S^\\lambda$\nwith any $S_N$-invariant Hermitian inner product and equip $M_\\lambda$ with\nthe normalized Hilbert-Schmidt inner product\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{M_\\lambda}:=d_\\lambda^{-1}\\operatorname{Tr}(g^*f).$$\nEquip each $S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$ with the product inner product,\ndefined on elementary tensors by\n$\\langle s\\otimes f,s'\\otimes g\\rangle=\n\\langle s,s'\\rangle\\langle f,g\\rangle$ and extended sesquilinearly, and give\nthe direct sum the orthogonal direct-sum inner product.\nThen the evaluation map\n$$\\Phi:\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\ \\ell(\\lambda)\\leq d}S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda\\longrightarrow H_N,\\qquad \\Phi(s\\otimes f):=f(s),$$\nis unitary. Under this decomposition every permutation acts on $S^\\lambda$\nand as the identity on $M_\\lambda$, while every diagonal $U(d)$ action acts\nas the identity on $S^\\lambda$ and by postcomposition on $M_\\lambda$. The\nmultiplicity spaces in the sum are nonzero; partitions with more than $d$\nrows have zero multiplicity space.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-234522-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234564-            "thm-quotient-universal-property": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234565-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234566:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-234567-          "statement": "Let $q_0:=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}\\in C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$. There is a pointed\nhomeomorphism\n$$\\bigl(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0\\bigr)\\cong \\bigl(\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z, (0,1,[0])\\bigr),$$\nand\n$$\\pi_1(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0)\\cong(\\mathbb Z,+).$$\nUnder the isomorphism specified in the proof, the counterclockwise exchange\nloop\n$$\\gamma(t):=\\{(\\cos\\pi t,\\sin\\pi t),(-\\cos\\pi t,-\\sin\\pi t)\\},\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq1,$$\nis a positive generator. The loop in which the relative direction makes one\nfull counterclockwise turn is $[\\gamma]^2$, so it represents twice the\ngenerator.",
research/plan-spec.json-234568-          "provenance": {
