research/plan-spec.json-234604-            "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234605-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234606:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-234607-          "statement": "Let $N\\geq1$, $\\mathcal H_N=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}$ with the standard\northonormal basis $|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle$ on each factor, and for\n$1\\leq i<N$ define $s_i$ to swap factors $i,i+1$. Put\n$|\\omega\\rangle=(|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$,\n$P_\\omega v=\\langle v,\\omega\\rangle\\omega$, let $P_i$ act as $P_\\omega$ on\nfactors $i,i+1$ and identity on all others, and set $e_i=I-s_i$. For every\nbond,\n$$e_i=I-s_i=2P_i,\\qquad e_i^*=e_i,\\qquad e_i^2=2e_i.$$\nFor $1\\leq i<N-1$,\n$$e_i e_{i+1}e_i=e_i,\\qquad e_{i+1}e_i e_{i+1}=e_{i+1},$$\nand $e_i e_j=e_j e_i$ when $|i-j|>1$. Consequently the assignment\n$u_i\\mapsto e_i$ defines a star-preserving algebra representation\n$$P_N(\\mathbb C,2)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{End}(\\mathcal H_N).$$\nFor $\\lambda\\vdash N$ with at most two rows, put\n$M_\\lambda=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,\\mathcal H_N)$ and use the\nunitary Schur–Weyl decomposition\n$\\mathcal H_N\\cong\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\,\\ell(\\lambda)\\le2}\nS^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda$. Each $e_i$ acts on the Specht factor as\n$I-\\rho_\\lambda((i,i+1))$ and as identity on the multiplicity factor. No\nfaithfulness assertion is made.",
research/plan-spec.json-234608-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234640-            "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234641-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234642:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-234643-          "statement": "Let $N\\geq1$, let $\\hbar>0$, and let real couplings\n$\\mathcal J_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be given for $1\\leq i<N$. On\n$\\mathcal H_N=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}$, let $\\sigma_i^a$ be the Pauli\nmatrix $\\sigma^a$ acting on factor $i$ and identity on all other factors,\nwhere\n$$\\sigma^x=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma^y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma^z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nLet $s_i$ swap factors $i,i+1$ and put $e_i=I-s_i$. Set\n$S_i^a:=\\hbar\\sigma_i^a/2$ for $a\\in\\{x,y,z\\}$, and define the finite-chain\noperator\n$$H:=\\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\\mathcal J_i\\hbar^{-2}\\sum_{a=x,y,z}S_i^aS_{i+1}^a.$$\nFor every bond,\n$$\\hbar^{-2}\\sum_a S_i^aS_{i+1}^a=\\tfrac12s_i-\\tfrac14I =\\tfrac14I-\\tfrac12e_i.$$\nThus $H=\\sum_i\\mathcal J_i(\\tfrac14I-\\tfrac12e_i)$ is self-adjoint, belongs\nto the represented Temperley–Lieb algebra, and on each summand of the unitary\ndecomposition\n$$\\mathcal H_N\\cong\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash N,\\,\\ell(\\lambda)\\le2} S^\\lambda\\otimes M_\\lambda,\\qquad M_\\lambda:=\\operatorname{Hom}_{S_N}(S^\\lambda,\\mathcal H_N),$$\nacts on $S^\\lambda$ and as identity on $M_\\lambda$. For $N=1$ the sum is\nempty and $H=0$.",
research/plan-spec.json-234644-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234682-            "def-covering-map-and-evenly-covered-neighbourhoods": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234683-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234684:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-234685-          "statement": "Use the pointed coordinate identification\n$$C_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhich sends $q_0=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}$ to $(0,1,[0])$, to write\n$$X:=C_2(\\mathbb R^2)\\cong\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z.$$\nPut $\\widetilde X:=\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ and define\n$$p(c,\\rho,t):=(c,\\rho,[t])\\in X,\\qquad T_n(c,\\rho,t):=(c,\\rho,t+n)\\quad(n\\in\\mathbb Z).$$\nDefine $U(1):=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}$ under complex multiplication. Let\n$\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$ satisfy\n$\\chi(0)=1$ and $\\chi(m+n)=\\chi(m)\\chi(n)$ for all $m,n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\nLet $\\mathbb Z$ act on\n$\\widetilde X\\times\\mathbb C$ by\n$$n\\cdot((c,\\rho,t),z):=((c,\\rho,t+n),\\chi(n)^{-1}z).$$\nThe homomorphism law for $\\chi$ makes this a group action, and its orbits\ndefine the quotient relation.\nDefine the **character quotient** and its projection by\n$$L_\\chi:=(\\widetilde X\\times\\mathbb C)/\\mathbb Z, \\qquad \\Pi_\\chi([\\widetilde x,z]):=p(\\widetilde x),$$\nwith the quotient topology on $L_\\chi$. Since $p(T_n\\widetilde x)=p(\\widetilde x)$,\nit induces a continuous map $\\Pi_\\chi:L_\\chi\\to X$. Once $p$ is known to be\na covering map, the **monodromy** of this quotient around a based loop means\nthe fiber map obtained by lifting the loop and keeping its complex coordinate\n$z$ constant along the lift.\n\nFor this item, a **unitary flat complex line bundle** over a space $X$ means a\ncontinuous surjection $\\Pi:L\\to X$ with local trivializations\n$\\Pi^{-1}(U)\\cong U\\times\\mathbb C$ over open sets $U$, whose changes of fiber\ncoordinate on overlaps are multiplication by functions $g:U\\cap V\\to U(1)$\nthat are locally constant (constant on some neighbourhood of each point).\nThe definition above names the quotient data; the following theorem verifies\nthat $L_\\chi$ has these line-bundle and flatness properties.",
research/plan-spec.json-234686-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234730-            "thm-path-lifting-for-covering-maps": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234731-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234732:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-234733-          "statement": "For every character $\\chi:\\mathbb Z\\to U(1)$, the coordinate covering\n$p:\\widetilde X\\to X$ and quotient $\\Pi_\\chi:L_\\chi\\to X$ defined by the\ncharacter-quotient construction have the following properties:\n\n1. $p$ is a universal covering map and its deck transformations are exactly\n   $T_n(c,\\rho,t)=(c,\\rho,t+n)$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n2. $\\Pi_\\chi$ is a unitary flat complex line bundle, meaning its local\n   transition phases are locally constant.\n3. Parallel transport defined by keeping the lifted fiber coordinate fixed\n   has monodromy $\\chi(n)$ on a based loop whose lift ends at $T_n\\widetilde x$.\n\nIn particular, a positive planar exchange has monodromy $\\chi(1)$ and a full\nrelative winding has monodromy $\\chi(2)$.",
research/plan-spec.json-234734-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234788-            "thm-nrqmx-planar-character-line-bundle": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234789-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234790:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-234791-          "statement": "Let $C_2(\\mathbb R^2)$ be the quotient space of two ordered distinct points\nin $\\mathbb R^2$ by transposition, with basepoint\n$q_0=\\{(1,0),(-1,0)\\}$. A **one-dimensional unitary character** means a group homomorphism\n$$\\chi:\\pi_1(C_2(\\mathbb R^2),q_0)\\longrightarrow U(1),$$\nwhere $U(1)$ is the multiplicative group of complex numbers of modulus one.\nFor every such character there is a unique angle modulo $2\\pi$ with\n$$\\chi(n[\\gamma])=e^{in\\theta}:=\\cos(n\\theta)+i\\sin(n\\theta),\\qquad n\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhere $\\gamma(t)=\\{(\\cos\\pi t,\\sin\\pi t),(-\\cos\\pi t,-\\sin\\pi t)\\}$\nis the counterclockwise exchange path and $[\\gamma]$ its positive generator.\nA full\ncounterclockwise turn of the relative direction is $2[\\gamma]$ and has phase\n$e^{2i\\theta}$. The character factors through the permutation group\n$S_2\\cong\\mathbb Z/2\\mathbb Z$ exactly when $\\theta\\equiv0$ or $\\pi$\nmodulo $2\\pi$; these are the symmetric and antisymmetric exchange characters.\nBoth sectors occur in $(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes2}$, but this theorem makes no\nphysical choice between them. The associated quotient $L_{\\chi_\\theta}$ is a\nflat unitary complex line bundle whose monodromy is $e^{i\\theta}$ around the\npositive exchange and $e^{2i\\theta}$ around a full relative winding.",
research/plan-spec.json-234792-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234837-            "thm-nrqmx-schur-weyl-exchange-blocks": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-234838-          },
research/plan-spec.json:234839:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-234840-          "statement": "The false claim is: if a linear operator on\n$H_2=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes2}$ commutes with the transposition that swaps the\ntwo tensor factors, then the whole space $H_2$ must lie in one exchange\neigenspace, either the symmetric $+1$ eigenspace or the antisymmetric $-1$\neigenspace. More generally, the claim that permutation invariance by itself\nchooses one of those two sectors is false.",
research/plan-spec.json-234841-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-234877-            "thm-tensor-product-basis-from-bases": "mathematical-premise"
