research/plan-spec.json:235339:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235340-          "statement": "Let $d,e\\ge1$ and let $\\mathcal A$ be a finite nonempty outcome set. A finite completely positive instrument from $\\mathbb C^d$ to $\\mathbb C^e$ is a family of completely positive complex-linear maps $I_a:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$, indexed by $a\\in\\mathcal A$, whose sum is trace-preserving. Then there are an integer $q\\ge1$, an isometry $V:\\mathbb C^d\\to\\mathbb C^e\\otimes\\mathbb C^q$, and pairwise orthogonal projections $Q_a$ on $\\mathbb C^q$ with $\\sum_aQ_a=I_q$ such that, for every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$,\n$$\nI_a(X)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left[(I_e\\otimes Q_a)VXV^*(I_e\\otimes Q_a)\\right].\n$$\nHere $E=\\mathbb C^q$ is the finite register space with its displayed orthonormal basis, and the partial trace is defined on its matrix units by $\\operatorname{Tr}_E(Y\\otimes|e_{a,r}\\rangle\\langle e_{b,s}|)=\\delta_{(a,r),(b,s)}Y$, extended linearly. The register basis is indexed by the Kraus labels $(a,r)$; a zero outcome map has no labels and corresponds to $Q_a=0$.",
research/plan-spec.json-235341-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235373-            "lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235374-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235375:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235376-          "statement": "Let $H$ be a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space, let $I,J$ be finite nonempty sets, and let $(P_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Q_j)_{j\\in J}$ be projection-valued measures: $P_i=P_i^*=P_i^2$, $Q_j=Q_j^*=Q_j^2$, distinct projections in each family are orthogonal, and $\\sum_iP_i=\\sum_jQ_j=I_H$. A joint POVM is a family of effects $(G_{ij})_{(i,j)\\in I\\times J}$ with $\\sum_{i,j}G_{ij}=I_H$, $\\sum_jG_{ij}=P_i$, and $\\sum_iG_{ij}=Q_j$. Such a joint POVM exists exactly when $P_iQ_j=Q_jP_i$ for every $i,j$. When it exists it is unique and equals $G_{ij}=P_iQ_j$.",
research/plan-spec.json-235377-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235413-            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235414-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235415:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-235416-          "statement": "Let $\\mathsf H_d$ be the real vector space of Hermitian $d\\times d$ complex matrices from the preceding definition. The pairing $\\langle A,B\\rangle_{\\mathrm{HS}}=\\operatorname{Tr}(AB)$ is real-valued, symmetric, real-bilinear, and positive definite on $\\mathsf H_d$. Its associated norm is $\\|A\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Tr}(A^2)}$. For every $m\\ge1$ and finite family $F_1,\\ldots,F_m\\in\\mathsf H_d$, the Gram matrix $G_{ab}=\\operatorname{Tr}(F_aF_b)$ is real symmetric positive semidefinite, equals $S^*S$ for the synthesis map $S c=\\sum_ac_aF_a$, and satisfies $\\ker G=\\ker S$.",
research/plan-spec.json-235417-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235447-            "lem-nrqm-composites-finite-effects-and-instruments": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235448-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235449:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235450-          "statement": "Input integers $d,m\\ge1$, a finite POVM $(F_a)_{a=1}^m$ on $\\mathbb C^d$ that spans the real Hermitian matrices, and real frequencies $f_a\\ge0$ with $\\sum_af_a=1$. Form the Gram matrix $G_{ab}=\\operatorname{Tr}(F_aF_b)$, compute its Moore–Penrose inverse $G^+$, set $D_a=\\sum_b(G^+)_{ba}F_b$, and return\n$$\nX=\\sum_{a=1}^m f_aD_a.\n$$\nAll sums and matrices are finite. The output is Hermitian. The procedure does not project $X$ onto the positive cone, and it makes no positivity claim for finite frequency inputs.",
research/plan-spec.json-235451-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235485-            "thm-moore-penrose-pseudoinverse-exists-and-is-unique": "mathematical-premise"
