research/plan-spec.json:235624:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235625-          "statement": "On $\\mathbb C^2$, define the Pauli matrices\n$$\n\\sigma_x=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma_y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad \\sigma_z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\nLet $I=I_2$, let $\\mathsf H_2=\\{A\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):A=A^*\\}$, and let $\\operatorname{Tr}$ denote the diagonal-sum trace. For $v\\in\\mathbb C^2$, write $v^*=\\bar v^{\\mathsf T}$. For $A\\in M_2(\\mathbb C)$, $A\\ge0$ means $A=A^*$ and $v^*Av\\ge0$ for every $v\\in\\mathbb C^2$. A finite POVM is a finite family of positive semidefinite matrices whose sum is $I$; it is informationally complete when its real linear span is $\\mathsf H_2$. The six matrices $F_{j,\\pm}=(I\\pm\\sigma_j)/6$, with $j\\in\\{x,y,z\\}$, form such an informationally complete POVM. For every $\\rho\\in M_2(\\mathbb C)$ with $\\rho\\ge0$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, there are unique $r_x,r_y,r_z\\in\\mathbb R$ such that $\\rho=(I+r_x\\sigma_x+r_y\\sigma_y+r_z\\sigma_z)/2$. Define $p_{j,\\pm}:=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho F_{j,\\pm})$; then $r_j=3(p_{j,+}-p_{j,-})$. Form $G_{(j,s),(k,t)}=\\operatorname{Tr}(F_{j,s}F_{k,t})$ and the canonical duals $D_{j,s}=\\sum_{k,t}(G^+)_{(k,t),(j,s)}F_{k,t}$ as in the preceding finite-frame definition. The linear-inversion procedure returns $X(f)=\\sum_{j,s}f_{j,s}D_{j,s}$ on a valid frequency vector, meaning $f_{j,s}\\ge0$ and $\\sum_{j,s}f_{j,s}=1$. It returns $\\rho$ when $f=p$. Some valid finite frequency vectors yield an output that is not positive semidefinite.",
research/plan-spec.json-235626-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235673-            "def-qsm-dt-channel-definition": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235674-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235675:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-235676-          "statement": "Let $d\\geq1$ and let $\\Phi_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be a family of finite quantum channels indexed by $t\\geq0$, where “finite quantum channel” has the meaning in [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]]. The family obeys the **semigroup law** when $\\Phi_0=\\operatorname{id}_{M_d(\\mathbb C)}$ and $\\Phi_{t+s}=\\Phi_t\\circ\\Phi_s$ for every $s,t\\geq0$.\n\nThe family is **CP-divisible** when for every $0\\leq s\\leq t$ there exists a finite quantum channel $V_{t,s}:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi_t=V_{t,s}\\circ\\Phi_s$. CP-divisibility is this specified factorization property; the definition does not assert that it is a universal meaning of “Markovian”.",
research/plan-spec.json-235677-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235772-            "lem-nrqmx-continuous-simplex-integrals": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235773-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235774:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-235775-          "statement": "Let $d\\geq1$, let $r\\geq0$ be an integer, let $H=H^*\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have units joule-seconds, and let $L_1,\\ldots,L_r\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, with the finite GKSL convention of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. Put $K=-iH/\\hbar-\\frac12\\sum_{a=1}^rL_a^*L_a$ and $R(u)=e^{uK}$ for $u\\geq0$, with the exponential defined by its norm-convergent power series. For a density matrix $\\rho\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, meaning $\\rho=\\rho^*\\geq0$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, define the no-jump branch at time $t\\geq0$ by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)=R(t)$ and its branch state by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)\\rho W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)^*$.\n\nAll time variables $u,t,s_1,\\ldots,s_n$ are measured in seconds. For $n\\geq1$, a jump-history label is $\\mathbf a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\in\\{1,\\ldots,r\\}^n$ and its jump times are seconds $0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t$. Define the corresponding branch operator by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=R(t-s_n)L_{a_n}R(s_n-s_{n-1})\\cdots L_{a_2}R(s_2-s_1)L_{a_1}R(s_1)$ and the unnormalized branch state by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*$. Its branch-weight density is the real-valued scalar function $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=\\operatorname{Tr}(W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*)$. For $n\\geq1$, let $\\overline{\\Delta}_n(t)=\\{(s_1,\\ldots,s_n)\\in\\mathbb R^n:0\\leq s_1\\leq\\cdots\\leq s_n\\leq t\\}$ and $\\Delta_n(t)=\\{(s_1,\\ldots,s_n)\\in\\mathbb R^n:0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t\\}$. Use the same formula for $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)$ on $\\overline{\\Delta}_n(t)$; it is continuous there because $R(u)=e^{uK}$ is continuous for $u\\geq0$. If $V$ is a finite-dimensional real or complex vector space and $F:\\overline{\\Delta}_n(t)\\to V$ has continuous coordinates in a basis, define $\\int_{\\Delta_n(t)}F(\\mathbf s)\\,d\\mathbf s$ as exactly the repeated one-variable Riemann integral over the closed limits shown here $\\int_0^t ds_n\\int_0^{s_n}ds_{n-1}\\cdots\\int_0^{s_2}F(s_1,\\ldots,s_n)\\,ds_1$, integrating each real coordinate; for complex coordinates, integrate their real and imaginary parts separately. This value is independent of the basis because a change of basis is a finite linear combination and scalar Riemann integration is linear. For $V=\\mathbb R$ this defines the scalar integral of the density $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)$; the same convention applies to branch-state integrands with values in $M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and to integrands with values in the finite-dimensional complex vector space of complex-linear maps $M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$. The full recursion is well-defined for these arbitrary continuous integrands by [[lem-nrqmx-continuous-simplex-integrals]]: every inner integral is continuous in all remaining ordered parameters, so the next integral exists. That lemma also proves basis independence, linear-map commutation, the norm bound by the supremum times $t^n/n!$, and positivity for nonnegative real scalar integrands. Set $\\int_a^a f(s)\\,ds=0$ for a degenerate interval; hence every $n\\geq1$ ordered-simplex integral at $t=0$ is zero. Let $\\Delta_0(t)=\\{()\\}$ and define $\\int_{\\Delta_0(t)}F=F(())$; in the expansion, the $n=0$ contribution is the no-jump branch already defined above. For $n\\geq1$, $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n/2}$ and $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n}$; integrating over the $n$ time variables gives a dimensionless weight. If $r=0$, there are no branches with $n\\geq1$. This definition alone does not assert that the branch weights sum to one.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-235776-          "provenance": {
