research/plan-spec.json-235811-            "thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient": "formulation-prerequisite"
research/plan-spec.json-235812-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235813:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235814-          "statement": "Work with finite-dimensional system and environment spaces $H_S=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_E=\\mathbb C^m$, where $d,m\\geq1$, using the ordered tensor product $H_S\\otimes H_E$ with the system factor first. A system or environment state is a positive semidefinite Hermitian matrix of trace one; these state matrices are dimensionless. At the initial time, for every allowed system state $\\rho$, adopt the joint preparation $\\rho\\otimes\\eta$, where the fixed environment state $\\eta$ does not depend on $\\rho$. For each $t\\geq0$, choose a declared unitary $U_t$ on $H_S\\otimes H_E$, meaning $U_t^*U_t=U_tU_t^*=I$, with $U_0=I$. The reduced system dynamics is the map $\\Phi_t(\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(\\rho\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$, where for the fixed product basis the finite partial trace has entries $(\\operatorname{Tr}_E R)_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{j=1}^mR_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,j)}$. This assignment excludes initially correlated joint states. It imposes no one-parameter group, continuity, or semigroup law on $U_t$.\n\nA time-homogeneous finite GKSL model may instead be selected as a separate approximation: choose a fixed finite-dimensional Hamiltonian matrix $H=H^*$ in joules, a constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and finitely many jump matrices $L_a$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, and use $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ for $t\\geq0$ with $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_a\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right)$. Here $\\mathcal L:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ is complex-linear, $[A,B]=AB-BA$, $\\{A,B\\}=AB+BA$, and $A^*$ is conjugate transpose. The exponential means $e^{t\\mathcal L}:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}t^n\\mathcal L^n/n!$, with $\\mathcal L^0=\\operatorname{id}$ and powers given by composition. This series converges absolutely in the operator norm induced by the finite matrix trace norm $\\|X\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}\\sqrt{X^*X}$, as in the finite GKSL semigroup theorem cited below; its hypotheses are exactly the finite Hermitian $H$, positive $\\hbar$, and finite jump family specified here. Each application must state its rates and physical regime. Selecting this approximation is not a consequence of the finite unitary reduction, and this postulate claims no weak-coupling or reservoir limit.\n\nIn both alternatives, the parameter $t$ indexing $U_t$ or $T_t$ denotes elapsed physical time measured in SI seconds. Thus $t\\mathcal L$ in the GKSL exponential is dimensionless, since the adopted finite GKSL generator has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$.",
research/plan-spec.json-235815-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235869-            "thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235870-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235871:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235872-          "statement": "In the finite product-state model, fix any $t\\geq0$ and define the complex-linear map $\\Phi_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ by $\\Phi_t(X)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(X\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$. Then $\\Phi_t$ is a finite quantum channel. The family $t\\mapsto\\Phi_t$ need not obey the semigroup law and need not be CP-divisible. The terms “semigroup law” and “CP-divisible” have the definitions of [[def-nrqmx-finite-channel-families]].",
research/plan-spec.json-235873-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235923-            "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235924-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235925:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235926-          "statement": "For the separately selected qubit GKSL model, let $H=\\hbar\\omega|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ with $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and let $L=\\sqrt{\\gamma}|0\\rangle\\langle1|$ with $\\gamma\\geq0$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, so $L$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$. For any initial density matrix $\\rho(0)$ in the ordered basis $(|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle)$, the solution $\\rho(t)=e^{t\\mathcal L}\\rho(0)$ for $t\\geq0$ satisfies $\\rho_{11}(t)=e^{-\\gamma t}\\rho_{11}(0)$, $\\rho_{00}(t)=\\rho_{00}(0)+(1-e^{-\\gamma t})\\rho_{11}(0)$, $\\rho_{01}(t)=e^{(i\\omega-\\gamma/2)t}\\rho_{01}(0)$, and $\\rho_{10}(t)=e^{(-i\\omega-\\gamma/2)t}\\rho_{10}(0)$.",
research/plan-spec.json-235927-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-235980-            "lem-nrqmx-continuous-simplex-integrals": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-235981-          },
research/plan-spec.json:235982:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-235983-          "statement": "Let $d\\geq1$, let $H=H^*\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have units joule-seconds, and let $L_1,\\ldots,L_r\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be a finite family with units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, where $r\\geq0$. For the selected time-homogeneous finite GKSL model, put $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_{a=1}^r(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\})$. With $K=-iH/\\hbar-\\frac12\\sum_aL_a^*L_a$ and the finite branch operators of [[def-nrqmx-finite-jump-trajectories]], each no-jump operator $R(u)=e^{uK}$ is a contraction, each branch state $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*$ is positive for $\\rho\\geq0$, and for every density matrix $\\rho$ and $t\\geq0$,\n\n$$e^{t\\mathcal L}(\\rho)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\ \\sum_{\\mathbf a\\in\\{1,\\ldots,r\\}^n}\\ \\int_{0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t}W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*\\,ds_1\\cdots ds_n,$$\n\nwhere the $n=0$ term is $R(t)\\rho R(t)^*$ and branches with $n\\geq1$ are absent when $r=0$. The series converges in trace norm, uniformly on each bounded time interval; the sum of all branch weights is one. This is an ensemble representation of the finite GKSL solution, not a unique microscopic trajectory or a claim about a particular detector.",
research/plan-spec.json-235984-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-236040-            "thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-236041-          },
research/plan-spec.json:236042:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-236043-          "statement": "For $H_S=H_E=\\mathbb C^2$, $Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$, $g>0$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, and $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, choose $H_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\hbar g\\,Z\\otimes Z$, $U_t=e^{-itH_{\\mathrm{tot}}/\\hbar}$, and the fixed environment state $|+\\rangle\\langle+|$ with $|+\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ in the displayed environment $Z$ eigenbasis. Here coherence means an off-diagonal matrix entry in the displayed system basis. The reduced map leaves the system populations unchanged and multiplies each coherence by $\\cos(2gt)$. At a positive time $s$ where the multiplier is zero, it erases the distinction between the two $X$ eigenstates; at $2s$ it restores them with their signs exchanged. Thus this finite reduced family is neither a semigroup nor CP-divisible.",
research/plan-spec.json-236044-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-236086-            "thm-exponential-limits-and-range": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-236087-          },
research/plan-spec.json:236088:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-236089-          "statement": "In the normalized jump-branch ensemble for the finite amplitude-damping model, let $\\gamma\\geq0$ be in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and take the initial qubit state $|1\\rangle\\langle1|$. The no-jump survival weight at time $t\\geq0$ is $e^{-\\gamma t}$. If $\\gamma>0$, the ensemble assigns the first-jump time density $\\gamma e^{-\\gamma t}$ and post-jump state $|0\\rangle\\langle0|$; because there is no drive and $L|0\\rangle=0$, there are no later jumps. If $\\gamma=0$, no jump occurs and the survival weight is one. This is a model branch distribution, not a detector observation.",
research/plan-spec.json-236090-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-237343-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237344-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237345:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-237346-          "statement": "Let $d,r\\ge1$ be integers, let $S\\subset\\mathbb Z^d$ be finite with $S=-S$, and let $T_\\delta\\in M_r(\\mathbb C)$ satisfy $T_{-\\delta}=T_\\delta^*$ for each $\\delta\\in S$. Give $\\mathbb C^r$ the inner product $\\langle v,w\\rangle_{\\mathbb C^r}=\\sum_{a=1}^r\\overline{v_a}w_a$, conjugate-linear in the first variable, and set $\\mathcal H=\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^d;\\mathbb C^r)$ with\n\n$$\\langle\\psi,\\phi\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}:=\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z^d}\\langle\\psi_n,\\phi_n\\rangle_{\\mathbb C^r}.$$\n\nLet $\\mathbb T^d_{2\\pi}:=\\mathbb R^d/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$ with normalized Haar measure $(2\\pi)^{-d}dk$ and orientation $dk_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge dk_d$, and set $\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb T^d_{2\\pi};\\mathbb C^r)$ with\n\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{\\mathcal K}:=(2\\pi)^{-d}\\int_{\\mathbb T^d_{2\\pi}}\\sum_{a=1}^r\\overline{f_a(k)}g_a(k)\\,dk.$$\n\nThus the target pairing, like the sequence-space pairing, is conjugate-linear in its first variable. On finitely supported $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H$ define\n\n$$ (H_0\\psi)_n=\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}T_\\delta\\psi_{n-\\delta}. $$\n\nOn finitely supported sequences define\n\n$$ (\\mathcal F_0\\psi)(k)=\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z^d}\\psi_n e^{-ik\\cdot n},\\qquad h(k)=\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}T_\\delta e^{-ik\\cdot\\delta}. $$\n\nAssume Countable Choice for the unitary-extension assertion. The **finite-range lattice Bloch model** is the bounded extension $H$ of $H_0$, together with the unitary extension $\\mathcal F:\\mathcal H\\to\\mathcal K$ of $\\mathcal F_0$ and its finite-matrix fibers $h(k)$. An **isolated band cluster of rank $m$** is a fixed nonempty consecutive block of $m$ eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity, of the Hermitian matrix $h(k)$ whose distance from the complementary eigenvalues is bounded below by a single $\\gamma>0$ for all $k$; if the block is the full spectrum, its projector is $I_r$. The **band projector** $P(k)$ is the orthogonal projection onto the sum of the eigenspaces in that block. For $d=2$, $\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}$ is the oriented Brillouin torus used on this page.\n\nThis definition concerns finite-range hopping on a discrete lattice. It does not identify that model with a continuum periodic Schrödinger operator.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-237347-          "justified_by": [
--
research/plan-spec.json-237396-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237397-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237398:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-237399-          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the data of [[def-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model]]. The finite-range operator $H_0$ extends uniquely to a bounded self-adjoint operator $H$ on $\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z^d;\\mathbb C^r)$, and $\\mathcal F_0$ extends uniquely to a unitary map $\\mathcal F:\\mathcal H\\to L^2(\\mathbb T^d_{2\\pi};\\mathbb C^r)$. For every $k$, $h(k)$ is Hermitian, $\\|h(k)\\|\\le\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}\\|T_\\delta\\|$, and\n\n$$ (\\mathcal F H\\mathcal F^{-1}f)(k)=h(k)f(k) $$\n\nas an equality in $L^2$. The matrix family $h$ is $C^\\infty$. If a nonempty consecutive eigenvalue block is separated from its complement by a uniform gap $\\gamma>0$, its orthogonal spectral projector $P(k)$ is $C^\\infty$ and is locally the Riesz projection\n\n$$ P(k)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\oint_\\Gamma(zI-h(k))^{-1}\\,dz, $$\n\nwhere $\\Gamma$ is a fixed finite union of positively oriented circles separating the selected eigenvalues from the others on the neighborhood in question.",
research/plan-spec.json-237400-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-237451-            "thm-increasing-basis-wedges-form-a-basis": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237452-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237453:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-237454-          "statement": "Let $M$ be a smooth manifold, let $r\\ge1$, and let $P:M\\to M_r(\\mathbb C)$ be a $C^2$ orthogonal projector of constant rank $m$, with $1\\le m\\le r$. Its image bundle is $E_P=\\{(x,v):P(x)v=v\\}$, with the projected connection $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$ of [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]]. The **determinant line bundle** is the complex line bundle\n\n$$\\det E_P:=\\bigwedge^m E_P\\subset M\\times\\bigwedge^m\\mathbb C^r.$$\n\nGive $\\bigwedge^m\\mathbb C^r$ the Hermitian inner product for which $e_{i_1}\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge e_{i_m}$, $i_1<\\cdots<i_m$, is an orthonormal basis, where $(e_1,\\ldots,e_r)$ is the standard basis of $\\mathbb C^r$. The **determinant-line projector** at $x$ is $Q(x):=\\bigwedge^m P(x)$; it is the orthogonal projection onto $\\bigwedge^m\\operatorname{im}P(x)$.",
research/plan-spec.json-237455-          "justified_by": [
--
research/plan-spec.json-237496-            "thm-increasing-basis-wedges-form-a-basis": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237497-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237498:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-237499-          "statement": "For the projector of [[def-nrqmx-determinant-line-projector]], $Q(x)=\\bigwedge^mP(x)$ is a $C^2$ orthogonal projector of rank one. Its range is exactly $\\det E_P(x)=\\bigwedge^m\\operatorname{im}P(x)$, so these ranges form the determinant line bundle.",
research/plan-spec.json-237500-          "justified_by": [],
