research/plan-spec.json-237541-            "thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237542-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237543:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-237544-          "statement": "Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, let $r\\ge1$, and let $P:M\\to M_r(\\mathbb C)$ be a $C^2$ orthogonal projector of constant rank $m$, with $1\\le m\\le r$. Write $E_P=\\{(x,v):P(x)v=v\\}$ and give it the projected connection $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$. For $x\\in M$ and $X,Y\\in T_xM$, define the curvature two-form with values in $M_r(\\mathbb C)$ by\n\n$$F_x(X,Y):=P(x)\\bigl(dP_x(X)dP_x(Y)-dP_x(Y)dP_x(X)\\bigr)P(x).$$\n\nLet $U\\subseteq M$ be open and let $V:U\\to\\mathbb C^{r\\times m}$ be a $C^2$ orthonormal frame, meaning $V(x)^*V(x)=I_m$ and $V(x)V(x)^*=P(x)$ for all $x\\in U$. Put $A=V^*dV$ and $w=v_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge v_m$, where $v_1,\\ldots,v_m$ are the columns of $V$. The induced projected connection on $\\det E_P$ has local connection one-form $a=\\operatorname{tr}A$ in the unit frame $w$, and its scalar curvature is\n\n$$da=\\operatorname{tr}(V^*FV)=\\operatorname{tr}F.$$\n\nThus the curvature of the determinant line is the trace of the original bundle curvature, with the same orientation and sign convention.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-237545-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-237606-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237607-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237608:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-237609-          "statement": "Set $\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}:=\\mathbb R^2/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^2$ with its quotient smooth structure and orientation induced by $dk_1\\wedge dk_2$. Orient the unit sphere $S^2\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ outward and let $\\omega_{S^2}$ be its normalized area form\n\n$$\\omega_{S^2}:=\\frac{1}{4\\pi}(x\\,dy\\wedge dz+y\\,dz\\wedge dx+z\\,dx\\wedge dy)\\big|_{S^2},$$\n\nso that $\\int_{S^2}\\omega_{S^2}=1$. For every smooth map $n:\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}\\to S^2$, its integral degree satisfies\n\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}}n^*\\omega_{S^2}=\\deg(n)\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nHere degree is the integer determined by $n_*[\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}]=\\deg(n)[S^2]$ in integral homology.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-237610-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-237669-            "thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237670-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237671:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-237672-          "statement": "Assume Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $P:\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}\\to M_r(\\mathbb C)$ be a $C^2$ orthogonal projector of constant rank $m\\ge1$; in particular it may be the projector of an isolated band cluster of a finite-range Bloch Hamiltonian. Use the orientation $dk_1\\wedge dk_2$, let $E_P=\\operatorname{im}P$, and let $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$ be the curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$. Then\n\n$$C(P):=\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}}\\operatorname{tr}F=\\left\\langle c_1(\\det E_P),[\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}]\\right\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\n\nFor every map $P:\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}\\times[0,1]\\to M_r(\\mathbb C)$ that is $C^2$ up to the boundary, with each $P_t(k):=P(k,t)$ an orthogonal projector of the same rank $m$, $C(P_t)$ is independent of $t$. A finite-range Bloch deformation covered by this clause is specified by an open interval $J\\supset[0,1]$, a finite symmetric $S\\subset\\mathbb Z^2$, and $C^2$ maps $T_\\delta:J\\to M_r(\\mathbb C)$ with $T_{-\\delta}(t)=T_\\delta(t)^*$, through\n\n$$h(k,t):=\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}T_\\delta(t)e^{-ik\\cdot\\delta}.$$\n\nFor one fixed consecutive eigenvalue block of size $m$, if the distance to its complementary spectrum is bounded below by one $\\gamma>0$ for all $(k,t)\\in\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}\\times[0,1]$, then its orthogonal spectral projector $P(k,t)$ is jointly $C^2$, so the same invariance applies. If $E_P$ has a global $C^2$ periodic orthonormal frame, then $C(P)=0$.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-237673-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-237732-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237733-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237734:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-237735-          "statement": "Assume Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let a two-orbital finite-range Bloch Hamiltonian on the oriented torus $\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}$ have the form\n\n$$h(k)=d_0(k)I+d_1(k)\\sigma_1+d_2(k)\\sigma_2+d_3(k)\\sigma_3,$$\n\nwhere $d_0,d_1,d_2,d_3$ are real smooth periodic functions, $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$ are the Pauli matrices, and $d=(d_1,d_2,d_3)$ is nowhere zero. Define $n=d/|d|$. The upper and lower band projectors are $P_+=(I+n\\cdot\\sigma)/2$ and $P_-=(I-n\\cdot\\sigma)/2$. With the Chern convention $C(P)=(i/2\\pi)\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}\\operatorname{tr}(P(dP\\wedge dP)P)$ and orientation $dk_1\\wedge dk_2$,\n\n$$C(P_+)=-\\deg(n)=-\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}n\\cdot(\\partial_{k_1}n\\times\\partial_{k_2}n)\\,dk_1\\,dk_2,\\qquad C(P_-)=+\\deg(n)=+\\frac{1}{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb T^2}n\\cdot(\\partial_{k_1}n\\times\\partial_{k_2}n)\\,dk_1\\,dk_2.$$",
research/plan-spec.json-237736-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-237793-            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-237794-          },
research/plan-spec.json:237795:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-237796-          "statement": "Assume Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $\\mathbf a_1,\\mathbf a_2,\\mathbf a_3\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be a basis of direct-lattice vectors, each measured in metres, and set $\\Lambda=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\mathbb Z\\mathbf a_j$ and $\\Lambda^*:=\\{\\mathbf G\\in\\mathbb R^3:\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf R\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z\\text{ for all }\\mathbf R\\in\\Lambda\\}$, whose vectors have units of inverse metres. The reciprocal-space torus is $\\mathbb R^3/\\Lambda^*$; let $U\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be an open set on which the quotient map is injective, giving local wave-vector coordinates. For $\\delta\\in\\mathbb Z^3$ put $\\mathbf R_\\delta=\\sum_j\\delta_j\\mathbf a_j$ and $\\theta_j(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf a_j\\pmod{2\\pi}$. Fix finite $S\\subset\\mathbb Z^3$ with $S=-S$ and hopping matrices $T_\\delta\\in M_r(\\mathbb C)$ satisfying $T_{-\\delta}=T_\\delta^*$; assign the entries of $T_\\delta$ units of joules. The finite-range physical fiber is\n\n$$h(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}T_\\delta e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf R_\\delta}=\\sum_{\\delta\\in S}T_\\delta e^{-i\\theta(\\mathbf k)\\cdot\\delta},$$\n\nwhich is the fiber in [[def-nrqmx-bloch-fiber-model]] written in physical reciprocal coordinates. Its entries and its eigenvalues have energy units (joules). Let $\\varepsilon:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be a $C^2$ simple eigenvalue of $h(\\mathbf k)$ and, after shrinking $U$ if needed, assume its distance from the other eigenvalues is at least some $\\gamma>0$ throughout $U$. Choose a $C^2$ local normalized eigenvector $u:U\\to\\mathbb C^r$ with $h(\\mathbf k)u(\\mathbf k)=\\varepsilon(\\mathbf k)u(\\mathbf k)$ and $u(\\mathbf k)^*u(\\mathbf k)=1$.\n\nLet $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^3$ denote a position coordinate measured in metres, $\\mathbf k\\in U$ the physical crystal wave vector measured in inverse metres (radians are dimensionless), $q$ a constant signed charge in coulombs, $\\mathbf E_{\\rm ext}\\in\\mathbb R^3$ a constant electric field in volts per metre, and $\\hbar>0$ the reduced Planck constant in joule seconds. Under the adopted single-band effective description, take\n\n$$L=\\hbar\\mathbf k\\cdot\\dot{\\mathbf x}+i\\hbar\\,u(\\mathbf k)^*\\nabla_{\\mathbf k}u(\\mathbf k)\\cdot\\dot{\\mathbf k}-\\varepsilon(\\mathbf k)+q\\mathbf E_{\\rm ext}\\cdot\\mathbf x.$$\n\nHere each term of $L$ has units of joules. Define the real Berry connection $\\mathcal A_j(\\mathbf k):=i u(\\mathbf k)^*\\partial_{k_j}u(\\mathbf k)$, whose components have units of metres, and the Berry-curvature vector $\\boldsymbol\\Omega(\\mathbf k):=\\nabla_{\\mathbf k}\\times\\mathcal A(\\mathbf k)$, whose components have units of square metres. For time $t$ measured in seconds, a compact interval $I=[t_0,t_1]$ with $t_0<t_1$, and curves $\\mathbf x,\\mathbf k\\in C^2(I;\\mathbb R^3)$ with $\\mathbf k(t)\\in U$, define the action $\\mathscr S[\\mathbf x,\\mathbf k]=\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}L(\\mathbf x(t),\\mathbf k(t),\\dot{\\mathbf x}(t),\\dot{\\mathbf k}(t))\\,dt$. A curve is **stationary** if $\\left.\\frac{d}{ds}\\right|_{s=0}\\mathscr S[\\mathbf x_s,\\mathbf k_s]=0$ for every jointly $C^2$ variation $(s,t)\\mapsto(\\mathbf x_s(t),\\mathbf k_s(t))$, $|s|<\\eta$, with $(\\mathbf x_0,\\mathbf k_0)=(\\mathbf x,\\mathbf k)$, fixed endpoints $\\mathbf x_s(t_i)=\\mathbf x(t_i)$ and $\\mathbf k_s(t_i)=\\mathbf k(t_i)$ for $i=0,1$, and $\\mathbf k_s(t)\\in U$. Every such $C^2$ stationary curve of this adopted effective action satisfies\n\n$$\\hbar\\dot{\\mathbf k}=q\\mathbf E_{\\rm ext},\\qquad \\dot{\\mathbf x}=\\frac{1}{\\hbar}\\nabla_{\\mathbf k}\\varepsilon-\\dot{\\mathbf k}\\times\\boldsymbol\\Omega.$$\n\nThe action has units of joule seconds. The equations are invariant under a local phase change of $u$. The words “wave packet” and “slow-force” name only the physical motivation and regime label of this adopted effective model; no wave-packet object is used in the deduction, and no approximation-validity or error-bound claim about underlying quantum dynamics is made. For the two-band specialization, take $r=2$. Let $\\mathbf b_1,\\mathbf b_2,\\mathbf b_3$ be the reciprocal basis, defined by $\\mathbf b_j\\cdot\\mathbf a_\\ell=2\\pi\\delta_{j\\ell}$, so $\\Lambda^*=\\sum_j\\mathbb Z\\mathbf b_j$. Fix $\\tau\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ and the closed reciprocal two-torus\n\n$$\\Sigma_\\tau=\\{[\\mathbf k]\\in\\mathbb R^3/\\Lambda^*: \\theta_3(\\mathbf k)=\\tau\\},\\qquad \\kappa_\\tau(\\theta_1,\\theta_2)=\\left[\\frac{\\theta_1\\mathbf b_1+\\theta_2\\mathbf b_2+\\tau\\mathbf b_3}{2\\pi}\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\kappa_\\tau:\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}\\to\\Sigma_\\tau$ is a diffeomorphism and fixes the orientation by $d\\theta_1\\wedge d\\theta_2$. Suppose the restriction has the two-band form $h\\circ\\kappa_\\tau=d_0I+d\\cdot\\sigma$ of [[thm-nrqmx-two-band-degree-formula]], with $d$ nowhere zero on this entire torus. Put $n=d/|d|$ and $P_s=(I+s n\\cdot\\sigma)/2$, $s\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$; the chosen local eigenvector belongs to that band wherever its chart meets the slice. Interpret $P_s$ also as the corresponding globally defined projector on $\\Sigma_\\tau$. Define the global real two-form\n\n$$\\mathcal B_s:=i\\,\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(P_s(dP_s\\wedge dP_s)P_s\\bigr).$$\n\nOn every local normalized eigenvector chart this is $d\\mathcal A$ for $\\mathcal A=i u^*du$; it does not require a global eigenvector. In physical reciprocal coordinates, the coordinate conversion on every such chart is\n\n$$\\kappa_\\tau^*\\mathcal B_s=\\frac{\\boldsymbol\\Omega(\\kappa_\\tau(\\theta))\\cdot(\\mathbf b_1\\times\\mathbf b_2)}{(2\\pi)^2}\\,d\\theta_1\\wedge d\\theta_2.$$\n\nThe coefficient is dimensionless, since $\\boldsymbol\\Omega$ has units of square metres and each reciprocal basis vector has inverse-metre units. With this orientation and the same band sign,\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{\\Sigma_\\tau}\\mathcal B_s=\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb T^2_{2\\pi}}\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(P_s(dP_s\\wedge dP_s)P_s\\bigr)=C(P_s)=-s\\deg(n).$$\n\nThe integrals are over the whole closed torus, and the exterior derivatives in the second integral are in the dimensionless $\\theta$ coordinates. This is the Chern convention of [[thm-nrqmx-two-band-degree-formula]].\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-237797-          "justified_by": [],
