research/plan-spec.json-238167-          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $d\\ge1$, $\\hbar>0$, let $H=H^*$ on $\\mathbb C^d$, and let $P=P^*=P^2$ be an orthogonal projection. Use the operator norm induced by the standard inner product. Put $P^\\perp=I_d-P$, $H_D=PHP+P^\\perp HP^\\perp$, and $V=H-H_D$. With finite matrix exponentials as defined on this page, for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\left\\|e^{-itH/\\hbar}-e^{-itH_D/\\hbar}\\right\\|\\le\\frac{|t|}{\\hbar}\\|V\\|,\\qquad \\left\\|P^\\perp e^{-itH/\\hbar}P\\right\\|\\le\\frac{|t|}{\\hbar}\\|V\\|.$$\nThe second inequality bounds amplitude leakage from the retained subspace $\\operatorname{ran}P$; it asserts no convergence for an unbounded coupling.",
research/plan-spec.json-238168-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-238240-            "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-238241-          },
research/plan-spec.json:238242:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-238243-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $d\\ge1$, $T>0$, $\\omega=2\\pi/T$, $\\hbar>0$, and let $H:\\mathbb R\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be $C^1$, $T$-periodic, and Hermitian at every time. On $\\mathscr H_T=L^2((0,T),dt;\\mathbb C^d)$ define\n$$D(K)=\\{f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_d): f_a\\in H^1(0,T)\\text{ for every }a,\\ f(0)=f(T)\\},\\qquad (Kf)(t)=H(t)f(t)-i\\hbar f'(t).$$\nHere each $H^1(0,T)$ function is represented by its unique absolutely continuous representative, so endpoint values are its traces, and $f'$ is its componentwise weak derivative. Then $K$ is self-adjoint on exactly $D(K)$. Choose $v_j$ and $\\varepsilon_j$ from the finite Floquet normal form, so $H_Fv_j=\\varepsilon_jv_j$ and $P(t)$ is its periodic unitary. The functions\n$$\\phi_{jn}(t)=T^{-1/2}e^{in\\omega t}P(t)v_j\\qquad(1\\le j\\le d,\\ n\\in\\mathbb Z)$$\nform an orthonormal basis of $\\mathscr H_T$ and satisfy $K\\phi_{jn}=(\\varepsilon_j+n\\hbar\\omega)\\phi_{jn}$.",
research/plan-spec.json-238244-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-238292-            "thm-nrqmx-floquet-sambe-domain": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-238293-          },
research/plan-spec.json:238294:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-238295-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Fix integers $d\\ge1$, $b,N\\ge0$, and real numbers $\\hbar>0$, $\\omega>0$, and $\\tau>0$. On every coordinate space $\\mathbb C^r$ used below, use the standard inner product $\\langle x,y\\rangle:=\\sum_{a=1}^{r}x_a\\overline{y_a}$, linear in the first argument, and induced norm $\\|x\\|=(\\sum_{a=1}^{r}|x_a|^2)^{1/2}$, as fixed in the finite-dimensional matrix exponential definition; matrix adjoints are conjugate transposes. Let $H_k\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ for $-b\\le k\\le b$ satisfy $H_{-k}=H_k^*$; set $H_k=0$ when $|k|>b$. Thus $H(t)=\\sum_{k=-b}^{b}H_ke^{ik\\omega t}$ is a finite Fourier polynomial with period $T=2\\pi/\\omega$. Put $q=(2N+1)d$ and use this coordinate inner product and norm on $\\mathbb C^q$. Let $S_\\tau$ be a caller-supplied total classical routine on $\\{M\\in M_q(\\mathbb C):M=M^*\\}$ whose declared interface, on input $M$, returns real numbers $\\widetilde\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\widetilde\\lambda_q$, an orthonormal basis $x_1,\\ldots,x_q$ for this inner product, and residuals $r_j=\\|Mx_j-\\widetilde\\lambda_jx_j\\|\\le\\tau$ for $1\\le j\\le q$. This algorithm returns that finite record unchanged and does not independently certify its residual claims.\n\nThe algorithm takes $(d,b,N,\\hbar,\\omega,(H_k)_{|k|\\le b},\\tau,S_\\tau)$ and returns a matrix $K_N$ and the eigensolver's output record:\n\n1. Order the Fourier indices as $-N,-N+1,\\ldots,N$ and allocate a $(2N+1)d$ by $(2N+1)d$ block matrix.\n\n2. For each ordered pair $n,m\\in\\{-N,\\ldots,N\\}$, assign the $d$ by $d$ block\n$$ (K_N)_{nm}:=H_{n-m}+n\\hbar\\omega\\,\\delta_{nm}I_d,$$\nwhere $\\delta_{nm}=1$ if $n=m$ and $0$ otherwise.\n\n3. Set $R:=S_\\tau(K_N)$ by one call, then return $(K_N,R)$.",
research/plan-spec.json-238296-          "justified_by": [
--
research/plan-spec.json-238343-            "thm-nrqm-intervals-twisted-periodic-momentum-domain": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-238344-          },
research/plan-spec.json:238345:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-238346-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $d\\ge1$ and $b,N\\ge0$ be integers, let $\\hbar>0$ and $\\omega>0$ be real, and set $T=2\\pi/\\omega$. Let $H_k\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ for $|k|\\le b$ satisfy $H_{-k}=H_k^*$, set $H_k=0$ for $|k|>b$, and put $H(t)=\\sum_{|k|\\le b}H_ke^{ik\\omega t}$. Set $q=(2N+1)d$. Fix $\\tau>0$ and a total routine $S_\\tau$ on $\\{M\\in M_q(\\mathbb C):M=M^*\\}$ with the standard inner product on $\\mathbb C^q$, that for each input $M$ returns real numbers $\\widetilde\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\widetilde\\lambda_q$, an orthonormal basis $x_1,\\ldots,x_q$, and residuals $r_j=\\|Mx_j-\\widetilde\\lambda_jx_j\\|\\le\\tau$ as in the algorithm Definition. Let $\\mathscr H_T=L^2((0,T),dt;\\mathbb C^d)$ with its componentwise $L^2$ inner product, and let $Kf=H(t)f-i\\hbar f'(t)$ on the domain of vectors whose components lie in $H^1(0,T)$ and have equal endpoint traces. Let $\\mathcal S_N$ be the span of $T^{-1/2}e^{in\\omega t}v$ with $-N\\le n\\le N$ and $v\\in\\mathbb C^d$, and let $\\Pi_N$ be its orthogonal projection. The matrix with blocks $(K_N)_{nm}=H_{n-m}+n\\hbar\\omega\\delta_{nm}I_d$ is exactly the matrix of $\\Pi_NK|_{\\mathcal S_N}$ in the ordered Fourier basis and is Hermitian. The block-assembly portion of the algorithm terminates after exactly $(2N+1)^2$ block assignments. The complete procedure is well-defined and terminates, because it then calls $S_\\tau$ once on the Hermitian matrix $K_N$. This is an exact compression result only; it asserts no convergence or spectral-accuracy guarantee for finite-section eigenvalues and no residual guarantee beyond the stipulated solver interface.",
research/plan-spec.json-238347-          "justified_by": [],
--
research/plan-spec.json-238418-            "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-238419-          },
research/plan-spec.json:238420:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-238421-          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. On $\\mathbb C^2$ let $\\sigma_z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$, choose real $E_0,A$, and choose $\\hbar>0$, $\\omega>0$, and $T=2\\pi/\\omega$. For $H(t)=(E_0+A\\cos(\\omega t))\\sigma_z$, the unique propagator from time $0$ is\n$$U(t,0)=\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\left(E_0t+\\frac{A}{\\omega}\\sin(\\omega t)\\right)\\sigma_z\\right].$$\nOne Floquet normal form is $H_F=E_0\\sigma_z$ and $P(t)=\\exp[-iA\\sin(\\omega t)\\sigma_z/(\\hbar\\omega)]$.",
research/plan-spec.json-238422-          "justified_by": [],
