research/plan-spec.json-238919-          "statement": "Specialize the finite-mode assignment to $M=1$, identify the coordinate vector $(z)\\in\\mathbb C^1$ with its single component $z\\in\\mathbb C$, and write $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C)$ for this one-mode Fock space. Write $|n\\rangle$ for the occupation vector with $n$ quanta in its unique mode. Let $\\rho$ be a density operator (a positive trace-class operator of trace one) on $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C)$. Let $\\Pi_n:=|n\\rangle\\langle n|$ be the ideal number-count PVM, with probabilities $p_n:=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho\\Pi_n)$. Suppose $\\bar n:=\\sum_{n\\ge0}np_n$ is finite and strictly positive, and $m_2:=\\sum_{n\\ge0}n(n-1)p_n$ is finite. Define the model's equal-time second factorial statistic by $g^{(2)}(0;\\rho):=m_2/\\bar n^2$. On finite-particle number vectors the one-mode maps satisfy $a|0\\rangle=0$, $a|n\\rangle=\\sqrt n|n-1\\rangle$ for $n\\ge1$, and $a^*|n\\rangle=\\sqrt{n+1}|n+1\\rangle$ for $n\\ge0$. Normal ordering here means placing creation maps to the left of annihilation maps; these actions give $a^*a^*aa|n\\rangle=n(n-1)|n\\rangle$ for $n\\ge0$. Thus $m_2$ is the corresponding number-basis factorial moment, defined here directly by its probability sum rather than by an unqualified expectation of an unbounded operator product. This uses only the ideal counting model; it asserts no time-resolved photocurrent law, empirical detection, or detector realization.\n\n",
