research/plan-spec.json:239015:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-239016-          "statement": "Assume the finite-mode assignment with two modes and its ideal number PVM, with AC and $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ as required by the implementer and completed Fock/tensor suppliers. Write $\\mathcal F^{(2)}=\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^2)$ in the ordered mode basis $(1,0),(0,1)$ and $\\mathcal F=\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C)$. The mode-factorization map is the unitary $\\mathcal J:\\mathcal F^{(2)}\\to\\mathcal F\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F$ determined by $\\mathcal J|m,n\\rangle=|m\\rangle\\otimes|n\\rangle$. Here $\\widehat\\otimes$ is the completed Hilbert tensor product, and the one-mode occupation vectors and their products use the same vacuum and mode order. Its unitary extension is proved below. Let $t,r\\in\\mathbb C$ be dimensionless amplitudes with $|t|^2+|r|^2=1$, and fix the complete unitary mode matrix $C=\\begin{pmatrix}t&-\\overline r\\\\r&\\overline t\\end{pmatrix}$. Use the state-action convention that the passive unitary $U_C$ satisfies $U_C\\Omega=\\Omega$, $U_Ca_1^*U_C^*=t a_1^*+r a_2^*$, and $U_Ca_2^*U_C^*=-\\overline r\\,a_1^*+\\overline t\\,a_2^*$ on the finite-particle core. Put $\\eta:=|t|^2$. For $n\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and $0\\le k\\le n$, write $\\binom nk:=n!/(k!(n-k)!)$. For input $|n,0\\rangle$ and $0<\\eta<1$, the joint output count law is\n$$\\mathbb P(k,\\ell)=\\begin{cases}\\binom nk\\eta^k(1-\\eta)^{n-k},&\\ell=n-k,\\ 0\\le k\\le n,\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$\nAt $\\eta=0$ and $\\eta=1$ the joint laws are respectively point masses at $(0,n)$ and $(n,0)$. Thus the first output count is binomial with parameters $(n,\\eta)$. For a dimensionless coherent input $|\\alpha,0\\rangle$ with $|\\alpha\\rangle=e^{-|\\alpha|^2/2}\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\alpha^n|n\\rangle/\\sqrt{n!}$, define $|\\alpha,0\\rangle=\\mathcal J^*(|\\alpha\\rangle\\otimes|0\\rangle)$, equivalently $e^{-|\\alpha|^2/2}\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\alpha^n|n,0\\rangle/\\sqrt{n!}$. The output state under this fixed mode factorization is $\\mathcal J U_C|\\alpha,0\\rangle=|t\\alpha\\rangle\\otimes|r\\alpha\\rangle$; on $\\mathcal F^{(2)}$ it is $\\mathcal J^*(|t\\alpha\\rangle\\otimes|r\\alpha\\rangle)$.\n\n",
