research/plan-spec.json-239069-          "statement": "Assume AC and $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the assigned finite-mode model, one input mode and a second mode prepared independently in vacuum $|0\\rangle\\langle0|$. Write $\\mathcal F:=\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C)$ for each one-mode Fock space and $\\mathcal F^{(2)}:=\\mathcal F_s(\\mathbb C^2)$ for the two-mode Fock space with ordered coordinate basis $e_1=(1,0),e_2=(0,1)$. In the completed product $\\mathcal F\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F$, define $\\mathcal I(|m\\rangle\\otimes|n\\rangle):=|m,n\\rangle$ on occupation vectors, where $|m,n\\rangle$ is the two-mode occupation basis in that ordered mode basis. The product-basis result and finite-mode Fock occupation-basis result listed in Statement references make $\\mathcal I$ extend uniquely to a unitary $\\mathcal I:\\mathcal F\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal F^{(2)}$. For $0\\le\\eta\\le1$, define $C_\\eta:=\\begin{pmatrix}\\sqrt\\eta&-\\sqrt{1-\\eta}\\\\\\sqrt{1-\\eta}&\\sqrt\\eta\\end{pmatrix}$ and let $U_\\eta:=U_{C_\\eta}$ be the two-mode beam-splitter unitary specified by the beam-splitter result in Statement references on $\\mathcal F^{(2)}$. Transport this unitary to the product space as $\\widetilde U_\\eta:=\\mathcal I^*U_\\eta\\mathcal I$. Define the vacuum insertion isometry $V_0:\\mathcal F\\to\\mathcal F\\widehat\\otimes\\mathcal F$ by $V_0\\psi=\\psi\\otimes|0\\rangle$. For every trace-class operator $\\rho$ on $\\mathcal F$, the vacuum product operator $\\rho\\otimes|0\\rangle\\langle0|$ means $V_0\\rho V_0^*$, a trace-class operator by the trace-class ideal and tensor-map suppliers. Define\n$$\\Phi_\\eta(\\rho):=\\operatorname{Tr}_2\\!\\left(\\widetilde U_\\eta(\\rho\\otimes|0\\rangle\\langle0|)\\widetilde U_\\eta^*\\right),$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Tr}_2$ is the trace-class partial trace over the second one-mode factor. Here CPTP means complex-linear, trace-preserving, and positive after every finite-dimensional ancilla amplification on trace-class operators. The map $\\Phi_\\eta$ is CPTP. For $0<\\eta<1$ and an input number state $|n\\rangle$, its output state is\n$$\\Phi_\\eta(|n\\rangle\\langle n|)=\\sum_{k=0}^n\\binom nk\\eta^k(1-\\eta)^{n-k}|k\\rangle\\langle k|,$$\nso ideal number detection has the binomial-thinning law. At $\\eta=0$ this number input is mapped to vacuum, and at $\\eta=1$ it is unchanged. For a coherent input $|\\alpha\\rangle$, its output is $|\\sqrt\\eta\\,\\alpha\\rangle\\langle\\sqrt\\eta\\,\\alpha|$ for every $0\\le\\eta\\le1$. On arbitrary input states, $\\Phi_0$ maps to vacuum and $\\Phi_1$ is the identity.\n\n",
