research/plan-spec.json-241387-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $d\\ge1$, let $K\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be symmetric positive definite, and let $j\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Let $\\gamma_{K^{-1}}=N_d(0,K^{-1})$, where covariance is the matrix $\\mathbb E[XX^T]$. Then\n$$\\frac{\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\exp(-\\tfrac12x^TKx+j^Tx)\\,dx} {\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\exp(-\\tfrac12x^TKx)\\,dx} =\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}e^{j^Tx}\\,d\\gamma_{K^{-1}}(x) =\\exp\\!\\left(\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j\\right).$$\nEquivalently, in the finite real Gaussian regulator with normalized weight $e^{-S}$ and $\\hbar=1$, completing the source shift $\\chi=K^{-1}j+\\eta$ gives\n$$\\tfrac12\\chi^TK\\chi-j^T\\chi=\\tfrac12\\eta^TK\\eta-\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j.$$\nUse $\\hbar=1$ and coordinate/source units for which $x^TKx$ and $j^Tx$ are dimensionless. Both integrals are finite; their common Gaussian normalization cancels in the ratio. No infinite-dimensional Lebesgue measure or determinant is asserted.",
research/plan-spec.json-241388-          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Proof technique:** direct Gaussian density and translation.\n\n**Given:** The objects and conventions in the Statement.\n\n1.1 By [F1]–[F2], $\\gamma_{K^{-1}}$ has Lebesgue density $c_K\\exp(-\\tfrac12x^TKx)$, where $c_K=(2\\pi)^{-d/2}\\det(K^{-1})^{-1/2}>0$. The expectation in the Statement is therefore the ratio of the displayed Lebesgue integrals, provided the numerator is finite. [A1, F1, F2, given]\n\n1.2 Since $K$ is invertible, direct expansion gives\n$$\\tfrac12x^TKx-j^Tx=\\tfrac12(x-K^{-1}j)^TK(x-K^{-1}j)-\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j.$$\nThe translation $x\\mapsto x-K^{-1}j$ preserves Lebesgue measure by [F3]–[F5], so the numerator is the denominator multiplied by $\\exp(\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j)$. The denominator is finite and positive because it is the normalizing integral of the probability density in [F2]; hence the numerator is also finite, and division proves both equalities. Expanding the same square proves the action identity. [F2, F3, F4, F5, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
--
research/plan-spec.json-241442-            "lem-qftx-finite-heavy-gaussian-square": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-241443-          },
research/plan-spec.json:241444:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-241445-          "statement": "Assume AC. Let $H$ be a nonzero complex Hilbert space, let $A:D(A)\\subseteq H\\to H$ be densely defined, self-adjoint and nonnegative, and let $M>0$. Set $K=M^2I+A$ on $D(A)$ and let $E_A$ be the spectral measure of $A$. Define $f_M(a)=(M^2+a)^{-1}$ for $a\\ge0$. The bounded spectral operator $f_M(A)$ is the inverse $K^{-1}$ and satisfies $\\|K^{-1}\\|\\le M^{-2}$. For $J\\in H$ define the matched quadratic effective-action contribution\n$$\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{eff}}(J):=-\\tfrac12\\langle J,K^{-1}J\\rangle.$$\nThe inner product is the complex Hilbert pairing, linear in its first variable; the displayed quadratic form is real and nonpositive. In natural units $\\hbar=c=1$, $A$ and $K$ have mass-squared units; normalize the source/pairing so $\\langle J,K^{-1}J\\rangle$ is dimensionless, hence $\\|J\\|^2$ has mass-squared units. In a finite real regulator, integration of $\\exp[-\\tfrac12\\chi^TK\\chi+j^T\\chi]$ divided by the zero-source Gaussian integral yields $\\exp[\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j]$, so the corresponding effective action changes by $-\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j$. Since $\\log$ is the inverse of $\\exp$ on positive reals, taking $-\\log$ of that normalized integral gives this effective-action contribution. Determinants and Lebesgue integration are finite-regulator quantities only; this definition supplies no infinite-dimensional Gaussian measure or determinant.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-241446-          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Proof technique:** direct spectral calculus with a finite-regulator Gaussian identity.\n\n**Given:** The objects and hypotheses in the Definition.\n\n1.1 The actual proof of [F1] uses Cauchy–Schwarz for its resolvent lower bound and closedness of $A$ for its resolvent graph argument. [F2] supplies the former. By [A1]–[A2] countable choice is available, so [F3] applies and self-adjointness gives $A=A^*$, hence $A$ is closed. This records the actual inputs of [F1]'s proof; the carrier's complete conditional proof was inspected. [A1, A2, F1, F2, F3]\n\n1.2 Let $g_M(a)=(M^2+a)^{-1}$. By [F1], $g_M(A)$ is bounded with norm at most $M^{-2}$. Moreover, $\\int a^2g_M(a)^2\\,d\\langle E_A(a)u,u\\rangle\\le\\|u\\|^2$ for every $u\\in H$, so the domain formula for $A$ puts $g_M(A)u$ in $D(A)$. For any $u\\in H$, truncate the spectral integrals to $[0,n]$; on each truncation the bounded Borel calculus is multiplicative, and the domain criterion just verified gives the graph limit $A g_M(A)u=\\int a g_M(a)\\,dE_A(a)u$. Hence $(M^2I+A)g_M(A)u=\\int(M^2+a)g_M(a)\\,dE_A(a)u=u$. For $v\\in D(A)$, the same truncation and scalar multiplication give $g_M(A)(M^2I+A)v=\\int g_M(a)(M^2+a)\\,dE_A(a)v=v$. Thus $K^{-1}=g_M(A)$ and $\\|K^{-1}\\|\\le M^{-2}$. Finally, the positive scalar multiplier gives $\\langle J,K^{-1}J\\rangle=\\int g_M(a)\\,d\\langle E_A(a)J,J\\rangle\\ge0$, so the quadratic form is real. [A3, A4, F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.1 In a finite real regulator with symmetric positive-definite matrix $K$, [F4] gives the normalized source integral $\\exp(\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j)$. Taking its negative logarithm using [F5] gives the effective-action shift $-\\tfrac12j^TK^{-1}j$. No determinant or Lebesgue integral beyond that finite regulator enters the definition. [F4, F5, given, algebra] ∎",
--
