research/plan-spec.json-241500-          "statement": "Assume the hypotheses and notation of the positive heavy-mode Gaussian matching data. Let $E\\ge0$ be an energy and let $P_E:=E_A([0,E^2])$ be the spectral projection of $A$. For $J=P_EJ\\in H$ and an integer $N\\ge0$, define\n$$S_NJ:=M^{-2}\\sum_{r=0}^{N}(-A/M^2)^rJ,$$\nwhich is well-defined since $J$ has bounded spectral support. Put $R_NJ:=K^{-1}J-S_NJ$. Then\n$$\\|R_NJ\\|\\le M^{-2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1}\\|J\\|,$$\nand\n$$\\left|\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{eff}}(J)+\\tfrac12\\langle J,S_NJ\\rangle\\right| =\\tfrac12|\\langle J,R_NJ\\rangle| \\le \\frac{\\|J\\|^2}{2M^2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1}.$$\nUse natural units $\\hbar=c=1$: $A$ has units of mass squared, $E,M$ have mass units, and $E^2/M^2$ is dimensionless. Under the source normalization of the positive heavy-mode Gaussian matching data, $\\|J\\|^2/M^2$ and the action remainder are dimensionless. These are exact conditional bounds for every $E\\ge0$; the truncation is small/useful when $E<M$. No claim is made for unrestricted sources or arbitrary interacting matching.\n\n",
research/plan-spec.json-241501-          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Proof technique:** direct scalar remainder and the spectral functional calculus.\n\n**Given:** The hypotheses and notation in the Statement.\n\n1.1 For every $a\\ge0$, putting $x=a/M^2$ and multiplying by $1+x$ gives\n$$\\frac1{M^2+a}=M^{-2}\\sum_{r=0}^{N}(-a/M^2)^r+\nM^{-2}\\frac{(-a/M^2)^{N+1}}{1+a/M^2}.$$\nThis finite geometric identity also holds at $a=0$ and for $N=0$. [given, algebra]\n\n1.2 On $J=P_EJ$, the spectral variable satisfies $0\\le a\\le E^2$. Therefore every polynomial term $A^rJ$ is defined, and the remainder multiplier satisfies\n$$\\left|M^{-2}\\frac{(-a/M^2)^{N+1}}{1+a/M^2}\\right|\n\\le M^{-2}(E^2/M^2)^{N+1},$$\nbecause $1+a/M^2\\ge1$. Extend this bounded multiplier by zero outside $[0,E^2]$ and apply [F1]. With [F2] this identifies the inverse kernel and proves the vector identity defining $R_NJ$ and its norm bound. [A1, A2, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.1 By [F2], the action remainder equals $\\tfrac12|\\langle J,R_NJ\\rangle|$. Cauchy–Schwarz [F3] and step 1.2 give the claimed bound. The factor $(E^2/M^2)^{N+1}$ decreases to zero only when $E<M$; for $E\\ge M$ the same finite-order bound remains true but need not be small. [F2, F3, step 1.2, algebra] ∎",
--
