research/plan-spec.json-241548-          "statement": "Assume AC and let $d\\ge1$. For a real symmetric positive-semidefinite covariance matrix $C$, write $\\gamma_C=N_d(0,C)$, allowing singular $C$. For a nonnegative Borel function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty]$, define the normalized shell transform\n$$T_Cf(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f(x+z)\\,d\\gamma_C(z)\\in[0,\\infty].$$\nThis is a Borel function of $x$. If $V:\\mathbb R^d\\to[0,\\infty)$ is finite-valued and Borel, define\n$$V_C(x):=-\\log\\bigl(T_C(e^{-V})(x)\\bigr).$$\nThen $V_C$ is a finite nonnegative Borel function, and $0<T_C(e^{-V})(x)\\le1$ for every $x$. The covariance convention is $\\mathbb E[ZZ^T]=C$. The coordinates $x,z$ use the same finite-regulator field-coordinate convention, so $C$ has the square units of those coordinates. The scalar $V(x)$ is dimensionless in $e^{-V(x)}$; if a physical interpretation identifies $V$ with an action, it is normalized as $S/\\hbar$. The measures $\\gamma_C$ are normalized probabilities, with no determinant/vacuum factor in $T_C$. The transform is defined at finite regulator only.",
research/plan-spec.json-241549-          "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n**Proof technique:** direct measurability and positivity check.\n\n**Given:** The objects and hypotheses in the Definition.\n\n1.1 The map $(x,z)\\mapsto f(x+z)$ is Borel because addition is continuous and $f$ is Borel. Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R^d$ is sigma-finite because $\\mathbb R^d=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}[-n,n]^d$ and each cube has finite measure; $\\gamma_C$ is a probability by [F1]. Thus [F2] makes $x\\mapsto T_Cf(x)$ Borel, including when the integral is infinite. [A1, F1, F2, given]\n\n1.2 For finite nonnegative Borel $V$, $g_x(z):=e^{-V(x+z)}$ is Borel and satisfies $0<g_x(z)\\le1$. Its integral against the probability $\\gamma_C$ is at most one. It is strictly positive: if the integral were zero, each set $\\{z:g_x(z)\\ge1/n\\}$ would have measure zero, while their countable union is all of $\\mathbb R^d$, contradicting $\\gamma_C(\\mathbb R^d)=1$. Thus $T_C(e^{-V})(x)\\in(0,1]$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.1 By [F3], the logarithm is defined at these values. Its continuity follows directly from [F4]: for $y_0>0$ and $\\epsilon>0$, strict increase and continuity of $\\exp$ give $\\exp(\\log y_0-\\epsilon)<y_0<\\exp(\\log y_0+\\epsilon)$; put $\\delta=\\min\\{y_0-\\exp(\\log y_0-\\epsilon),\\exp(\\log y_0+\\epsilon)-y_0\\}>0$. If $y>0$ and $|y-y_0|<\\delta$, then $\\exp(\\log y_0-\\epsilon)<y<\\exp(\\log y_0+\\epsilon)$, and strict monotonicity gives $|\\log y-\\log y_0|<\\epsilon$. Hence $V_C$ is finite, nonnegative, and Borel. If $C=0$, then for each coordinate $Z_i$, $\\mathbb E Z_i^2=C_{ii}=0$. For every $n\\ge1$, $0=\\mathbb E Z_i^2\\ge n^{-2}\\mathbb P(|Z_i|\\ge n^{-1})$, so $Z_i=0$ almost surely; the finite intersection gives $Z=0$ almost surely and $\\gamma_0=\\delta_0$; hence $T_0f=f$ and $V_0=V$. [F1, F3, F4, step 1.2, algebra] ∎",
--
