research/plan-spec.json-243171-          "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$, write Euclidean space as $E_d=\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{d-1}$ with coordinates $x=(t,\\mathbf x)$, and put $S_d=\\mathcal S(E_d;\\mathbb C)$ and $S_{d,n}=\\mathcal S(E_d^n;\\mathbb C)$ for $n\\ge1$, with $S_{d,0}=\\mathbb C$. For $d=1$, $\\mathbb R^0$ is the one-point vector space with unit measure. A Schwinger hierarchy is a sequence $S=(S_n)_{n\\ge0}$ with $S_0=1$ and each $S_n\\in S_{d,n}'$; its moments on elementary tests are $\\langle S_n,f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n\\rangle$. The associated unital algebraic test algebra is $T_d=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}^{\\rm alg}S_d^{\\otimes_{\\rm alg}n}$ with concatenation multiplication, unit $1\\in S_{d,0}=\\mathbb C$, and involution $(f_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes f_n)^*=\\overline f_n\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\overline f_1$ and $(c1)^*=\\overline c1$. The moments define $\\omega_S:T_d\\to\\mathbb C$ by linear extension. At $d=4$ this is the same four-dimensional algebra and moment convention used by the positive-tempered-moment definition.\n\nOrdinary moment positivity means $\\omega_S(1)=1$ and $\\omega_S(a^*a)\\ge0$ for every $a\\in T_d$. It is distinct from reflection positivity. Let $\\vartheta(t,\\mathbf x)=(-t,\\mathbf x)$ and $E_{d,+}=\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t>0\\}$. The positive-time polynomial algebra $T_{d,+}$ is the unital star subalgebra generated by tests in $C_c^\\infty(E_{d,+};\\mathbb C)$. Define the antilinear reflection $\\Theta$ by $\\Theta f=\\overline{f\\circ\\vartheta}$ on degree one, $\\Theta(c1)=\\overline c1$, and $\\Theta(ab)=\\Theta(b)\\Theta(a)$. Reflection positivity is $\\omega_S(\\Theta a\\,a)\\in[0,\\infty)$ for every $a\\in T_{d,+}$. For the finite-site version, fix a finite set $J$, a permutation $\\rho:J\\to J$ with $\\rho^2=\\operatorname{id}$, and a specified subset $J_+\\subseteq J$. Let $A_J=\\mathbb C[z_j:j\\in J]$ be the commutative polynomial algebra with $z_j^*=z_j$ and conjugation of coefficients, and let $A_{J,+}$ be its unital subalgebra generated by $z_j$ for $j\\in J_+$. Define $\\Theta_J$ by conjugating coefficients and replacing every $z_j$ by $z_{\\rho(j)}$. For a specified complex-linear functional $\\omega:A_J\\to\\mathbb C$ with $\\omega(1)=1$, ordinary positivity means $\\omega(a^*a)\\in[0,\\infty)$ for all $a\\in A_J$, and reflection positivity means $\\omega((\\Theta_Ja)a)\\in[0,\\infty)$ for all $a\\in A_{J,+}$. One may also use $Q=A_J/I$ for an ideal $I\\subseteq A_J$ invariant under both $*$ and $\\Theta_J$, provided $I\\subseteq\\ker\\omega:=\\{a\\in A_J:\\omega(a)=0\\}$. Writing $[a]=a+I$, define $[a]^*=[a^*]$, $\\overline\\Theta_J[a]=[\\Theta_Ja]$, the positive-site algebra $Q_+=\\{[a]:a\\in A_{J,+}\\}$, and the normalized complex-linear functional $\\overline\\omega([a])=\\omega(a)$. The kernel condition makes this value independent of the representative: if $a-b\\in I$, then $\\omega(a)-\\omega(b)=\\omega(a-b)=0$. On $Q$, ordinary positivity means $\\overline\\omega(u^*u)\\in[0,\\infty)$ for every $u\\in Q$, and reflection positivity means $\\overline\\omega((\\overline\\Theta_Ju)u)\\in[0,\\infty)$ for every $u\\in Q_+$. Equivalently, one may specify a normalized complex-linear functional $\\overline\\omega$ on $Q$ first and set $\\omega(a)=\\overline\\omega([a])$; this automatically satisfies the kernel condition. An exchanging pair of sites is $J=\\{-,+\\}$ with $\\rho(-)=+$, $\\rho(+)=-$, and $J_+=\\{+\\}$.\n\nA regulated hierarchy is a family $S^\\lambda=(S^\\lambda_n)_{n\\ge0}$ indexed by a nonempty directed set $(\\Lambda,\\preceq)$, where directed means every two indices have a common upper bound. A distributional continuum limit is a hierarchy $S$ such that for every fixed $n$ and every $\\Phi\\in S_{d,n}$, $\\langle S^\\lambda_n,\\Phi\\rangle\\to\\langle S_n,\\Phi\\rangle$ as $\\lambda$ advances in $\\Lambda$. A limit contract records these pairings, the regulator family, and any additional uniform estimates; pointwise-in-$n$ convergence by itself asserts no uniform bound as $n$ varies.\n\nFor explicit comparison, define the Schwartz seminorm $P_k(\\Phi)=\\max_{|\\alpha|,|\\beta|\\le k}\\sup_{x\\in E_d^n}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta\\Phi(x)|$ on $S_{d,n}$. A per-order uniform continuity record is a choice, for each fixed $n$, of $r_n\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and $C_n\\ge0$ such that $|\\langle S^\\lambda_n,\\Phi\\rangle|\\le C_nP_{r_n}(\\Phi)$ for every $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$ and $\\Phi\\in S_{d,n}$. Separately, a uniform-order bound on the limit hierarchy is the explicit cross-order condition that there exist fixed $s\\in\\mathbb N$ and $A,B,q>0$ with $|\\langle S_n,\\Phi\\rangle|\\le A B^n(n!)^q P_{sn}(\\Phi)$ for every $n\\ge1$ and $\\Phi\\in S_{d,n}$. This bound is recorded separately and is not inferred from distributional convergence or asserted equivalent to a corrected Osterwalder–Schrader regularity hypothesis.\n\nFor $\\gamma(x)=Rx+a$ with $R\\in O(d)$ and $a\\in E_d$, define $(U_\\gamma\\Phi)(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\Phi(\\gamma^{-1}x_1,\\ldots,\\gamma^{-1}x_n)$. Euclidean invariance means $\\langle S_n,U_\\gamma\\Phi\\rangle=\\langle S_n,\\Phi\\rangle$ for every such $\\gamma$ and $\\Phi\\in S_{d,n}$. Permutation symmetry means invariance of the pairing under each permutation of its $n$ argument blocks. Clustering means that for every $n,m\\ge1$, $f\\in S_{d,n}$ and $g\\in S_{d,m}$,\n\n$$\n\\langle S_{n+m},f\\otimes\\tau_{ae_0}g\\rangle\\longrightarrow \\langle S_n,f\\rangle\\langle S_m,g\\rangle\\quad(a\\to+\\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $e_0=(1,\\mathbf0)$ and $(\\tau_{ae_0}g)(x_1,\\ldots,x_m)=g(x_1-ae_0,\\ldots,x_m-ae_0)$. This contract definition asserts neither existence of a regulated limit nor any of these additional properties.",
