research/plan-spec.json-243542-          },
research/plan-spec.json-243543-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, its countable-family consequence, and $m>0$. Let $\\Phi$, $\\mu_C$, the fixed nonnegative mollifier $\\rho$, $\\Phi_\\epsilon$, and $W_{\\epsilon,n}(f)$ be as in the earlier cutoff definition. Here $L^2(\\mu_C)$ is the space of square-integrable complex random variables modulo almost-everywhere equality, with norm $\\|X\\|_2=(\\mathbb E_{\\mu_C}|X|^2)^{1/2}$. For every integer $n\\ge1$ and every bounded measurable compactly supported $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb C$, the family $W_{\\epsilon,n}(f)$ is Cauchy in $L^2(\\mu_C)$ as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$; write its limit as $W_n(f)$. If $f$ is real-valued, then $W_n(f)$ is a real $L^2$-class. For all such $f,g$,\n\n$$\\mathbb E_{\\mu_C}[\\overline{W_n(f)}W_n(g)]=n!\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\overline{f(x)}g(y)G_m(x-y)^n\\,d^2x\\,d^2y.$$\n\nThe kernel satisfies $|G_m(z)|\\le K_m(1+|\\log|z||)$ for $0<|z|\\le1$ and $|G_m(z)|\\le K_m e^{-m|z|}/\\sqrt{m|z|}$ for $|z|\\ge1$, as established by the earlier covariance-kernel analysis.",
research/plan-spec.json:243544:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
