research/plan-spec.json-245678-          "statement": "Let $p:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal Y$ and $q:\\mathcal Y\\to\\mathcal Z$ be functors of finite groupoids. The iterated homotopy fiber over any $z\\in\\mathcal Z$ is equivalent to the homotopy fiber of $q\\circ p$, so $(q\\circ p)_!=q_!p_!$. Consequently, for composable spans of finite groupoids, the induced push-pull maps compose by the homotopy-pullback composite span. If $\\mathcal X\\xleftarrow{a}\\mathcal E\\xrightarrow{b}\\mathcal Y$ is a span, equip invariant functions with\n\n$$\\langle f,g\\rangle_{\\mathcal X}:=\\sum_{[x]\\in\\pi_0(\\mathcal X)} \\frac{\\overline{f([x])}\\,g([x])}{|\\operatorname{Aut}_{\\mathcal X}(x)|}.$$\n\nThen the adjoint of $b_!a^*$ is $a_!b^*$, the map of the transposed span.",
research/plan-spec.json-245679-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-245725-            "thm-induced-fundamental-group-map-functoriality": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-245726-          },
research/plan-spec.json:245727:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-245728-          "statement": "Let $K$ be a finite CW complex, possibly disconnected, and let $G$ be a finite group. Write its connected components as $K_1,\\ldots,K_m$ and choose a vertex $v_j$ in each. Let $\\mathcal R_G(K)$ be the product over $j$ of the action groupoids whose objects are homomorphisms $\\rho:\\pi_1(K_j,v_j)\\to G$ and whose arrows $\\rho\\to\\rho'$ are elements $a\\in G$ satisfying $\\rho'(\\gamma)=a\\rho(\\gamma)a^{-1}$ for every loop class $\\gamma$; identity arrows are labeled by $1$, and if $a:\\rho\\to\\rho'$ and $b:\\rho'\\to\\rho''$, then their composite $b\\circ a$ is labeled by $ba$. For $K=\\varnothing$, this empty product is the terminal groupoid. Choosing a maximal tree in each component, inverse path holonomy defines an equivalence $\\Phi_K:\\mathcal C_G(K)\\simeq\\mathcal R_G(K)$: for an edge path traversing $e_1,\\ldots,e_n$ with signs $\\epsilon_k$, its holonomy is $H_A(e_1^{\\epsilon_1}\\cdots e_n^{\\epsilon_n})=g_{e_n}^{\\epsilon_n}\\cdots g_{e_1}^{\\epsilon_1}$, and $\\Phi_K(A)$ sends a loop class represented by an edge loop $[\\gamma]$ to $H_A(\\bar\\gamma)$; a gauge arrow $h$ maps to the conjugating arrow $h(v_j)$ on component $K_j$. In particular, for connected $K$,\n\n$$|\\mathcal C_G(K)|=\\frac{|\\operatorname{Hom}(\\pi_1(K,v_1),G)|}{|G|},$$\n\nand for arbitrary finite $K$,\n\n$$|\\mathcal C_G(K)|=\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\\frac{|\\operatorname{Hom}(\\pi_1(K_j,v_j),G)|}{|G|}.$$\n\nThe action retains all conjugation stabilizers. For a subcomplex inclusion $\\jmath:L\\hookrightarrow K$, choose a base vertex $v_i$ in each component $L_i$, a base vertex $w_{j(i)}$ in its containing component $K_{j(i)}$, and an edge path $\\gamma_i$ in $K_{j(i)}$ from $w_{j(i)}$ to $v_i$. The associated based map is $\\jmath_{\\gamma_i,*}([\\ell])=[\\gamma_i*\\jmath(\\ell)*\\bar\\gamma_i]$. Under $\\Phi_K$ and $\\Phi_L$, restriction corresponds to precomposition by these maps, up to the natural isomorphism whose component at a flat assignment $A$ is conjugation by $H_A(\\bar\\gamma_i)$. Thus the partition function depends only on the fundamental-group representation groupoid, not on the chosen finite cell decomposition.\n\n",
