research/plan-spec.json:246782:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-246783-          "statement": "Fix an integer $n\\ge2$, let $R=\\mathbb Q[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ with $\\deg x_j=2$, and let $S_n$ act on $R$ by permuting variables. Let $\\mathrm{SBim}_n$ be the idempotent-complete additive monoidal category of graded $(R,R)$-Soergel bimodules generated by the unit bimodule $R$, the bimodules $B_i=R\\otimes_{R^{s_i}}R(1)$ for the adjacent transpositions $s_i=(i\\ i+1)$, internal grading shifts, finite direct sums, tensor products over $R$, and direct summands. Use the shift convention $M(1)_d=M_{d+1}$; on the split Grothendieck ring it is recorded by $v[M]=[M(1)]$. Let $K^b(\\mathrm{SBim}_n)$ denote the category of bounded cochain complexes in $\\mathrm{SBim}_n$ with chain maps modulo chain homotopy.\n\nFor $1\\le i<n$, let $F_i=[B_i\\xrightarrow{\\varepsilon_i}R(1)]$ in cohomological degrees $0,1$ and $F_i^{-1}=[R(-1)\\xrightarrow{\\eta_i}B_i]$ in degrees $-1,0$, with the maps and grading conventions of the positive and negative Rouquier generator complexes. For a signed word $\\sigma=\\sigma_{i_1}^{\\epsilon_1}\\cdots\\sigma_{i_r}^{\\epsilon_r}$, $\\epsilon_k\\in\\{+1,-1\\}$, let $F(\\sigma)=F_{i_1}^{\\epsilon_1}\\otimes_R\\cdots\\otimes_R F_{i_r}^{\\epsilon_r}$, using signed tensor totalization and left-to-right bracketing; the empty word gives $R$ in degree zero. If $\\beta\\in B_n$ is the braid represented by $\\sigma$, the **Rouquier categorified line operator** $F(\\beta)$ is this complex considered up to the canonical homotopy equivalence between words representing $\\beta$. Tensor product is the categorical stacking operation. The comparison maps give the unit and coherent multiplication maps $F(\\beta)\\otimes_R F(\\gamma)\\simeq F(\\beta\\gamma)$; equivalently, left tensoring defines the coherent braid-group action on $K^b(R\\text{-grmod})$.\n\nA **physical defect realization** is additional data. A monoidal category $\\mathcal D$ here means a category with a tensor bifunctor, a unit object, associator and unit isomorphisms satisfying the pentagon and unit triangles; its objects are declared line-defect types, its morphisms are declared junction maps, and its tensor product is the chosen stacking operation. The realization is a strong monoidal functor $\\mathcal R:K^b(\\mathrm{SBim}_n)\\to\\mathcal D$, meaning a functor with natural product and unit isomorphisms satisfying those same coherence equations and assigning images to every morphism, including junction maps, in its domain. Calling $\\mathcal D$ a category of defects in a particular quantum field theory also requires an independently specified physical model. The Soergel and Rouquier data alone supply neither $\\mathcal D$ nor this functor.",
research/plan-spec.json-246784-          "provenance": {
--
research/plan-spec.json-246833-          "justified_by": [],
research/plan-spec.json-246834-          "aliases": [],
research/plan-spec.json:246835:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-246836-          "statement": "Fix $n\\ge2$ and the type-A Rouquier categorical braid-line model. Let $\\chi(C)=\\sum_m(-1)^m[C^m]$ be the alternating class of a bounded cochain complex in the split Grothendieck ring $K_0^{\\mathrm{split}}(\\mathrm{SBim}_n)$, and use the shift convention $v[X]=[X(1)]$. Let $\\Phi:K_0^{\\mathrm{split}}(\\mathrm{SBim}_n)\\to H_{S_n}$ be the algebra isomorphism with $\\Phi([B_i])=H_i=v(T_i+1)$ and Hecke parameter $q=v^{-2}$. For $\\beta\\in B_n$, let $e(\\beta)$ be its exponent sum, the number of positive letters minus the number of negative letters in any signed word for $\\beta$, and let $T_\\beta$ be the image of $\\beta$ under the group homomorphism $B_n\\to H_{S_n}^{\\times}$ sending $\\sigma_i\\mapsto T_i$. Then\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\!\\left(\\chi(F(\\beta))\\right)=v^{e(\\beta)}T_\\beta.\n$$\n\nConsequently the normalized map $\\beta\\mapsto v^{-e(\\beta)}\\Phi(\\chi(F(\\beta)))$ is the standard Hecke braid homomorphism, and its normalization is multiplicative under braid stacking. Homotopy-equivalent complexes have the same Euler class, but need not be isomorphic as graded complexes.",
research/plan-spec.json-246837-          "provenance": {
