research/plan-spec.json-246876-          "statement": "Fix a field $\\Bbbk$, a finite color set $I$, a symmetrizable Cartan matrix $A=(a_{ij})_{i,j\\in I}$, and positive integers $d_i$ satisfying $d_i a_{ij}=d_j a_{ji}$. Here $a_{ii}=2$, $a_{ij}\\le0$ for $i\\ne j$, and $a_{ij}=0$ exactly when $a_{ji}=0$. Fix a weight lattice $X$ (a free abelian group), simple roots $\\alpha_i\\in X$, and integer-valued pairings $\\langle\\lambda,\\alpha_i^\\vee\\rangle$ with $\\langle\\alpha_j,\\alpha_i^\\vee\\rangle=a_{ij}$. Choose polynomials $Q_{ij}(u,v)\\in\\Bbbk[u,v]$ with $Q_{ii}=0$, $Q_{ij}(u,v)=Q_{ji}(v,u)$, and $Q_{ij}\\ne0$ for $i\\ne j$; each $Q_{ij}$ is homogeneous for $\\deg u=2d_i$, $\\deg v=2d_j$, of weighted degree $-2d_i a_{ij}$.\n\nFor $\\nu=\\sum_{i\\in I}m_i i\\in\\mathbb N[I]$ of height $r=\\sum_i m_i$, a **quiver Hecke algebra** $R_Q(\\nu)$ is the graded $\\Bbbk$-algebra generated by idempotents $e(\\mathbf i)$ for sequences $\\mathbf i=(i_1,\\ldots,i_r)$ with content $\\nu$, dots $y_1,\\ldots,y_r$, and crossings $\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\psi_{r-1}$. Put $s_k\\mathbf i=(i_1,\\ldots,i_{k+1},i_k,\\ldots,i_r)$. The defining relations are\n\n$$\ne(\\mathbf i)e(\\mathbf j)=\\delta_{\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j}e(\\mathbf i),\\qquad \\sum_{\\mathbf i}e(\\mathbf i)=1,\\qquad y_a e(\\mathbf i)=e(\\mathbf i)y_a,\\qquad y_a y_b=y_b y_a,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\psi_k e(\\mathbf i)=e(s_k\\mathbf i)\\psi_k,\\qquad \\psi_k y_a=y_a\\psi_k\\ \\ (a\\ne k,k+1),\n$$\n\n$$\n(\\psi_k y_{k+1}-y_k\\psi_k)e(\\mathbf i)=(y_{k+1}\\psi_k-\\psi_k y_k)e(\\mathbf i)=\\delta_{i_k,i_{k+1}}e(\\mathbf i),\\qquad \\psi_k^2e(\\mathbf i)=Q_{i_k,i_{k+1}}(y_k,y_{k+1})e(\\mathbf i),\n$$\n\n$$\n\\psi_k\\psi_l=\\psi_l\\psi_k\\ \\ (|k-l|>1),\\qquad (\\psi_{k+1}\\psi_k\\psi_{k+1}-\\psi_k\\psi_{k+1}\\psi_k)e(\\mathbf i)=\\begin{cases}\\displaystyle\\frac{Q_{i_k,i_{k+1}}(y_{k+2},y_{k+1})-Q_{i_k,i_{k+1}}(y_k,y_{k+1})}{y_{k+2}-y_k}e(\\mathbf i),&i_k=i_{k+2}\\ne i_{k+1},\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe displayed quotient is a polynomial because its numerator vanishes when $y_{k+2}=y_k$. The degrees are $\\deg e(\\mathbf i)=0$, $\\deg(y_k e(\\mathbf i))=2d_{i_k}$, and $\\deg(\\psi_k e(\\mathbf i))=-d_{i_k}a_{i_k i_{k+1}}$.\n\nFor this definition, a small additive $\\Bbbk$-linear graded abelian category has a set of objects and morphisms, finite biproducts, kernels and cokernels with every monomorphism a kernel and every epimorphism a cokernel, $\\Bbbk$-vector-space Hom groups with bilinear composition, and a decomposition $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal C}(M,N)=\\bigoplus_{s\\in\\mathbb Z}\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal C}^s(M,N)$ with identities in degree zero and composition adding internal degrees. This internal degree is separate from any cohomological degree. An exact functor is a $\\Bbbk$-linear functor preserving short exact sequences in the underlying abelian categories. A functor $G:\\mathcal C\\to\\mathcal D$ between such graded categories is **degree-preserving (degree zero)** if\n$$\nG\\!\\left(\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal C}^s(M,N)\\right)\\subseteq\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal D}^s(GM,GN)\n$$\nfor every $M,N$ and $s\\in\\mathbb Z$. An adjunction $F\\dashv E$ is specified by natural unit and counit maps satisfying the two triangle identities. A natural transformation is a family of morphisms commuting with every source morphism; homogeneous degree $s$ means each component belongs to the internal degree-$s$ Hom space. If a proposed action uses internal shifts in an adjunction, those shifts and the degrees of its unit and counit maps are part of the specified data. For an abelian category $\\mathcal C$, its exact Grothendieck group $K_0(\\mathcal C)$ is generated by isomorphism classes $[M]$ with relations $[B]=[A]+[C]$ for each short exact sequence $0\\to A\\to B\\to C\\to0$.\n\nA **KLR categorical action contract** specifies, for each $\\lambda\\in X$, such a small additive $\\Bbbk$-linear graded abelian category $\\mathcal C_\\lambda$, degree-preserving exact functors $F_i:\\mathcal C_\\lambda\\to\\mathcal C_{\\lambda-\\alpha_i}$ with specified degree-preserving exact right adjoints $E_i:\\mathcal C_{\\lambda-\\alpha_i}\\to\\mathcal C_\\lambda$, and homogeneous natural transformations $x_i:F_i\\Rightarrow F_i$ of degree $2d_i$ and $\\tau_{ij}:F_iF_j\\Rightarrow F_jF_i$ of degree $-d_i a_{ij}$. If a proposed adjunction convention uses internal shifts, those shifts and the degrees of its unit and counit maps must be stated explicitly. For each $\\nu$ and $\\lambda$, the direct sum of composite functors $F_{i_1}\\cdots F_{i_r}$ over sequences of content $\\nu$ must carry an action of $R_Q(\\nu)$: $e(\\mathbf i)$ is projection to the indicated sequence summand, $y_k$ acts by the dot on the $k$th factor, and $\\psi_k$ acts by the crossing on adjacent factors, all with the relations displayed above. Functor-composition order and grading shifts must be fixed in the proposed action and used consistently.\n\nThis contract records required data and checks; it does not assert that any such categories, functors, or transformations exist. A claim of an **integrable** action additionally states and proves local nilpotence: for every object $M$ and color $i$, some powers $E_i^N(M)$ and $F_i^N(M)$ vanish. A claimed highest-weight action additionally specifies a weight $\\lambda$, a chosen object in $\\mathcal C_\\lambda$ killed by every $E_i$, and the precise subcategory generated from it by the $F_i$, shifts, finite sums, and summands. To assert that the induced operators on $K_0(\\mathcal C):=\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\in X}K_0(\\mathcal C_\\lambda)$ form a representation of $U_q(\\mathfrak g)$, the contract must identify the exact Grothendieck-group convention, establish every required quantum-group relation, and cite a decategorification theorem whose hypotheses match the specified categories and functors. Merely satisfying the positive KLR relations does not establish those extra conclusions.\n\nA physical interpretation as line-changing defects requires a separately specified defect 2-category, whose objects are declared defect sectors, whose 1-morphisms are declared defects changing sectors, and whose 2-morphisms are declared junction maps. A 2-functor here assigns objects, functors, and natural transformations while preserving identities and both compositions; a weak target additionally requires its coherence isomorphisms and equations. No physical defect realization is part of this mathematical contract.\n\n",
