research/plan-spec.json-256343-            "thm-ringel-map-embedding-and-finite-dynkin-criterion": "mathematical-premise"
research/plan-spec.json-256344-          },
research/plan-spec.json:256345:          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
research/plan-spec.json-256346-          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice as inherited through the cited Hall supplier. Let\n$V=\\mathbb C^2$ with ordered orthonormal basis $|\\uparrow\\rangle,|\\downarrow\\rangle$.\nOn $V$ define\n$$ E=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad F=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad H=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}. $$\nThey satisfy $[H,E]=2E$, $[H,F]=-2F$, and $[E,F]=H$, and therefore define\nthe standard two-dimensional representation of $\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)$.\nWrite $S^+=E$, $S^-=F$, and $S^z=H/2$.\n\nFor each integer $N\\geq1$, the spin space is\n$\\mathcal H_N=V^{\\otimes N}$ with its tensor-product inner product. If $A$ is\nan endomorphism of $V$, let $A_j$ act as $A$ on factor $j$ and as the identity\non the other factors. Define the diagonal generators\n$$ S_N^+=\\sum_{j=1}^N E_j,\\qquad S_N^-=\\sum_{j=1}^N F_j,\\qquad S_N^z=\\frac12\\sum_{j=1}^N H_j. $$\nFor $m\\in\\{-N/2,-N/2+1,\\ldots,N/2\\}$, its weight space is\n$\\mathcal H_{N,m}=\\ker(S_N^z-mI)$. These are dimensionless matrices in the\nalgebraic spin convention.\n\nFor each integer $r\\geq0$, define $V(r)$ to be the vector space\n$\\mathbb C[x,y]_r$ of homogeneous polynomials of degree $r$, with\n$\\mathfrak{sl}_2$ action\n$$ E=x\\partial_y,\\qquad F=y\\partial_x,\\qquad H=x\\partial_x-y\\partial_y. $$\nThe ordered monomials $x^{r-k}y^k$ for $0\\leq k\\leq r$ form its basis.\nUnder $x\\mapsto|\\uparrow\\rangle$ and $y\\mapsto|\\downarrow\\rangle$,\n$V(1)$ is the representation on $V$ above. A tensor power $V(1)^{\\otimes N}$\nuses the diagonal action defined by the sums $S_N^+,S_N^-,2S_N^z$.\n\nLet $q_H>1$ be a prime power, fix a finite field $\\mathbb F_{q_H}$, and put\n$\\nu=\\sqrt{q_H}>1$ and $K=\\mathbb Q(\\nu)$. The representation category\n$\\mathcal A=\\mathrm{Vect}^{\\mathrm{fd}}_{\\mathbb F_{q_H}}$ of the one-vertex,\nno-arrow quiver has one simple isomorphism class, represented by\n$S=\\mathbb F_{q_H}$. For objects $M,N$, write\n$\\chi_0(M,N)=\\dim_{\\mathbb F_{q_H}}\\operatorname{Hom}(M,N)-\n\\dim_{\\mathbb F_{q_H}}\\operatorname{YExt}^1(M,N)$. In the Hall convention of\n[[def-euler-twisted-hall-algebra]], the Hall space is\n$H_{\\mathcal A,\\nu}=\\bigoplus_{[M]}K[M]$, with basis $[M]$ indexed by\nisomorphism classes and product\n$$[M]\\star[N]=\\nu^{\\chi_0(M,N)}\\sum_{[L]}g^L_{M,N}[L],$$\nwhere $g^L_{M,N}$ counts subspaces $N'\\subseteq L$ with $N'\\cong N$ and\n$L/N'\\cong M$, or equivalently short exact sequences\n$0\\to N\\xrightarrow{i}L\\xrightarrow{p}M\\to0$ modulo changes of the\nendpoint labels: $(i,p)\\sim(i\\circ a,b\\circ p)$ for\n$a\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(N)$ and $b\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(M)$, with $L$\nfixed pointwise. Each orbit is determined by $\\operatorname{im}i=N'$;\nwithout this quotient, each such subspace contributes\n$|\\operatorname{Aut}(N)|\\,|\\operatorname{Aut}(M)|$ labelled sequences.\nThus the twist is part of the multiplication.\nFor $M\\cong\\mathbb F_{q_H}^{m}$ and $N\\cong\\mathbb F_{q_H}^{n}$,\n$\\dim\\operatorname{Hom}(M,N)=mn$ and every short exact sequence splits, so\n$\\operatorname{YExt}^1(M,N)=0$ and $\\chi_0(M,N)=mn$.\n\nBy [[def-extended-hall-algebra-and-composition-subalgebra]], the composition\nsubalgebra is the subalgebra of this Hall algebra generated by the classes of\nsimple objects; its torus extension is the distinct extended composition\nsubalgebra. Denote the former by $\\mathcal C$ here, so\n$\\mathcal C=K\\langle[S]\\rangle\\subseteq H_{\\mathcal A,\\nu}$. The rank-one\npositive quantum half is $U_\\nu^+(\\mathfrak{sl}_2)=K[E]$, the polynomial\nalgebra on one generator. The Ringel map $E\\mapsto[S]$ embeds this positive\nhalf with image $\\mathcal C$; since the one-vertex graph is finite Dynkin type\n$A_1$, the finite-Dynkin criterion identifies $\\mathcal C$ with the full Hall\nalgebra in this case. The simple object is unique up to isomorphism, not as a\nchosen representative. The Axiom of Choice is inherited through the cited\nRingel supplier.\n\n",
