research/plan-spec.json-258576-          "physical_scope": "Classical stationary nondegenerate bifurcate horizons in smooth diffeomorphism-covariant metric theories whose Lagrangian depends algebraically on curvature and has no covariant derivatives of curvature. The result is a conditional Noether-charge identity, not a dynamical second law or an empirical or quantum statement.\n",
research/plan-spec.json-258577-          "statement": "Use the stationary Noether data of the preceding Definition, with fixed $\\xi$, compact boundaryless bifurcation surface $B$, and surface gravity $\\kappa>0$. Choose the covariant first-variation potential and $Q_\\xi$ in the canonical local representative class of the preceding Noether-charge decomposition theorem. Let $\\nabla$ be the Levi-Civita connection of $g$, use the curvature convention\n$$R(X,Y)Z=\\nabla_X\\nabla_YZ-\\nabla_Y\\nabla_XZ-\\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z,$$\nand write $R_{abcd}:=g(R(e_c,e_d)e_b,e_a)$ in any local frame and $R:=g^{ac}g^{bd}R_{abcd}$. Write $\\mathcal L=\\ell(g,\\psi,\\nabla\\psi,\\ldots,R)\\,\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g$, where $\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g$ is the oriented metric volume form and $\\ell$ depends smoothly on the Riemann tensor algebraically, with no covariant derivatives of $R$. Define $P^{abcd}$ to be the Riemann-symmetric metric representative of the differential of $\\ell$ in its curvature argument:\n$$\\delta\\ell\\big|_{g,\\psi,\\nabla\\psi,\\ldots}=P^{abcd}\\delta R_{abcd}.$$\nHere $P$ satisfies $P^{abcd}=-P^{bacd}=-P^{abdc}=P^{cdab}$ and $P^{a[bcd]}=0$. Let $dA_B$ be the positive volume form of the induced Riemannian metric on $B$ (in local coordinates with induced metric $h$, $dA_B=\\sqrt{\\det h}\\,dy^1\\cdots dy^{n-2}$), and define the stationary Wald Noether-charge functional\n$$S_W:=-\\frac{2\\pi k_B}{\\hbar}\\int_B P^{abcd}\\epsilon_{ab}\\epsilon_{cd}\\,dA_B.$$\nFor every admitted smooth linearized perturbation at the stationary solution, with $\\xi$ and $B$ fixed and the residual constraint variation $\\delta C_\\xi=0$, the chosen Noether charge satisfies\n$$\\delta\\int_B Q_\\xi=\\frac{\\hbar\\kappa}{2\\pi k_B}\\,\\delta S_W.$$\nHere $\\hbar>0$ is Planck's constant, $k_B>0$ is Boltzmann's constant, and the action normalization is $c=1$. The identity is evaluated at the unperturbed value of $\\kappa$; it does not include a $\\delta\\kappa$ term.",
--
research/plan-spec.json-258623-          "reference": "items/pthm-tgrx-noether-first-law.md#statement",
research/plan-spec.json-258624-          "design_state": "Step 1 carrier authored for batch 117; local support item added only where needed to prove the planned stationary Noether-charge result.",
research/plan-spec.json:258625:          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
research/plan-spec.json-258626-          "physical_scope": "Conditional first law for a classical stationary nondegenerate bifurcate horizon with the specified covariant potential, globally compatible Noether charge, vanishing linearized constraint variation, and integrable asymptotic Hamiltonian charge. It is not a dynamical law, a quantum result, or empirical confirmation of a gravity model.\n",
research/plan-spec.json-258627-          "statement": "For the stationary Noether data of the preceding Definition, choose $Q_\\xi$ in the canonical local representative class of the preceding Noether-charge decomposition theorem. Let $\\delta\\phi$ be an admitted linearized solution with fixed $\\xi$ and $\\delta C_\\xi=0$. Let $C_R$ be finite oriented smooth singular hypersurface chains with $\\partial C_R=S_R-B$, where the boundary orientations are outward at $S_R$ and inward at $B$. Then\n$$d(\\delta Q_\\xi-\\iota_\\xi\\theta)=0$$\non each $C_R$, and\n$$\\delta H_\\xi=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{S_R}(\\delta Q_\\xi-\\iota_\\xi\\theta) =\\int_B\\delta Q_\\xi =\\frac{\\hbar\\kappa}{2\\pi k_B}\\,\\delta S_W.$$\nThe last equality uses the derivative-free curvature hypothesis and the Noether entropy functional of the preceding theorem. The identity is conditional on the exact boundary-charge and vanishing-constraint assumptions; $\\kappa$ is the unperturbed surface gravity. For the Einstein-Hilbert Lagrangian $\\mathcal L_{\\rm EH}=R\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g/(16\\pi G)$ with Newton constant $G>0$ in the same $c=1$ action normalization, it gives $S_W=k_BA_B/(4G\\hbar)$, where $A_B:=\\int_B dA_B$.",
--
