research/plan-spec.json-258689-          "physical_scope": "Classical f(R) metric Lagrangians on smooth stationary nondegenerate bifurcate horizons under the algebraic-curvature assumptions of the stationary Noether first law. This is not a dynamical entropy law.\n",
research/plan-spec.json-258690-          "statement": "In units with $c=1$, let $\\mathcal L=f(R)\\boldsymbol\\epsilon_g/(16\\pi G)$, where $G>0$ is the Newton coupling in the given $n$-dimensional action normalization, with units $L^{n-2}/\\mathrm{action}$. Choose an open interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ and $f\\in C^\\infty(J)$, and admit only smooth background fields and finite-dimensional smooth field families for which $R_{\\phi_p}(x)\\in J$ for every parameter $p$ and spacetime point $x\\in M$. Thus this composed Lagrangian is smooth throughout the spacetime and variation domain required by the preceding first-law theorem; regularity only at curvature values on $B$ is not assumed. The scalar curvature $R$ has units $L^{-2}$, so $f$ has those units and $f'(R)$ is dimensionless. Under the same smooth stationary horizon assumptions as the preceding theorem,\n$$S_W=\\frac{k_B}{4G\\hbar}\\int_B f'(R)\\,dA_B.$$\nThe example computes the Noether-charge functional only; it asserts no monotonicity along a dynamical horizon.\n\n",
