research/plan-spec.json-260560-          "statement": "A **finite fusion transfer count model** consists of a finite nonempty label set $I$, a distinguished label $0\\in I$, and an explicitly supplied matrix $N=(N_{ab})_{a,b\\in I}$ whose entries lie in $\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$. The entry $N_{ab}$ is the multiplicity of elementary transitions from label $b$ to label $a$; formally, the transition copies from $b$ to $a$ are the triples $(b,a,j)$ with integer $1\\leq j\\leq N_{ab}$. A length-$n$ path is a sequence of $n+1$ labels together with a chosen transition copy between each consecutive pair. View each natural-number entry as a real number under the usual inclusion $\\mathbb N\\subset\\mathbb R$. For a finite set $J$ and $F=\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, $F^J$ means the functions $J\\to F$, with pointwise addition $(x+y)_j=x_j+y_j$ and scalar multiplication $(\\lambda x)_j=\\lambda x_j$. For real matrices $A,B$ indexed by $I$, define $(A+B)_{ab}:=A_{ab}+B_{ab}$. Their action on $x\\in\\mathbb R^I$ is $(Ax)_a:=\\sum_{b\\in I}A_{ab}x_b$. Matrix multiplication is $(AB)_{ac}:=\\sum_{b\\in I}A_{ab}B_{bc}$; it is associative by regrouping finite sums. These definitions are compatible because $((AB)x)_a=\\sum_{b,c\\in I}A_{ab}B_{bc}x_c=\\sum_{b\\in I}A_{ab}(Bx)_b=(A(Bx))_a$, and $((A+B)C)_{ac}=\\sum_b(A_{ab}+B_{ab})C_{bc}=(AC)_{ac}+(BC)_{ac}$. Thus the natural-entry powers can also act on real vectors and the addition used below distributes over matrix multiplication. Let $I_I$ be the identity matrix with entries $(I_I)_{ab}=1$ if $a=b$ and $0$ otherwise, and use the power convention $N^0:=I_I$ and $N^{n+1}:=NN^n$ for $n\\geq0$. Define the vacuum-return count $D_n:=(N^n)_{00}$. If $D_n>0$, set $H_n:=\\mathbb C^{D_n}$, with coordinates $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_{D_n})$ and inner product $\\langle x,y\\rangle:=\\sum_{j=1}^{D_n}x_j\\overline{y_j}$, linear in the first argument. This pairing satisfies the inner-product axioms of [[def-inner-product-space]]: finite distributivity gives $\\langle\\alpha x+\\beta z,y\\rangle=\\alpha\\langle x,y\\rangle+\\beta\\langle z,y\\rangle$, and conjugating the finite sum gives $\\overline{\\langle y,x\\rangle}=\\sum_j\\overline{y_j}x_j=\\langle x,y\\rangle$. Writing $x_j=a_j+ib_j$ with real $a_j,b_j$ gives $\\langle x,x\\rangle=\\sum_j(a_j^2+b_j^2)\\geq0$; if this sum is zero, each nonnegative summand is zero, hence every $a_j=b_j=0$ and $x=0$. Conversely $x=0$ gives zero. The conjugation identities used here follow directly from $\\overline{a+ib}=a-ib$ and finite distributivity. Therefore $H_n$ is a finite-dimensional complex inner-product space; its coordinate unit vectors form a basis since every $x$ equals their linear combination with coefficients $x_j$, and a zero combination forces every coefficient to vanish. Let $I_{H_n}$ be its identity endomorphism and define $\\rho_n:=D_n^{-1}I_{H_n}$. If $D_n=0$, no normalized operator $\\rho_n$ is assigned. The matrix $N$ is model data; no fusion category, quantum group, or representation is presumed to realize it.\n\n",
