           "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Use natural units on Minkowski $\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and oriented Euclidean spatial slice with $\\epsilon_{123}=1$. Fix the global group $SU(2)$, basis $T_a=-i\\sigma_a/2$, and positive $\\operatorname{Ad}$-invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$ with $\\kappa(T_a,T_b)=\\delta_{ab}$. Let $g>0$ be dimensionless and let $A_i,\\Phi$ be smooth static fields in the zero-potential adjoint-Higgs model, with $[A_i]=[\\Phi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, $F_{ij}=\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i+[A_i,A_j]$, $D_i\\Phi=\\partial_i\\Phi+[A_i,\\Phi]$, and $B_i=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}$. Require the model's smooth finite-energy boundary reduction: $|\\Phi|_\\kappa\\to v>0$ for a fixed $v$ of dimension $\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, with smooth $n_\\infty=\\Phi/v$, the limiting tangential covariant derivative of $n_\\infty$ vanishes, and the limiting magnetic field lies in its stabilizer algebra. Suppose the energy\n\n$$E=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\sum_i\\bigl(\\kappa(B_i,B_i)+\\kappa(D_i\\Phi,D_i\\Phi)\\bigr)\\,d^3x$$\n\nis finite and the model's asymptotic reduction and surface limit\n\n$$m=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{S_R^2}\\kappa(\\Phi,B_i)\\hat x_i\\,dS$$\n\nexist. Put $T=m/g^2$. Then both identities hold:\n\n$$E=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\sum_i\\kappa(B_i-D_i\\Phi,B_i-D_i\\Phi)\\,d^3x+T,$$\n\n$$E=\\frac{1}{2g^2}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\sum_i\\kappa(B_i+D_i\\Phi,B_i+D_i\\Phi)\\,d^3x-T.$$\n\nConsequently $E\\ge |T|$. If $T>0$, equality holds exactly when $B_i=D_i\\Phi$ for every $i$ and every $x$; if $T<0$, equality holds exactly when $B_i=-D_i\\Phi$ everywhere. If $T=0$, equality holds exactly when $B_i=0$ and $D_i\\Phi=0$ everywhere. These are conditional classical deductions for the adopted energy functional; the word BPS here refers only to classical energy saturation and asserts no quantum state or supersymmetry central-charge relation.",
